--
-- Data for Name: pmtl_application_data; Type: TABLE DATA; Schema: public; Owner: postgres
--

COPY public.pmtl_application_data (board_notes, pronouns, class_year, underrepresented_group_in_stem, why_join_jumbocode, volunteering_experience, in_person_this_semester, in_person_next_semester, weekly_meeting_availability, classes_taken, technologies, intro_to_cs, project_proud_of, links, rank_wmcc, rank_village_food_hub, rank_wily_network, rank_somerville_museum, rank_dillar_academy, rank_neip, rank_lgbtq_senior_housing, rank_lcs_tutoring, rank_english_at_large, rank_bread_and_roses, rank_a2empowerment, rank_tufts_general_counsel, preferences_elaboration, additional_info, jumbocode_previous_projects, fake_name, confirmed_team) FROM stdin;
	she/her	2027	Yes	I'm eager to gain hands-on coding experience by working on real-world project. I also want to develop the key skills needed to thrive in the industry. Above all, I'm driven to contribute meaningfully and make a positive impact on the community.	I used to volunteer to teach chess for children at an elementary school. 	Yes	Yes	I'm available Mon and Wed after 8pm, Tuesday after 3pm, Thursday after 5pm and Friday after 12pm. I'm also available all day during weekends.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Python	CS11	I haven't built any particular projects yet. 	\N	3	1	2	11	9	12	5	8	4	10	6	7	All the projects are really interesting and meaningful. I would love to be in any of these projects. My preference is just based on how I feel like I would vibe with the PMs. I think I would learn a lot if I have a chance to work in any of these amazing projects. 	\N	No.	Denethor II	
	She/Hers	Masters Student (non-Tufts undergrad)	Yes	I want to join JumboCode because I’m excited about working on impactful projects that support non-profit organizations. I’m a people-person, and I look forward to collaborating with new friends while contributing to meaningful causes. By joining the club, I hope to expand my technical skills in development and build strong connections with like-minded individuals.	I was a mentor in Women in Tech, a Chinese online organization, where I provided one-on-one consultations to female students, helping them with career planning, mock interviews, and resume revisions. During my undergraduate studies, I also served as a teaching assistant, assisting my professor in designing projects, setting grading criteria, and implementing an autograder for a newly updated mobile app development course.	Yes	Yes	I’m available for a one-hour weekly team meeting on Monday/Wednesday after 4:30 PM, Tuesday/Thursday after 6 PM, Friday anytime before 7:30 PM, and I’m free all day on Saturday and Sunday.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 115: Database Systems, CS 121: Software Engineering, CS 160: Algorithms, Some of the courses I seletced are not taken at Tufts, but I have completed equivalent courses during my undergraduate studies at the University of Michigan, Ann Arbor.	C/C++, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express	A very simple hello-world C++ program in high school.	The project that I am proud of is decomposing a monolithic messaging backend into containerized microservices deployed on Amazon EKS. While working as a backend developer in the industry, the product's entire messaging backend was a monolith with lots of interdependent features. As the company grew, such monolithic architecture presented significant challenges in supporting increased read-and-write traffic and incorporating new features. Driven by the discovery that few interdependent features in the monolith were tied to messaging functionalities, I explored the possibility of breaking down the monolith into multiple self-contained services. This investigation ultimately introduced me to a concept known as microservices. After collaborating with all messaging teams for 16 months, our team successfully transformed our message backend into a distributed system where features are independently deployable microservices that communicate over a network. Our contributions have thus provided our messaging backend with greater flexibility and scalability.	\N	3	9	11	6	10	12	7	4	2	8	1	5	I prefer to work on the backend of the projects if it is possible.	\N	no	Fatty Bolger	
	He series	2027	Yes	I want to learn more of how to actually apply what I'm learning to the real world, and I feel that JumboCode is the perfect place for that because it's accepting to beginners while also having very impactful projects for those who need them. I believe that this is the perfect club to get a feel for seeing how software engineering is actually implemented with a team of people who can teach me exactly what I want to know. What's also appealing is satisfaction of actually helping people through what I'm learning. Overall, I want to get a sense of how these projects actually come to life and contribute what I can our community.  	I came from a volunteer heavy community back home, but, especially at school, it always felt very transactional. I never had super high numbers of volunteer hours but I always tried to do what I could ever now and then. I would often take leftover food from my job and donate them or sign up for a couple volunteer events my school hosted about once a month. During my senior year of high school I was part of my schools community engagement board to help organize volunteer events for food insecurity specifically. More recently, I've been a FOCUS support staff and plan to apply for leader next year, and I'm currently a CS11 teaching assistant. I really just try to have the mentality of leaving whatever I'm doing better than how I found it, even if it's just picking up a piece of trash. 	Yes	Yes	Around that same time should work for me every day except monday and thursday. It's honestly tough for me to say because I have a lot of scattered gaps in my schedule. I believe that evenings to nights usually work great since there are no class conflicts. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++	CS11 was my first ever introduction. Never coded before. 	Over the summer I made a super bad rock paper scissors game, but it was something I made entirely by myself with no help despite its flaws. It wasn't anything crazy 