ate individual challenges and made sure to provide them with my resources so that their research would be successful. This experience gave me the opportunity to take a part in their learning process and contribute to their education, which I valued. \nMy experience with service extends to my current role as a CS TA which I started this semester, where I continue to help students. 	Yes	Yes	Mon/Wed any time after 8, Tue/Thurs anytime after 7, Fri after 3, and anytime Saturday and Sunday	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Git/GitHub	Intro class in high school	One project I am particularly proud of is a personal website I developed to showcase my work and interests while gaining experience in building a website from the ground up. This project was especially exciting for me, because it was my first independent project, without the guidance of a class or teacher. Through my own research, I taught myself the skills to complete the website. I used HTML, CSS, and Javascript for the front-end structure, styling, and interactive elements, and GitHub for version control and hosting. \nA challenge I encountered was the learning curve associated with learning three new programming languages. Navigating these new languages and each of their programming concepts was initially intimidating. I also had issues managing the GitHub repository and merging conflicts. However, these challenges forced me to deepen my understanding of web programming and GitHub, which I recognized the value of. \nStarting a project independently without anyone else’s support was daunting, which compelled me to become resourceful and seek out information online. Although the personal website may not represent my most creative or polished work in terms of design and user experience, I am proud of it because of the valuable lessons I learned throughout, such as problem solving, self motivation, and technical skills that served me well in future projects. \n	\N	8	10	5	3	11