	He/Him	2027	No	Honestly, I think this is a really great opportunity that I can learn a lot from and use to set myself up for a successful career. I come from a low-income background and have had very little exposure to CS growing up. It was something that seemed like a cool field that would forever be outside of my skill set. I actually came to Tufts thinking I’d major in Astrophysics (I’m definitely not cut out for that), but after taking CS 11 in my first semester here, my perspective changed completely. I’m now in the school of engineering as a declared computer engineering major!! Since the requirements of my major are more heavily saturated with electrical engineering classes, and very few software classes (I only have to take up to CS 15), I think a JumboCode project is one of the best ways I can learn serious front and back end software skills. I’m considering taking more CS classes outside of my requirements, so I’d love to take the chance to learn in a team based environment. In fact, I want to join JumboCode because this would be my first opportunity to work on a major programming project outside of classes. Of course I want to earn internships and do some kind of research like everybody else, but I feel like I don’t have the best platform to stand on since my software background and resume is nearly non-existent. I think JumboCode can help me start the process of learning the skills and earning the right to apply for other team-based projects in the future! More importantly though, the actual projects that I can be a part of this year resonate with me and my life experiences. I’ll go more in depth about those experiences in the question below, but most of if not all of the “social justice” volunteering I’ve done has been related to helping low/no-income people. A lot of that volunteering centered around helping with the education of kids who really haven’t been given much opportunity to receive proper schooling. Seeing that a lot of the projects this year are dedicated to organizations that want to help the less fortunate is exciting because it gives me the chance to both learn about the software side of things and leave a meaningful impact on a community that needs help!	I have a very special relationship with social justice work because of the opportunities I’ve been given throughout my life. Starting in middle school, I qualified for and became a part of the REACH program. Among many things, REACH is a program committed to providing social justice minded education to underserved students. I benefited a lot from this program and my attendance at Tufts is major proof of that. My “work” for justice started in the summer before my freshman year in high school when I tried gathering information about the homeless crisis in my neighborhood of Coney Island. A major part of this experience was going around to different people and interviewing them about their backgrounds and the circumstances that lead them to homelessness. I recognized that I alone couldn’t address this problem and give those people a second chance at life. I just hoped that having these conversations would put a more human weight to the overall issue of homelessness in NYC. As someone who’s been temporarily homeless, I recognize how lucky I am to have had my grandparents and their home to provide shelter for my family. This eighth grade project really solidified that fact, since almost every person I interviewed ended up on the street due to incredibly unfortunate circumstances. Since the high school I attended actually created the REACH program, much of this social justice work continued. I participated in the school’s midnight runs, where we hopped in a van at midnight and drove around the streets of Manhattan delivering food to food pantries and people living on the street. I volunteered at De La Salle academy as an educational assistant, which was a middle school with a very similar mission to REACH. I also volunteered as a mentor and counselor for the REACH program! Like I said earlier, I was extremely blessed to receive the education that I did. Because of that, I intend to use my education as a tool for giving back to these communities. So many people poured their time and resources into me, so I think it is only fair that I do the same so that at least one other kid out there can be given the chance to do something great with their life. I hope to help leave the same sort of impact on other peoples' lives as the impact that was left on mine.	Yes	Yes	My Fridays and weekends are completely available! I can find the time during the week, it'll just be a little trickier. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++	I did take a computer science elective in high school, but I think CS 11 was the first "real" introduction.	In my junior year of high school, I was part of the school's "JFR" committee which was pretty much a group of students that worked on a schoolwide social justice project that culminated in a "JFR day" at the end of the school year. The whole point of this project was to apply my high school's core values to a specific social justice theme that was voted for by a group of faculty members. That year, our theme was environmental justice and our task was to plan out seminars, assemblies, and activities throughout the year that engaged the student body in something actionable to participate in our mission. On the actual JFR day, there were 12 different group activities that students could participate in. My involvement in building JFR day and planning the events that lead up to JFR day was evenly split between attending committee meetings and doing my own research. Doing research was easy enough on its own, but the biggest problems came up at these meetings. Since we needed to plan a bunch of different events, there was constantly disagreement between students and faculty about pretty much any idea that came up. The biggest problem we had for pretty much the whole course of this project was actually getting students to care about environmental justice. I mean, how many students actually pay attention to their school wide assemblies, right? At the end of the day, we just had to understand our audience and come up with ideas that made the most sense for them and not necessarily what made the most sense for us. For example, we came up with a schoolwide competition where students recorded their environmentally conscious contributions to an app and received a proportional amount of points. At the end of the competition period, the top scorers were rewarded with gift cards. We also "hired" a group of students in charge of digging through the trash cans during lunch periods to remove recyclable items from them and put them in their proper bins. In the end, our goal was to turn a seemingly boring series of assemblies and seminars into an engaging continuous event that actually had students participating in environmental justice! We were proud of the work we accomplished and even though we probably didn't engage every single student's interest, we at least had everyone participating in our JFR day! 	\N	7	4	10	12	1	8	11	2	3	5	9	6	I tried ranking my preferences based on the projects I feel like I identify/resonate with the most. I'd like to continue serving people from low-income backgrounds and I feel like I'm most strongly suited to working in these sorts of environments. I know I've said this ad nauseum already, but I really want to do my part in helping everyone get their fair shot at living a better life regardless of their financial circumstances!	Thank you for this opportunity! Regardless of how this goes, you guys do awesome work :)	Nope!	Boromir	
