	she/her	2026	Yes	For the past two years, I was able to participate in JumboCode as part of the Bennett Brothers Balm team and then the Bi Women Quarterly team, and I can say with confidence that this club has been one of my favorite parts of attending Tufts. Not only has it taught me how to work on the Front and Back-end of programming projects, but JumboCode has also introduced me to partner programming (and all of the scheduling that goes into working with others) and gave me a sneak peek into more professional projects that will hopefully prepare me for future employment. Most importantly to me, though, this club helped me to feel more connected to the local communities here, as the Bennett Brothers were based out of Saugus, MA; and Bi Women Quarterly, the Boston area. I am hoping that, by doing JumboCode for a third year, I will be able to enhance my learning of code, become even more prepared for professional work as it relates to CS, and help even more people.	As previously mentioned, I have been a part of JumboCode for two years now. For the Bennett Brothers, I helped to make a website that could track their suppliers and expenses for their business in which they sold lip balms and donated the proceeds to cancer research. For Bi Women Quarterly, I had a hand in creating a website that could manage publication submissions from both a user and admin standpoint.\n\nI have also been a part of Tufts’ FOCUS for the past three years, as a member in my Freshman year, support staff in my sophomore year, and leader this year. During my Freshman year, I was part of one of the Racial Justice Advocacy groups, and helped organizations in the Boston area (such as Community Servings by packaging food, and MassVoterTable by canvassing). Last year, I worked with many different groups to volunteer and with the Freshmen directly, making sure their transition from high school to Tufts went smoothly. This year, I was able to head one of the three the groups for Placemaking, a theme focused around maintaining third places such as schools and green spaces without raising prices or displacing locals. We were able to work with SCALE (Somerville Center for Adult Learning Experiences) to paint classrooms, MAMAS (Mutual Aid Medford & Somerville) to discuss mutual aid, and Somerville Community Growing Center to help with upkeep.\n\nAdditionally, this will be my third year as a member in Tuft’s Girls in STEM, which is a club that works with girls attending Andrews Middle School. Once a week, we visit the school, do science experiments with the students, and teach them about important, but typically lesser-known, women in STEM.\n\nIn high school, I was a part of Key Club, which is a community service group, and National Honor Society. I was also a peer tutor for all four years, in which I tutored around 5 high school students each week in various subjects (but usually math and science).	Yes	Yes	Most nights after 7, except for Fridays and Saturdays	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, Python	My first introduction to CS was in elementary school, when I taught myself how to code with Khan Academy. After that, I took AP CS A in my senior year, where I learned Java.	Just last summer, I got into making spreadsheets for random data sets, and taught myself how to use Apps Scripts on Google Sheets, which is basically a way to implement JavaScript into your Google Sheet in order to do more complicated functions. \n\nMy favorite spreadsheet that I have made is a sort of “mock SIS,” in which I created a database with around 500 courses that are currently offered by Tufts (including information about what attributes they have, number of credits, typical semesters run, etc.). Then, in various different tabs, I programmed a way to create a new schedule or upload an old one, see if that schedule fulfills my major and minor requirements, and store that schedule for later use.\n\nTo build the project, I started off quite simply, trying to do as much of the code as I could with the provided functions within Google Sheets. As I did, I made a list of functions I could not make, and then proceeded to program those ones using Apps Scripts. Finally, I made the sheets aesthetically pleasing and added conditional formatting.\n\nThe most challenging part of this project was definitely the code itself. I was still relatively new to JavaScript and Apps Scripts when I began it, so I had to teach myself as I went, much like the coding we do in JumboCode. Another challenging – and less expected – issue I ran into was the efficiency of my code. Because there are so many tabs in the spreadsheet and so many functions, I often found that my code was running too slowly so I needed to find ways to make it simpler and less expensive.	\N	3	1	2	11	10	5	9	8	12	7	4	6	My top choice for projects this year is Village Hub Food, for many reasons. After being a part of FOCUS for the past three years, and having the opportunity to work with groups such as Community Servings and Mutual Aid Medford and Somerville (which connects mutual aid and food via community fridges and delivering groceries), I have become very passionate about issues surrounding food accessibility and inequity. While helping out the Somerville Growing Center during FOCUS this year as well, it was really brought to my attention how limited Somerville's access to clean, open green spaces are, so I really appreciate how this project is connected to maintaining the environment as well through sustainability. In addition to the cause itself, the plan for Village Food Hub's web-app reminds me a lot of the web-app for Bennett Brother's Balm, as they both deal with keeping track of data (Bennett Brothers with suppliers and expenses, and Village Food Hub with inventory and demographics). I found this type of web app to be fascinating and very comprehendible (and very similar to the set-up of a spreadsheet, which I am very