nterface using React Native and Expo, then implementing the back-end using Firebase and Node.js, and finally connecting the two. I used Git/Github for version control throughout. I had very little experience with React Native, so this was challenging. However, with time, effort, and the occasional late-night tutorial sessions, I was able to slowly improve. Additionally, this was my first experience with designing my own tech stack. I spent a lot of time researching different technologies, choosing ones that best fit my project, and then figuring out how to implement them into the project. While this process was frustrating at times, it improved my ability to understand documentation and choose certain technologies for a specific task.\n\nIn the end, building CookNook was incredibly rewarding. I was able to create a functional app that solved a problem I personally experienced, and the project gave me a deeper understanding of mobile development, back-end integration, and using AI to enhance user experiences. While there were moments of uncertainty, those challenges pushed me to learn and grow as a developer. The skills I gained from researching, problem-solving, and iterating on the app’s design have equipped me with greater confidence to take on future technical challenges.	\N	3	1	7	4	9	6	10	5	2	8	11	12	I’m really drawn to projects that make a real difference in local communities. Village Food Hub’s mission to improve access to food and reduce waste is something I care about, and building a system to track their pantry inventory and volunteers would have a huge impact on how they operate. For English At Large, creating a simple library system for their tutors would make accessing resources so much easier, which directly helps immigrants who are working hard to improve their English skills. The West Medford Community Center project is also appealing—helping automate the newsletter process would save the Executive Director a lot of time and let them focus more on oth