	she/her/hers	2027	Yes	Most of my current programming experience comes from the classroom, and while I find it fun and challenging, I have a desire to apply what I'm learning to the real world. This past summer, I started taking a self-paced web development course on Udemy, and I found that I really enjoyed the process of designing visually-appealing, efficient websites that have the potential to help people. I am also fairly new to computer science in general, so I would love to take this opportunity to improve my skills, receive valuable guidance from people with more experience in the field, and give back to the community!	I was a TA for my art studio, where I helped kids aged 5-12 learn a variety of art forms - digital drawing, watercolor, basket weaving, etc - as well as a TA for a 7th grade math class at my local Chinese school. I've also done volunteer work picking up trash around my neighborhood, hosting a crafts table at a community 4th of July event, and playing the violin for elderly people at senior homes.	Yes	Yes	I am currently available Monday through Thursday after 8, Friday after 12, and all day on the weekends.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Node.js/Express, Note that I do have some experience in all of these except for React and TypeScript, but I would not say I'm comfortable with them yet.	I took several summer Python classes in middle school, but nothing stuck until I took Intro to Object Oriented Programming senior year of high school. I then took CS11 last semester and am taking CS15 now.	Coming out of CS11 last semester, I was eager to keep coding and to make a project of my own. Early in the summer, my mom expressed to me her frustration at work - as a scientist, one of her responsibilities was manually sorting through over 700 source racks containing lab samples and re-distributing them into destination racks. There were several constraints that made the problem tedious and and time-consuming - for example, each source rack held a random amount of samples ranging from 0 to 96, and she was required to work in batches of 20 racks at a time. The 20 racks in a batch could be split into any combination of source and destination racks she wanted (ex. 19 source + 1 destination, or 17 source + 3 destination), which added a layer of complexity. Hearing this, I decided that I would create a C++ program that could take in rack data, efficiently distribute the racks, and output the results to a csv file, in order to help save my mom from future headaches.\n\nI am proud of this project because I was able to apply my limited knowledge of C++ and object-oriented programming to create a fully functional program that stored data in an efficient way.  I won’t get into the nitty gritty details, but my program implemented a Source Rack class, a Batch class containing a vector to add Source Racks to, and an array holding the sample frequencies of the Source Racks (the number of source racks containing 1 sample, 2 samples, 3 samples, etc). The solution relied on mathematical analysis of the sample frequencies array to build the batches one by one. It would try out different source racks to add to the current batch, with the goal of filling the destination rack(s) as completely as possible while minimizing the total number of batches, switching strategies when the current one didn't seem to be working out.\n\nThrough this project, I gained a lot of practice planning, testing, and debugging. I also had to do my own research on concepts not taught in CS11, like handling C++ exceptions. Perhaps the most difficult part was translating my human decision process into code - for example, isolating what factors indicate that a strategy is not working so that the program could detect this and move onto a different strategy (without me directly telling it to). I found that the best way to deal with complex coding problems like this was to write a clear, detailed implementation plan. I consider my work to have paid off in the end, especially after seeing the genuine look of appreciation my mom had when I presented her with my solution. I helped make her life easier, and I hope to continue helping others through JumboCode!	https://github.com/Candleliving96	4	1	9	12	2	7	11	6	3	5	10	8	I ranked the projects mostly based on the issues I am most interested in, with food security and education being near the top. I also ranked the West Medford Community Center project pretty high because I have experience writing, publishing, and laying out newspapers from high school. With that being said, I would love to be a part of any one of these projects!	\N	I have not been part of JumboCode before.	Éomer	
