	she/her	2027	Yes	I’m really excited to join JumboCode because I deeply admire the club's commitment to community through its focused projects and team-building initiatives. As an aspiring software engineer, I believe JumboCode offers a valuable opportunity to gain hands-on experience with large-scale projects and develop both my technical and collaborative skills.\n\nBeing part of JumboCode will allow me to work on meaningful projects and grow my professional network, providing valuable exposure to the realities of software engineering. I’m eager to connect with clients, project leaders, and fellow developers, learning from their experiences and contributing to the success of the club’s diverse projects. The welcoming and inclusive environment at JumboCode, along with its strong values of community and growth, deeply resonate with me. I would be thrilled to contribute to the club’s ongoing success and further my development as a software engineer.	Community engagement has always been something I'm passionate about. In high school, I  volunteered at a local park where I helped build a sensory garden for children and participated in beach and trail clean-ups. I also worked with We Care Act NYC, a student-run chapter of the We Care Act non-profit, as their Director of Outreach. Our organizations goal was to increasing access to technology and computer literacy and my department specifically focused on building relationships with organizations for funding and used device donations. Through successful partnerships with local community centers, libraries, and larger organizations, we organized used device donation centers and ran refurbishing workshops. The refurbished devices were then redistributed to underprivileged students.\n\nMore recently, I volunteered with Y2Y at the Harvard Square Youth Shelter, where I assisted with serving visitors dinner and helped with the upkeep of the shelter. JumboCode will hopefully help me to become a more active in my current community.	Yes	Yes	I will be fully available for a one-hour weekly meeting and will also be available for the Sunday all-club meetings!	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, React, Python	My introduction to computer science began in my sophomore year of high school when I worked as a research intern at a clinical psychology lab. I had to learn python as my responsibilities were data cleaning, processing and analysis. As I learned python I really enjoyed the aspect of working with data and even more so the challenge of programming itself. It was a really fun experience for me to play around with code and see what I can do with it. This experience is what i credit for my love for cs!My first intro to CS was 	One project I'm particularly proud of is Zap! from CS15. For this project, I implemented Huffman coding to compress and decompress text files.\n\nI built the program by constructing a Huffman tree. Huffman coding is a lossless compression algorithm that builds a binary tree where more frequent characters have shorter paths from the root, and less frequent characters have longer paths. I started by creating nodes for the two lowest-frequency characters and combined them into a parent node. I continued this process until there were only two parent nodes left, which I then joined to form the root. The tree is designed so that the path from the root to less frequent letters is longer, while more frequent letters have shorter paths, ensuring that we use the least number of bits to encode the text.\n\nThe most challenging part of this project was figuring out the right traversal method for serializing and deserializing the Huffman tree. This step was critical for accurately reconstructing the tree during decompression and ensuring that the encoded text could be correctly decoded. To solve this, I had to ensure that I understood the different binary tree traversal methods and pay close attention to how the Huffman tree was built. I'm a visual learner, and so pictures of trees and traversal paths were a great help, as well as office hours and lecture slides, which ultimately helped me figure out a variation of pre-order traversal algorithm to serialize and deserialize the Huffman tree to accomplish he compression and decompression.	No github but i'm happy to show you my CS projects/HWs so far	10	6	9	7	5	3	8	1	11	12	2	4	I'm genuinely excited about all these projects and the impacts they're making in the various fields they focus on. To elborate on my project preference, I was a tutor for several years and LCS tutoring project resonates with be because I know firsthand the difference a well-organized tutoring system can make for both student and tutors. I would love an opportunity to contribute to LCS tutoring and support tutors in the process. I'm also very impressed by the global reach and impact of A2Empowerment. I care deeply about expanding education especially in areas where women typically don't have access to education and I love that A2Empowerment is taking that leap in supporting women and education. I think it's super inspiring the work A2Empowerment is doing with this project and I would love to contribute to this project and show my support for their mission.\n\nThe New England project and the tufts counsel project are also two project I'm really interested in joining because of they offer na unique opportunity to dive into the legal side a little bit. I've always been interested in law and this would be a great way to learn somethings about justice reform and compliance along with legal processes and how technology can really fit into this field. I think each and every one of the 12 projects are incredibly inspiring impactful and I would welcome an opportunity to contribute to any one of them!	\N	I have not, this is my first year applying!	Goldberry	
