	she/her	2027	Yes	       As someone interested in software development, I find the opportunity offered by  JumboCode to learn new technologies and applications in a team environment while also supporting local nonprofit organizations very appealing. Nonprofits have positively impacted me (I owe my interest in computer science to Girls Who Code, a nonprofit that promotes tech to young women) and I want to give back by helping develop applications that these organizations need to function efficiently. \n\tWhile the theoretical aspects I’ve covered in my computer science classes at Tufts are essential to being successful in a technology-based career, there’s no better education than hands-on experience. Participating in JumboCode will allow me to delve into the project development process and utilize new platforms like Git, AWS, and Vercel, which I wouldn’t learn in a lecture. \n\tBeing a part of a team will allow me to be a part of a community of people similarly interested in giving back to the community while learning from more experienced team members. I’ve already benefited from the advice of upperclassmen, and I would love to learn more from team leaders, especially about which classes to take and how to best navigate the technology field. This past summer, during my internship, I worked on a team and enjoyed the mentorship and learning environment. I hope being part of JumboCode will offer a similarly rewarding experience. 	       I have been greatly affected by many nonprofits promoting women in STEM careers, especially Girls Who Code. I started in their summer immersion program, then became a participant in my high school’s chapter, and eventually I co-led this chapter and also co-founded a GWC club at the middle school level. At Tufts, I’ve participated in Work Prep programs and gained valuable career advice from mentors in fields that interest me. \nI’ve witnessed first-hand GWC’s impact on the next generation of female students, including me. I’ve had rewarding experiences on both sides, as a mentor and a mentee. It has been rewarding to encourage young students and learn from older professionals as part of a nationwide community of women and nonbinary individuals. \n\tGirls Who Code does incredible things for young students, and I’ve loved my experience working with them. I’ve been able to continue participating in female-led groups at Tufts in the Women in Computer Science club, where I am currently on the logistics committee for the upcoming Women in Technology conference. My experiences with nonprofits over the years has given me a deep understanding and appreciation of their impact. Participating in JumboCode offers a great opportunity to continue to make a positive contribution to the broader Boston community. 	Yes	Yes	I am free almost every night after 6pm except Monday and Tuesday. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Python	I joined Girls Who Code in high school!	       This past summer I was developing a project with a friend that lets Tufts students connect by seeing who is in their classes, their clubs, and their dorms. We're both really excited about it, because it was something both of us could've used when we were trying to meet people freshman year. It's still currently in development, but it was a great learning experience because neither of us had built a project on that scale before. We started out using PHP, XAMPP, HTML, and CSS, but then realized that was a really bad idea, so halfway through we switched to Javascript, MongoDB, and HTML/CSS. We faced a lot of challenges along the way, most having to do with working on the backend. We fixed the bugs we found by doing a lot of googling and watching many YouTube videos. It's been a very rewarding experience, and we're going to try and finish it before the end of the year. 	\N	5	4	8	3	9	7	2	11	6	1	10	12	I'm most interested in Bread & Roses and the LGBTQ Senior Housing. I find Bread & Roses interesting because at h