	he/him	2028	No	JumboCode, to me, embodies my favorite parts of programming projects. It’s an organization that does what I have done independently in high school on another level in terms of quality, impact, collaboration, and fun! I want to improve my knowledge of web-development and scripting languages, spend time with coders that are way smarter than I am, increase the number of languages I can read and write, enjoy good food while talking about algorithms, and do more work with databases – my current plan is a major in data science. I want to make a difference, and JumboCode is an incredible opportunity to do just that while benefiting from the experience myself and spending time with people who seem both extremely knowledgeable, welcoming and kind, and pretty hilarious (from what the presentations told me). 	I have volunteered with youth basketball programs, taught golf lessons for kids through a non-profit called First Tee (which aims to increase access to the sport of golf), delivered beds for kids and families in need for the organization My Very Own Bed (for which my mother is one of a few full-time employees), spent time in my old fifth-grade classroom before school, helping out in their math class. I was a tutor for my entire junior and senior year, and I fully plan to be a TA for CS11 next year. I volunteered my time as a manager/assistant coach for my high school basketball team after my season was ended by injury, running pre-season practices and open gyms, starting a website, and keeping the team Instagram I started up-to-date. 	Yes	Yes	I could meet at that time on all days other than Tuesday, and every evening is free other than Wednesday, when I have class until 7. Mondays and Fridays, I am completely free until 1:30pm.	\N	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, SQL, Python	My dad is an MIT software engineer, so I’ve always been around the idea of computer science and definitely someone who was always excited about it to the dorkiest extent. I first came in contact with code in my junior year of high school, when I took IB CS SL, which was in Python, and then HL in my senior year, which was in Java and focused more on OOP, recursion, ADTs, and other techniques. I am currently in CS 11 only because I had very little experience with memory allocation. 	The project I’m most proud of was my CS Internal Assessment coding project in senior year. Quickly, the IA is simple: find a client, their problem, and use computer science techniques to build a solution. My client was my uncle, who was trying to improve his golf swing by visually comparing his golf swing video to that of some PGA professional, trying to eyeball discrepancies between the two. He wanted a more consistent, accurate, and precise way to compare the two swings to gain useful insight. I took this and wanted to build an app that could in some way “quantify” a golf swing and output numbers that could tell the user information.\nThis goes pretty in depth, but just wanted to demonstrate my thought processes about the challenges and the actual algorithms.\nUsing a concept we had been learning about in the IB CS Case Study and through Google’s MediaPipe API, I managed to get a green dot that would track each part of a subject’s body in a video, which is known as Human Pose Estimation. Once the body part key points could be tracked, the fact that each could be represented on a 2D coordinate plane helped me to begin building the statistics. Taking only videos from straight behind the golfer, some of the simpler stats would be something like initial knee bend angle, or initial back angle, which were simply found by discerning the angle between three points in space. One of the most complex statistics to calculate was one that I called top-arm angle, which was essentially the angle at which the golfer’s front arm would make with a horizontal line along the golfer’s shoulder at the moment the wrists were at the highest point of the golf swing. However, you cannot just track the y-values of the wrist and find the max over the entire video, because the follow through is almost always higher than the takeback. Similarly, you can’t solve that problem by taking the second-highest, because it’s very likely that the second highest is also in the follow through. The solution was not perfect, but it worked essentially every time with a video submitted at iPhone slo-mo speed. Using a Python class I built called peakFinder, the current coordinates of the wrist would be constantly stored at each frame of the video until they began to increase (meaning move back down the screen, or when the max point would have already happened). Once this happened, a count threshold variable would begin counting frames that the y-value continued to move down the screen, and once thirty frames had passed it would conclude that the highest point was that last stored maximum. If, at any point, the wrist started going back up, the threshold variable would reset. It wasn’t perfect, however, because often the golfer would move their hands ever so slightly up and down at the beginning of the swing and throw the threshold variable past 30. I added another condition to prevent this: the max wrist value had to be more than 200 pixels above the wrist value in the first frame, making sure it was actually at the top of the swing. I also found success in tracking a stat that would measure how much horizontal shift had taken place in the wrists from the beginning of the swing to when the wrists were at the waist. For example, Rory McIlroy’s number here was 10 pixels forward; Collin Morikawa’s was 10 backward. Mine was 112 forward, so safe to say I had found some useful discrepancy. \nUsing OOP in Python, these statistics were all assembled and then compared, in a TKinter GUI for the user, with the same statistics for Rory McIlroy, and then a “Similarity Score” was created with Pearson’s Correlation Coefficient. The score was saved in a SQL database with the user’s name, score, and date, so that their progress could be observed. Overall, in addition to learning so much more about Human Pose Estimation, SQL queries and tables, OOP, and Python in general, I took away how valuable it is to plan out the concept of how an algorithm will work, like the top-arm angle, before ever coding. The fun, for me, was the conceptual problem solving and trying to make something that actually worked. At the end, I submitted my golf swing and the program gave me a 61/100. I chose another pro at random, Collin Morikawa, and submitted a video of his swing. The program scored him a 99.5/100. In some way or another, reasonable analysis was taking place. \n	\N	9	12	4	10	5	1	11	7	8	6	3	2	I'm really interested in developing my comfort with Databases and Python in the name of data science, so the University Counsel and the Innocence Project would be great, and AWS is very interesting to me. I also think that these organizations are doing such important work, and I would love to be a part of their effort. 	Thank you guys so much for running this amazing organization - no matter what, your mission for community good and learning is top-notch!	\N	Lobelia Sackville-Baggins	
