al responsibility with entrepreneurship, resulting in the donation of over 200 hats to more than 10 cancer wards and hospitals across Thailand. \n	Yes	Yes	M, W: after 7:30pm | Th: 12pm-5pm and after 7:30pm | Fri: after 12pm | Sat/sun: anytime	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, SQL, Python	CS11	One project I’m particularly proud of is my work during my internship at AI AND ROBOTICS VENTURE: CARIVA, where I developed a text classification system for medical notes. The project was exciting because it involved applying natural language processing (NLP) and information retrieval techniques that I learned from Stanford’s XCS224U course. I used tools like Jupyter Lab, Python, Pandas, Scikit-learn, and spaCy to preprocess and classify medical notes, accurately matching them to the appropriate headings.\n\nAnother project I am proud of is that I created a chatbot capable of answering medical questions with a 90% accuracy when compared with the given cases. This involved extensive testing of different models from Hugging Face and refining preprocessing techniques to achieve reliable performance. One of the major challenges I faced was selecting models that could provide a high accuracy rate. This involved extensive experimentation with various models and testing different preprocessing tools to determine the most effective approach. \n\nOverall, the experience was highly rewarding as it allowed me to combine my technical skills with real-world medical data, creating a system that could potentially streamline the workflow for healthcare professionals.\n	\N	10	5	9	12	8	1	11	6	2	4	3	7	I chose the New England Innocence Project as my first choice because, as an international student, I'm eager to learn more about the U.S. legal system. This project offers a unique opportunity to gain insight into how legal processes work while contributing to a cause that has a profound impact on people's lives. My second choice is English at Large because, having gone through the process of 