	he/him	2028	No	I want to join JumboCode because it combines two things I'm passionate about: making a positive social impact, and doing so through the means of coding. I'm captivated with the chance to use my technical skills to contribute to real-world projects that benefit communities, an opportunity I didn't realize was so accessible at Tufts until I researched JumboCode during my college application. By joining the club, I hope to gain hands-on software development experience, as well as learn how to best work in teams on a project, as most of my coding experience has been individual. I'm also excited to make like-minded friends who share the same drive to create change through code.	Throughout middle and high school, I actively volunteered for several non-profits focused on community engagement and inclusion. I worked with the Miracle League of MA, a baseball league for children with physical and cognitive challenges, where I assisted players on the field. Additionally, I volunteered with AB PIP STEM, a non-profit that organizes free STEM activities for K-12 students in my hometown. I've also contributed to local food preparation efforts for homeless individuals and families in Massachusetts. Finally, in my senior year, I served as a TA for the AP Computer Science A class at my high school.	Yes	Yes	Assuming the meetings are at night, I have class from 6:00 - 7:15pm on Monday, 6:00 - 8:20pm on Wednesday, and 6:00 - 9:00pm on Thursday. Other than that, I'm available at any time. (Also free most afternoons if that's the case).	\N	C/C++, Git/GitHub, Python	My first introduction to CS was through Khan Academy around 3rd grade. I started by making simple games like Space Invaders.	One of the projects I’m most proud of is a website I built with a fellow AP Computer Science A TA during my senior year. Our goal was to simplify the coding experience for students, particularly when it came to running unit tests for their assignments. The existing platform, repl.it, had unit test integration, but it was difficult to navigate and often left students confused about how to verify their code. We saw this as an opportunity to make the process more intuitive.\n\nWe developed a website where students could paste their code, select which JUnit tests they wanted to run, and immediately see which tests passed or failed, with clear feedback. This project was particularly exciting because it allowed us to solve a real problem students were facing in real-time. We used JavaScript and HTML to create the user interface and connected it to a back-end that ran the JUnit tests (I mostly worked on the back-end). One of the biggest challenges we faced was implementing testing for all of the specific rules, such as indentation conventions, which had a lot of special cases.	\N	7	6	12	5	4	9	11	3	1	2	10	8	English at Large: As a child of immigrant parents, I've seen firsthand how language barriers can create a sense of isolation and hinder opportunities for integration into a new community. This project resonates with me because it directly supports people who are actively working to improve their English skills and engage in their communities. The idea of creating a library system to organize resources for tutors excites me because it automates a process that shouldn't be manual. I'm passionate about automation and building efficient systems, and I believe this project offers the perfect opportunity to apply those skills while making a meaningful impact.\n\nBread & Roses: I've had the opportunity to volunteer preparing meals for homeless individuals and families, and I've seen how access to nutritious food can uplift a community. Bread & Roses' mission to provide meals in a welcoming environment draws me to the project too, I think it's really cool that the volunteers form relationships with Merrimack Valley's community members. Additionally, I'm enticed with the fact that the team is creating a scheduling web-app, as I keep a pretty meticulous schedule myself. (Also, Late night LeetCode sounds really fun I've been grinding LC recently)\n\nLCS Tutoring: As a former tutor, I know that the tutoring experience is so rewarding for both the tutor and the student. Helping others grow in their academic understanding and confidence is something I'm passionate about, so I'd love to be a part of a project streamlining matching tutors and tutees. It's also awesome that we would be helping students at Tufts. I'd love to be able to help my friends here get help for their classes, especially so they stop complaining about their professors (jk).\n\nDillar Academy: I'd like to join this project for similar reasons as English at Large and LCS tutoring - helping students access education is something I deeply care about, especially when it comes to learning a language that would help unite people. Also, even as a freshman, I've already had my own issues with SIS, so I know that it can be frustrating to have a class registration platform that is inefficient/dysfunctional.\n\nI'd love to join any project, but these particularly caught my interest!	\N	I haven't, being a freshman.	The Witch-king of Angmar	
	she/her	2027	Yes	I would love to join the JumboCode community! One thing that really stood out to me after attending the GIM was that developers learn as they go, which excites me. As someone who entered college with minimal coding experience but enjoyed taking CS11 and CS15, I’m eager to dive into new challenges and expand my skills as a JumboCode developer. I’m especially excited about the opportunity to work on web development and app design, improving both front-end and back-end design systems. I also love the idea of helping local businesses set up more effective and efficient systems! By joining JumboCode, I hope to get better at working with various types of interfaces and design while gaining hands-on experience. Additionally, I see this as a fantastic chance to connect with fellow developers, project managers, technical leads, and designers, building relationships that will enhance both my social and professional growth. I can’t wait to be part of this amazing community, learn from others, and contribute to meaningful projects!	Throughout all four years of high school, I was involved in a non-profit organization that teaches youth AI and coding—AiGoLearning. In my roles as an instructor, Vice President, and Head of Training (volunteer), I taught children Scratch and Python, developed lesson plans, and led the #STEMForAll initiative, which provided free sessions to underrepresented communities. I also worked on recruiting and onboarding high school and college instructors, including interviewing them and reviewing parent feedback. This experience taught me a great deal about teaching and the behind-the-scenes work of a non-profit organization, which was newly established during the COVID-19 pandemic. Additionally, I served as a Leadership Intern (volunteer) at Camp Invention in my hometown for four years. There, I led groups of about twenty elementary school children, collaborating with instructors to guide campers in building STEM-related projects and encouraging them to think creatively using recycled materials.\n\nIn college, I am an avid volunteer. I volunteer weekly at Boston Children's Hospital for four hours, where I plan events with employees and interact with patients to give caretakers a break from the hospital's chaos. I am also a volunteer at the Y2Y (Youth to Youth) shelter in Harvard Square, where I serve dinners to young adults in collaboration with Harvard University dining halls. My duties include interacting with residents, serving food, cleaning, preparing bedding for new guests, and helping guests develop certain life skills. In my spare time, I am part of the Cultural Bridge Club, continuing from high school, where I tutor children from Mainland China in English. Lastly, I am involved with the SIS Lab (Social Identity and Stigma Lab), where we work on coding projects to highlight stigmatization and attend conferences to present various projects. This year, I hope to become a mentor for children in a few other clubs, such as Junior Jumbos and Strong Women Strong Girls, where I can inspire and support the youth.	Yes	Yes	Below is my availability for a one-hour weekly team meeting:\n\nMondays: 6:00 PM - 10:00 PM\nTuesdays: 12:00 PM - 2:00 PM and 8:00 PM - 10:00 PM\nThursdays: 4:00 PM - 6:00 PM and 8:00 PM - 10:00 PM\nFridays: 6:00 PM - 10:00 PM\nSaturdays: Anytime after 12:00 PM\nSundays: Anytime after 12:00 PM	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, Git/GitHub, Python, I am a fast learner and am eager to learn!	In the summer before my freshman year of college, I taught myself Python. CS11 was my first formal introduction to C/C++.	Last year, I participated in BostonHacks with a friend and two people I met at Boston University. We developed a mobile app called RELASTIC to make eco-friendly choices more intuitive. Specifically, by using eco-friendly products, users could earn points, and this reward system allowed them to redeem points for other items. I was the designer for the team, focusing on creating a user-friendly interface and selecting color schemes. I also collaborated with the Boston University students on the back-end development. Personally, it was challenging because we were working under a time constraint and I was learning as I went, but we managed to finish on time.\n\nAnother project I enjoyed was SushiGo! in CS11. SushiGo! is a game my family and I often played when I was younger, so it was nostalgic to turn this card game into a Terminal and VSCode application. The coding portion was obviously challenging, especially when it didn’t pass the tests despite knowing how it was supposed to work, but the desire to see the end result motivated me to persist.	My projects are shared on GitHub.	5	8	2	10	4	9	12	7	6	3	1	11	Honestly, I can see myself being a good fit for any of the groups mentioned – lovely vibes 🤭. Since this is my first year applying to be in JumboCode, I based my preferences on my past experiences with volunteering and working. With extensive experience in teaching and tutoring children, I ranked the teams focused on education highly. Additionally, having worked in a food shelter, I was particularly interested in teams addressing food security. After considering my interests and how much I relate to the various teams, I ranked them accordingly. That being said, I am very open-minded and would be excited to work on multiple teams within JumboCode that address different issues.	I would love this opportunity! Thank you for your time and consideration!	This is my first year applying to be a part of JumboCode!	Khamûl	
