g tiebreaker rules. I had to implement trackers for head-to-head results, conference records, and strength of victory comparisons. \nThe initial version of the forecaster was developed in Python, however, I am currently in the process of transitioning the project to C++ to highlight it as a data-structures project and improve the tool's efficiency. \n\n(I’m currently in the process of converting the scripts, so the tool on my GitHub is poorly documented and requires some manual data entry before running. The main scripts are available, but if you’d like to see it in action, feel free to PM me).	https://github.com/Rstark28	7	6	5	10	12	2	9	4	11	1	8	3	When sorting the projects, I considered each organization's mission, the proposed solution's features, my interest in building the solution, the tech stack and my comfort level with it (or my desire to learn it), and the skills and interests of the project leads.\n\nI landed on my top choice, Bread and Roses, because of the organizations direct involvement in community relations and the enthusiastic project leads.	\N	\N	Shagrat	
