	he/him	2026	No	The main reason I want to join JumboCode is that I want to get more involved with the CS community at Tufts, as well as wanting to contribute to a project with actual clients and working in a team with other students. I like that all of these projects benefit real people as opposed to CS homework which only benefits me. 	I've been a TA for CS11, CS15, and this semester CS105. To be honest, I haven't worked with non-profits outside of volunteering at a kitchen in my hometown. In high school I made a chrome extension that blocked facebook ads and also collected a database of those ads to see the extent by which facebook allowed misinformation in its ads. 	Yes	Yes	I should be mostly free on weekends, and throughout the week I have a lot of availability on Mondays and Wednesdays. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 105: Programming Languages, CS 160: Algorithms	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	My dad showed me scratch when I was in elementary school.	I'm pretty proud of the Facebook adblocker chrome extension (which I no longer maintain :( ), since Facebook in particular is pretty sophisticated about disguising their ads as normal content. At least at the time that I was making my adblocker, the word "Sponsored" which they were required to include for every ad was split in the HTML source code so that every letter was in it's own tag. Furthermore, between every letter was a bunch of junk letter tags (much like filesofpix), so that figuring out whether or not a post was sponsored or not from the HTML required examination into which letters were actually showing up on the page, and which ones were hidden. There were some other details which I don't fully remember, but that was the main challenge that I was really relieved to figure out, since at the time most of the main adblockers couldn't block facebook ads. I was also pretty happy about the result, since I was able to assemble my own database of facebook ads, and I was able to find some ads that were sketchy. For example, there was an ad for a sign that said "No vaccine solicitation", which by itself isn't misinformation. However, following that ad to it's website led to a website spewing misinformation about vaccines. It was fascinating to me comparing the extreme ads in the database to the ads that my parents saw in their facebook feeds, since the types of ads they saw were far different. IDK if this info is important but I used typescript to make the extension and mongodb for the database. 	\N	1	7	5	6	8	9	11	2	4	3	10	12	I chose my top project choices because I feel that they are especially connected to communities within Tuft's local surroundings, and I feel like I could do more to involve myself in them. In particular, I like that these projects help programs that encourage community engagement from within the community rather than acting as an outside force to the betterment community, although that is still obviously valuable. That being said, I wouldn't have a problem working on any of the projects and would be happy on any of these teams. 	\N	No. 	Hirluin	
