	He/Him	2028	No	I want to join JumboCode for three main reasons: to use my web development skills to benefit others, to connect with people who share similar interests, and to improve my personal and professional skills by working with a client and a team. Up until now, the websites I’ve built have been for personal enjoyment or as platforms for social media and outreach, either for myself or a school group, without a larger purpose. Joining JumboCode would allow me to continue doing what I love while making a meaningful impact for a broader community.\n\nAt the GIM, I saw that the current members of JumboCode are the kind of people I would enjoy collaborating with. Everyone was friendly, outgoing, and most importantly, excited about their projects. Some of the clubs and student groups I’ve been in before have been less friendly towards newcomers so I am excited to work with a group of people as equally motivated as me. \n\nCurrently, I would describe myself as an amateur web developer, as I primarily use raw HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to build my projects. Based on the tech stacks presented at the GIM and the workflows described by the Tech Leads, I believe that joining JumboCode will help me develop more professional web development skills. This experience would allow me to create more accessible websites in the future, making them usable for a wider range of people.	\tIn high school I did a lot of volunteer work with a community farm called Unity Farm Sanctuary that rescues large animals in the area around my hometown. This has been my favorite volunteer experience as there were many events such as open houses which spread awareness about the cause and I also got to know the animals. I also volunteered for two summers at my town library as an English, Math, and Computer Science tutor for young students. There weren’t many tutors for young students (elementary - early middle school) in my town, so the library started the program to fill in the education gap.	Yes	Yes	- Monday 7:00am - 5:00pm\n- Wednesday 7:00am - 12:00pm\n- Thursday 7:00am - 11:00am\n- Friday: 11:00am - 10:00pm\n- Every other Saturday: 7:00am - 5:00pm\n\nI could also meet via Zoom if teams prefer that!	In the process of taking CS11!	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Python	Scratch and Code.org in elementary school! Scratch became my favorite platform for computer science as I enjoyed being able to draw art and use it in my games!	This summer I worked with a group of Tufts students to develop a website for a platform called ‘Puppy AI’. Puppy AI is a series of lessons centered around the LEGO SPIKE that teach students the basic concepts of reinforcement learning. When I joined the team they had already developed the lessons and built a very basic website. Thus, my primary role became front-end development for the site. \n\nWe used raw HTML, CSS, and JS to build the website. We stored everything (code, images, icons, etc) in a GitHub repository and committed to the repository via VS Code through GitHub Desktop. The project was unique because all of the code that we had to run on the SPIKE was in Python. Thus, we used Micro Python to embed and run Python files and Python editors in the page. \n\nMany technical challenges arose due to the two-way communication challenges between the site and the SPIKE itself. We also struggled to enable Bluetooth functionality. Working primarily on the front-end development, I faced many redesigns after we tested the website on students in the area and also on my coworkers. In particular there was a code editor portion of the website that took a while to build as all of its features needed to be clear to the user yet not too overwhelming to use. This was the first web dev project I had ever really done in a group so it was difficult at first to coordinate all of our commits to the repository. However, once we started to communicate more it became very fun to be able to bounce ideas off of my teammates!\n	Personal Website: https://www.goldmanwilliam.com/  Github: https://github.com/iliketocode2	4	8	10	1	7	11	9	5	2	3	6	12	Somerville Museum Group\nMy first choice is the Somerville Museum Group because I am an avid museum-goer! I’ve always enjoyed visiting the Science Museum, the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston, and others. In my opinion, helping museums is very important since they allow others to share their art and ideas, encouraging self expression while also preserving culture and history. I’ve never really thought about how museums archive their collections, so this would be an exciting opportunity to learn more about that process. In addition, creating a database for the store and for the archives seems like it will be a challenging but interesting problem; I have never created a large database before so this will be a first! Holden and Zach brought great energy to their presentation, and I believe working with them would be a lot of fun! Finally, I have yet to actually go to the Summerville museum, so this would also be an opportunity for me to learn about and actually contribute to a museum in my local area. \n\nEnglish at Large\nI chose English at Large as my second option because during my time teaching I could have benefited from a similar resource. The last few summers I’ve been teaching students how to code. However, the curriculum for many of these programs is often patchy and doesn’t provide a full education for the students. Having an easy place to pull resources from would have made a huge difference in how I was able to teach students and progress them through their learning. Although English at Large focuses on a different subject area than my own experience, I can see how this project could significantly impact both teachers and students. It would feel very rewarding to know that my work could make such an impact on the education of others.\n\nBread and Roses\nI ranked Bread and Roses third due to my past experience working with food pantries. When I lived on Cape Ann, my school held food drives for The Open Door, a local food pantry. I think that food security is one of the most important issues in our country and people should never have to go hungry. The impact of Bread and Roses is substantial in our state and I would like to be part of this change.\n\nRemaining Preferences\nI ranked the rest of my preferences based on how connected I feel to the cause each group supports. I was drawn to certain organizations because of my personal background and past experiences. Additionally, I’m excited about the possibility of working with Amazon Cloud Services, so I prioritized those projects slightly higher on my list.	\N	\N	Imrahil	
