s through Harvard's free online intro course called CS50, which I took the summer before Freshman year. The first coding courses I took in a school setting, however, were at Tufts (CS11).	I am proud of a personal project that I started over the summer which is currently in the works called Tufts Handoff. It is a full stack web app that will allow Tufts students to buy/sell furniture or other items to other students, and I got the idea when I had trouble finding furniture for my off-campus rental. I started working on the project with no web dev experience or classes under my belt, but was able to teach myself a lot of interesting tech. Through online tutorials and doing smaller projects for each technology, I was able to teach myself React, Express, MongoDB, and how to build a RESTful API from scratch. I also implemented user authentication with Firebase and image storage with Cloudinary. I have a large portion of the project done, and plan on learning/implementing the remaining features to hopefully get the website online this semester. I am proud of the hours I put into Handoff and believe I will have an easier time learning new skills because of it. I would be excited to apply web development knowledge in a more formal team setting and continue learning from experienced tech leads and developers.	\N	1	9	12	7	11	10	8	5	4	2	6	3	My first choice is the West Medford Community Center, as it is a challenging project that blends technologies I am accustomed to (React, Tailwind, MongoDB, TypeScript) with those I am excited to learn (Prisma, AWS, Vercel). WMCC has a broad outreach across the entire Medford community, and I would be excited to work on a project that can reach such a large number of people of all ages. I know firsthand that the team leaders are all very experienced, as I've actually met with Winston before to get advice about my personal web development project. \n\nMy second choice is Bread and Roses. I have experience volunteering at food pantries and I kn