on	My first introduction to computer science was at my town's summer camp, where I learned Scratch animal programming in one of my courses. 	A project that really excited me was the Gerp project in CS15: Data Structures. The project task was to create a program that given a file directory, could query the directory for user-inputted words. It needed to be able to search in case-sensitive and insensitive. The project excited me because of its complexity - to build it, my teammate and I represented the file directory as a tree. We then parsed through this tree, storing each word of each file as a key in a HashMap that mapped words to their occurrences (line #, directory, and line) within the file system. The biggest challenge I faced was with duplicate capitalization words on the same line during case-insensitive search - our program incorrectly printed duplicate lines for the different capitalizations of the same word on the same line. We ultimately solved this using a Set and storing all the file#, line# as pairs within this set, then reprinting manually the lines using this set. Apart from this, it was just an amazing and exciting challenge to think of the numerous project parts - a 2D vector to represent files and their lines, a giant HashMap of words, a file tree, etc. But ultimately, it was my most proud programming project ever since it was quite complex and so rewarding when it finally worked out in the end. My partner (Jennifer Luo, PM of English at Large) and I had so much fun working together on this project. 	\N	6	12	11	5	8	3	9	7	2	4	10	1	I'm really interested in the Tufts General Counsel team because I'm particularly interested in the intersection between software and healthcare - with both parents having been Software Engineers at medical companies, I found it amazing how the software built could impact so many lives, and I hope to be able to make a similar impact on others through working on this project. Moreover, I've been very interested in learning 