le to work with children and found a fun way to make learning fun for them, which was by incorporating a lot of jokes and talking to them about their interests.\n	Yes	Yes	I am free usually after 6PM every day	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub	My first experience with computer science was in my 8th-grade computer class. Throughout the class, we mainly focused on how we can type the fastest but eventually delved into basic web development which we learned through Code.Org. We learned HTML, CSS, and a little bit of Javascript at the time. The website used blocks to let us code our websites, but there was a functionality to turn off the blocks so we could write the code ourselves. I wanted to challenge myself so I did that, and found that I had a general understanding of the syntax, and began to continue teaching myself how to code. I fell in love with coding. Being able to create and see results of your hard labor is extremely inspiring and only keeps me driven to keep learning CS. 	     A year ago I was introduced to a program called Tech Flex Leaders by my cousin who was previously in this program. This program taught HTML, CSS, and Javascript, and then assigned you to groups of 4 to create a website for a mock client. My team only had 3 people because one of the members didn’t contribute to the project at all, so my team and I were forced to take on more responsibility. We were assigned to make a cookbook recipe app, where if you put in 3 ingredients the program will spit out 4 recipes that you can use and replicate in the real world. I was in charge of the Javascript, while my other team members split the HTML and CSS. Taking on the Javacsript alone was daunting, and I came across a lot of issues during it. One of the most prominent issues was figuring out how to make the program give out 4 recipes when the API only taught how to give 1. This process involved a lot of W3 schools and a lot of dead ends, however eventually I realized the solution was staring at me in the face. I had to add another input to the function that was I, so each recipe was assigned to that value of I, and then I was able to spit out a recipe for that. My team put a lot of hard work into that website, and it eventually paid off in the end resulting in us doing well during our presentations to the other students.\n	https://final-project-spring-2023.darrylde.repl.co	5	10	7	9	1	4	8	6	2	3	11	12	1. Im very passionate about this project because I am also a muslim person and knowing that the Uyghur people can easily have a more accessible education from work that I can work on really speaks to me. I am really enjoyed hearing from the PM's as they all seemed like amazing people to work with! 2. I also really liked the idea of English at Large because my entire family are immigrants and they have struggled to learn English at common community college classes so an application to help through small group instruction would be beneficial to both them and the Boston community. \n3.Bread and Roses is also a project that I am passionate about working on because there is a lot of food insecurity in my neighborhood in los Angeles so making an application to tackle that would be beneficial for both residents in Boston and in cities. \n4.New England Innocence Project: I was interested in learning about this project from the presenters because this isn't a issue that Is common from where I am from but the idea of helping these innocent people and prevent future wrongful convictions would be something I am very interested in!\n5. The West Medford Community Center is something I am interested in as well because I liked the idea of bringing people together through newsletter and publications.\nThe rest of them I ranked based on personal interest!	Not at the moment!	Yes! I worked in the Lantern Club with my PM's being Sristi and Thomas.	Bard the Bowman	
	He/His	Masters Student (Tufts undergrad)	Yes	As a fifth-year student at Tufts, I’ve always wanted to participate in JumboCode but never found the time. Now, I’m excited about the opportunity to finally engage in one of the projects and connect with more people in the CS community. Being part of JumboCode would allow me to check off a long-standing goal, while expanding my network and contributing to meaningful projects.\n\nAnother key reason I want to join is to diversify my resume. In past job interviews, I’ve often been asked about my ability to work in teams and contribute to the success of group efforts. JumboCode presents a great chance to strengthen these skills by collaborating on real-world coding projects. I’m eager to refine my teamwork abilities and contribute to impactful, collective work.\n\nLastly, I have a solid background in web development, including experience with JavaScript, HTML, CSS, and hosting methods for application deployment. As a TA for CS-20, I’ve built on this knowledge and I’m now looking to apply it in a practical setting. By joining JumboCode, I hope to deepen my understanding of frameworks like React.js and Next.js, and gain exposure to a broader tech stack. This experience will not only help me solve real-world problems through technological innovation but also shape the next steps in my career.	Since my freshman year at Tufts, I’ve been actively involved in volunteering. I coordinated a national project in Brazil (BRASA-Pre) to mentor 100 students—80% of whom were from public schools—to prepare for American college applications.\n\nAt Tufts, I engaged with the Tisch Scholar program, the university’s highest civic honor program, for three years. Through this program, I took courses in the Civic College and volunteered at the Cambridge Health Alliance in their Public Health department, where I provided IT support and assisted in the allocation of half a million dollars to NGOs during the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\nAdditionally, I volunteered as a web developer for Professor Lisa Diorio, helping build a website for the Tufts Human Factors & Ergonomics Society. Though this eventually became paid work, it began with my desire to gain experience and contribute to a meaningful project.\n\nLast summer, I utilized the Tufts Career Grant to teach robotics and project innovation to children in Brazil, preparing them for the FIRST LEGO League (FLL) competitions, which focus on building autonomous robots using LEGO. I’ve also volunteered as a judge for FLL regional tournaments in Brazil.\n\n	Yes	Yes	Monday: 4-8pm\nTuesday: 4-8pm\nWednesday: 4-8pm\nFriday: 8am-6pm\nSaturday: 8am-6pm	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 105: Programming Languages, CS 160: Algorithms, CS 170: Computation Theory	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Python	FIRST Lego League Robotics in high school	One project I’m particularly proud of is building the website for the Tufts Human Factors & Engineering Psychology Society. I was invited to work on this by Professor Lisa Diorio, who reached out to me after I performed well in her Web Programming class. This opportunity allowed me to apply my web development skills in a practical setting while learning new concepts along the way.\n\nThe project was a great way to keep my web programming knowledge fresh, and I learned the importance of creating user-friendly interfaces. For example, the site needed to be easily maintained by non-technical users, so we opted to use WordPress as the content management system—something I initially didn’t see as a priority but later recognized as essential for long-term maintenance.\n\nOne of the biggest takeaways from this experience was the importance of client feedback and communication. Regular check-ins with the clients helped reduce unnecessary revisions and ensured we were aligned on the goals of the project. Additionally, I learned the value of maintaining consistency in design, especially when working in teams. Following the same CSS guidelines for colors, fonts, and spacing minimized confusion and made collaboration smoother.\n\nThis project challenged me to think beyond just writing code and focus on user experience, client needs, and effective teamwork—lessons that will undoubtedly help me in future real-world projects.	https://fifth-island.github.io/fifth-island-portfolio/	1	2	5	4	11	7	3	6	10	9	8	12	West Medford Community Center (First Choice)\n\nI am particularly drawn to the West Medford Community Center project because of the opportunity to build a user-friendly web app that simplifies newsletter creation and management. Improving user experience (UX) is a skill I want to further develop, and this project provides an excellent platform to design applications that are accessible, intuitive, and useful for users with varying technical abilities. Additionally, the tech stack for this project is exciting. I have a strong interest in CSS and have been eager to explore TailwindCSS as a way to enhance my frontend design capabilities. This project will also give me the opportunity to further my experience with Prisma, which I’ve worked with in the past year.\n\nMoreover, I’m currently enrolled in a Big Data course, where I’m increasing my knowledge of Amazon Web Services (AWS). This project’s use of AWS aligns with my academic focus and will help me build a stronger foundation in cloud services. Overall, this project combines several of my interests, from UX to cutting-edge technologies, making it my top choice.\n\nVillage Hub Food (Second Choice)\n\nI am also highly interested in the Village Hub Food project. The mission of increasing food access and promoting sustainability resonates deeply with me, as I have experience in this area from my time volunteering with the Cambridge Health Alliance. During that time, I helped maintain the Somerville Food Coalition’s website and track food access, which helped me realize the value of using tech for social good. Although I didn’t build a web app then, this project offers me the chance to apply my skills in a meaningful way.\n\nThe tech stack is another aspect that excites me, as it shares similarities with the West Medford project, including the use of TailwindCSS and Prisma. Additionally, building a system to track food pantry inventory and demographics would allow me to use my skills to support a cause I’m passionate about, while gaining valuable experience in web development.	\N	No	Beorn	
