	He/Him	2026	No	Over the past three years, my college experience has transformed my relationship with coding making me more fond of the discipline, especially since I had little exposure to it before coming to Tufts. Now that I have a more developed foundation than three years ago, I want to join JumboCode to continue nurturing my passion and explore the meaningful impact coding can have on nonprofit organizations and beyond. JumboCode offers me the invaluable experience of working on real-world projects while connecting with like-minded individuals who can help me grow. I’ve faced countless challenges, frustrations, and self doubt in my coding ability, but my journey with coding has also taught me patience, perseverance, and how to overcome failures. Furthermore, those moments have elevated my ability to tackle coding problems at a level than I initially thought possible. I’ve come to realize that the process of taking a project from concept to completion brings me immense satisfaction, and I’m excited for the opportunity to further test and develop my skills with JumboCode. Achieving quality working code is what makes the journey worthwhile, and it drives my hunger to grow as a developer and expand my toolkit. Joining JumboCode would also allow me to contribute to the community in a meaningful way and knowing that our work could have the chance of impacting organizations in a positive manner is incredibly exciting and rewarding. 	Last semester, I took a class called “Computing for Developing Regions,” which gave me the opportunity to work on research projects focused on addressing the needs of underserved communities. My project, a web application called Find-A-Friend, aimed to connect isolated and needy individuals in South Asia (specifically India, Pakistan, Bangladesh) who had access to WhatsApp, the region's most popular messaging platform. I worked mainly on the front-end interface and leveraged a custom WhatsApp chatbot and AI to match people based on shared interests and conversation patterns. While the project initially targeted low-income users, it became clear that it could also serve a broader audience. However, the experience challenged me to account for the specific limitations faced by low-income individuals, such as limited internet speed, technology costs, ease of use, and cultural factors like gender roles, which significantly shape how technology is used in countries like Pakistan (in class, we learned that men in Pakistan hold more power than women and as a consequence, technology is more widely used in the male gender). In addition to this experience, I recently joined the Tufts Theta Chi Philanthropy Committee and will be participating in initiatives such as the Tufts Day of Service, the Breast Cancer Walk, and fundraising activities like Waffle Sales, where all proceeds go to the Harvard Square Homeless Shelter. I also have experience working with the YMCA of Metropolitan Los Angeles as a summer camp counselor, where I chaperoned and mentored kids of various ages.	Yes	Yes	I am flexible this semester and will be available weekly. I'm only taking three courses for this fall with the intention of devoting a good amount of my time to clubs I'm involved with and other extracurricular activities.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, Python, MongoDB, Tailwind (basic knowledge), Figma	CS11	One personal project that excited me this summer was building my own website. The project went through several iterations and, while it’s not yet accessible online, it has been a valuable learning experience. Initially, I used HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to build the basic structure and functionality. I also used Figma to design the layout, which helped me visualize the user interface. Once I gained a foundational understanding, I transitioned to using React for its component-based architecture, and Tailwind CSS to streamline styling. The main goal of this project was to expose myself to different web development tools and languages, learning as much as I could along the way. Although my website is still in production and hasn't been hosted yet, the process has been both challenging and rewarding. Two key obstacles I’m currently facing are: 1) mastering the learning curve of Tailwind CSS to implement a more dynamic and responsive design, and 2) navigating the process of purchasing, registering, and efficiently using web hosting for my domain, as I plan to maintain the site long-term. My vision for the site is to create a sleek, modern, and highly interactive experience, similar to the personal websites of experienced developers I admire. While I recognize that achieving that level of polish requires a deep technical understanding, I’m eager to continue improving my skills and overcoming these challenges as I move forward.	https://github.com/jpuka01	8	2	12	5	1	11	6	7	9	3	4	10	All the projects show great potential in terms of my interest for working with web applications, as well as more database driven projects. However I lean towards more on web-app based projects as the front-end programming languages mentioned in the GIM is what I feel most comfortable working with, but I also back-down on more back-end oriented projects as well if need be.	Just excited to hear back!	\N	Thrór	
