	she/her	2028	Yes	I stumbled upon freelance web design in an attempt to teach myself HTML and CSS in high school. In order to practice what I had learned, I reached out to struggling local businesses asking if I could redesign their websites; before long, this became one of my main extracurriculars. Although this work was extremely rewarding, I lacked a mentor and the backing of a team in order to scale the project to non-static content. What stood out to me about JumboCode was the robust support and opportunities that it provides for students like myself to build impactful web solutions. Since I did not have access to mentorship in my freelance position, I really appreciate that each team offers a formal structure with upperclassmen PM, TL, and Designers who supervise each project. For this reason, I also plan to be very active in the JumboCode community through participating in technical workshops, trainings, and any other networking opportunities.	PRESIDENT AND FOUNDER, Sage Hill Coders OCTOBER 2020 TO JUNE 2024\nRan events and weekly meetings | Developed and implemented coding projects for school clubs and other members of the community | Competed in team hackathons | Created website | Managed Instagram\n\nCODING TEACHER, Sage Hill School SUMMER 2022, 2023, 2024\nIn charge of annual coding camps | Curates and teaches stimulating courses in website development and artificial intelligence | Only student teacher | Privately tutors custom curriculums year-round\n\nFREELANCE WEB DESIGNER AND SOCIAL MEDIA MANAGER,\nJUNE 2021 TO PRESENT\nDesigns and updates websites | Shares engaging content on Facebook and Instagram, increasing number of followers by 200% on average | Works with small local businesses in Southern California\n\nHEAD OF WEBSITE DEVELOPMENT AND TEACHER’S ASSISTANT, Sage Prosthetics\nAUGUST 2022 TO JUNE 2024\nHandled all updates to our website | Mentored younger students on website management | Supervised student projects, primarily including free, custom hand prosthetics for children\n\nC.E.O. AND FOUNDER, Super Pengoo Studios JUNE 2021 TO PRESENT\nPublished Therabot! (mental health journaling app) on Apple and Android App Stores | Distributed Therabot! for use in clinical trials and formal therapy sessions | Designed website\n\nTAEKWONDO INSTRUCTOR, Victory Taekwondo JULY 2021 TO PRESENT\nLeads classes multiple times per week (when I'm back home!) | Supervises community events | Mentors young students | 3rd degree black belt\n\nSTUDENT LEADER, Sage Advocates of Multicultural Education [SAME]\nMAY 2021 TO JUNE 2024\nLead weekly inclusivity efforts on campus | Presented yearly at Sage Hill’s Multicultural Symposium | Attended SDLC in 2020 as the first freshman nominee and Impact: Creating Youth Advocates in 2021	Yes	Yes	I am available Monday-Wednesday after 10:30 am, Tuesday-Thursday after 4:30 pm, and Friday-Sunday all day -- with a few exceptions. Monday, I have a lab from 7-9:30, and Wednesday, I have a recitation from 3-3:50 and a lab from 6-8.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, ES 4	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Swift, Git/GitHub, Python, Java + JavaFX, Gluon Mobile, Swift, Unity, and C#	I took an "Hour of Code" workshop in 7th grade and immediately began weekly coding lessons with my grandfather, a computer science professor, that lasted until my senior year of high school and reached the graduate level.	I believe in mental health awareness, specifically destigmatizing self-care such as psychotherapy, journaling, and meditation. During the pandemic, I was startled by the lack of access and recognition for mental health resources. Mental health struggles are real, and, contrary to public belief, they affect everyone. \n\nTo address this issue, I developed a journaling program, Therabot, using Java and JavaFX. Beyond offering custom journaling prompts, Therabot stores the mood score, or self-reported mood change, from each journaling session in an encrypted, local file and reports insights from that statistic using an artificial intelligence-based algorithm. Therabot received first place in the Orange County Science and Engineering Fair, but I knew that its potential extended further. I immediately contacted my school’s administration to orchestrate mass test trials at my school. With overwhelmingly positive feedback, most testers improved their mood score but noted that the software would be more convenient on their phones. Thus, it was very important that I created Therabot!, the mobile application; however, this process did not come without challenges.\n\nThe biggest hurdle I climbed was adapting Therabot! for both Apple and Android, who maintain separate requirements. Java, JavaFX, GitHub, CSS, XML, Gluon Mobile, NetBeans, \nEclipse, Ubuntu, Terminal, and Swift, most of which I was unfamiliar with prior to pursuing this project, made this mobile diversity possible. Without a mentor to help me learn these languages, I utilized YouTube along with public forums during the learning process. Initially, I had difficulty, but as my expertise grew, so did my patience. I am proud of myself for persevering because I was able to implement all the features I had hoped to include.\n\nWith accessibility at the forefront of this transition, the app is available for free and independent of Wi-Fi. Therabot! has received recognition from Congress and within the mental health community. The most valuable reward from this extensive project, however, has been the skills gained and lives touched along the way.\n\nMy experience in developing Therabot excites me because it demonstrates how computer science and education empower us to impact the world. I find it inspiring that coding allows us to positively impact our greater community and address topics that matter to us. 	I'm still working on organizing my portfolio, but let me know if you want to see a specific project that I've mentioned!	9	6	10	1	4	11	5	3	8	2	7	12	1. Somerville Museum - Designing the database for a museum so close to us here at Tufts sounds super impactful, and I appreciate how much flexibility there is in building a project from scratch. Also, I really appreciated the shitpost on the slides; it seems like the leadership team in that project is really chill. :)\n\n2. Bread & Roses - This project also sounds really impactful as food insecurity is still a major humanitarian issue. I think the concept of making a web-app for volunteers sounds very effective and in-alignment with my previous experiences. Additionally, the leadership team also seems very fun for this group.\n\n3. LCS Tutoring - Speaking from personal experience, finding the right tutor can make all the difference for a struggling student. I think integrating technology into the matching process is a really effective way of tackling this problem. I also really appreciate that this project impacts our fellow Jumbos!	Thank you so much for considering me for JumboCode; I look forward to hearing back from you!	n/a -- I'm a freshman :(	Azog	
