	he/him	2027	Yes	Jumbocode offers a unique opportunity to merge my professional development goals with meaningful, community-centered work. I’m eager to gain hands-on experience in software engineering and to develop my proficiency in SQL, a critical skill for the data science and analyst roles I am pursuing. However, what excites me most about Jumbocode is that the projects directly benefit communities in need, which resonates with my personal desire to use my computer science skills to help those less fortunate than myself. By collaborating with a diverse team of computer scientists, I hope to learn problem-solving from other perspectives and gain the experience necessary to drive change both for my career and within different communities. 	I’ve always been committed to fostering social change within my community, particularly by helping others access professional development opportunities. I was fortunate to attend schools with strong networks that provided resources to help me secure internships and prepare for my career. Many of my peers, however, didn’t have access to the same opportunities. As a way to give back, I’ve worked to bridge that gap by connecting them to my personal network and helping them find internships and career-building resources. My goal is to facilitate introductions between young people in my neighborhood and professionals in fields they are interested in. My hope is to continue supporting underprivileged communities by helping them overcome barriers to success.	Yes	Yes	I'm available everyday after 2:45 and 11:45 on Friday. I'm very flexible on the weekends.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++	My first introduction to computer science was an Introduction to Java course I took my Sophomore year of high school.	The project I’m most proud of involved measuring the amount of misinformation about stocks and investing within the Tufts community using Bayesian modeling with PyMC. This was a significant milestone for me, as it was the first time I had to acquire new skills from scratch. Prior to this, I had no experience with Bayesian statistics, very limited knowledge of Python, and no familiarity with the PyMC package. Through a combination of seeking guidance from peers and professors and dedicating time to self-study, I was able to grasp the fundamentals of Bayesian statistics and effectively utilize PyMC to build a Bayesian model. This model used student test scores to represent and quantify the level of misinformation surrounding stocks and investing at Tufts. Completing this project not only deepened my understanding of statistical modeling but also strengthened my problem-solving and learning abilities, which I now apply to other challenges.	\N	10	5	2	4	7	1	12	9	3	11	8	6	I like the New England Innocence project the most in terms of impact. It always invokes much emotion when I see or hear about a person being wrongly convicted of a crime. This project is the one I'm most passionate about. It also like many of my other top 5 choices, uses SQL for the backend which is the skill I'm looking to develop.	\N	I haven't been part of JumboCode before. 	Celebrimbor	
