	he/him	2028	No	I want to join JumboCode to both find a community of socially minded and diverse coders at Tufts and make a lasting impact on the local community. The idea of a computer science club that can bring together computer science students, gives valuable coding experience, and does so through community service is very exciting to me. Going into college, it seemed to me that most of my time coding outside of classes would be limited to hackathons, LeetCode, and projects that serve only monetary gain or were for a resume. Although I enjoyed coding, I was dreading the seemingly meaningless grind that many computer science students endure in hopes of landing an internship or job. Although JumboCode will impart the same technical skills as these activities, it will be exceedingly meaningful not just as an experience to myself, but to an organization committed to doing good.  By joining this club, I hope to gain a community of civically minded coders, coding skills, and most importantly a meaningful and productive experience working with a non-profit organization.	My experience with volunteering has mainly been with my local food pantry, my local library, and several community engagement programs for children with disabilities. With my local food pantry, with which I have been volunteering since middle school, my responsibilities were to procure, organize, pack, and deliver groceries to those in my own and surrounding towns. With my local library, I taught beginner computer science courses to elementary and middle schoolers, varying in subject matter from scratch to java. Unlike the food pantry, which often stressed confidentiality between volunteers and patrons, this experience allowed me to develop meaningful relationships with my students and get them excited about coding. Lastly I was involved with several organizations dedicated to serving children with disabilities most often assisting in running activities such as sporting events or various outings. As someone with family members with disabilities, this service has been extremely meaningful to me as I’ve seen the type of impact it can have on people with my own eyes in my own home. Additionally, this is the service I am most excited to continue to engage with at Tufts, as I have already joined the Giving Camp Club and done the Giving Camp pre-orientation under FOCUS which focuses on adults with disabilities.	Yes	Yes	Availability:\nMonday: 10:30-2:30, 6-8:30\nTuesday: 12-10\nWednesday: 10:30-2:30, 7:30-8:30\nThursday: 12:00-7:00\nFriday:10:30-2:30, 6:30-10\nSaturday:10-9\nSunday: 10:00-2:00, 4:00-10:00	\N	HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Python	My introduction to computer science was a high school class I took in 10th grade called "Computer Science" where we learned some C.	A project I worked on that I was proud of was a website I created with some members of my high school computer science club that connected high school computer science students with businesses and non-profits for what we called "micro-internships," short coding projects that didn't require more than a week or so to be completed. Before starting this project, I had very little experience with creating websites and had no experience with any of the tools we were going to be using. Despite this, I worked hard to attain the necessary coding skills and even more time to help construct the website. To build the website, we used TypeScript, Angular, MongoDB, GitHub and Node.js. Although all of these tools were used, I most often worked with angular and TypeScript. Beyond the coding aspect, this project provided us with many administrative challenges as we went to local businesses and tried to convince them that working with us would be beneficial both to the students and them. As a team, we were able to connect several students to these companies, however each individual micro-internship was taking us tens of hours of door to door work to find. Despite our struggles in connecting students with these companies, we were able to amass more than 3,000 registered users on our website. Eventually, due to dwindling usership and lack of internships, the domain owner of the website decide to hyperlink it to one of his other similar ventures, which I was not involved in. Despite it not working out the way I wanted in the end, this experience made me learn a lot more about coding and helped me know I wanted to pursue CS in college.	\N	5	1	8	3	7	2	6	4	11	9	10	12	I chose Village Hub Food as my first choice because I have worked with local food pantries before and understand both the good that they do and the lack of resources that they often operate with. The food pantries I have worked with often use notebooks upon notebooks to keep track of clients, donations, scheduling for volunteers, and any other administrative task that needs completing. I would be very excited to work with Village Food Hub for this reason, as I'd have first hand experience with the issue being addressed. I selected my second choice, the New England Innocence Project because of the chance to help make a massive impact on those unfairly imprisoned by the criminal justice system. The criminal justice system and prison reform is very interesting to me, so I believe I would find this project highly meaningful. I selected my third choice, the Somerville Museum, because I enjoy learning about history and I believe I would very much enjoy working with the organization. Although these are the projects I would most like to work with, all of these organizations do tremendous good and I would be equally excited to be working with any of them.	\N	No	Elrohir	
