roud of is one I completed at a hackathon with a partner. Being environmentally oriented individuals, my partner and I decided to make a website that would calculate the carbon footprint of an inputted meal. We drew the data from a nationally sourced carbon footprint fact sheet, using APIs to retrieve the numbers and panda, a python library, to parse the data and form tables. However, at the time (early 2023), the both of us only had experience with Python and Java –– there was the issue of creating a UI and connecting it to our existing backend code. Although I had experience in Django, a framework that utilizes Python to develop a full-stack website, its rigid structure proved inefficient for the allotted time. Thus, drawing upon a newfound motivation, I learned Flask in the few hours and fully coded a basic frontend design and bridged it with the code that my partner refined in the meantime. Although the entirety of the website was quite simple (with the addition of a cool name: Carbon "Food"print), it is one that I am most proud of. That sense of hardened focus is something I will never quite forget. In a similar vein, I hope to involve myself in such learning at JumboCode as well. 	\N	3	12	6	2	4	11	5	9	1	8	10	7	My first choice is English at Large. As I have immigrant parents myself (and a large extended family all over Asia), being able to connect with others of such a demographic is something I especially value. My time traveling internationally has led me to become familiar with navigating various language barriers, as well as understand the small yet celebrated joy achieved from successful communication. (I myself am on a 35 day duolingo streak in efforts to improve my Chinese reading skills, so meeting other people with such language determination and culture integration is truly inspiring). \nIn terms of technical experience, the idea of the project is similar to one I have worked on previously. During a computer science class taken in high school, I worked with a partner to construct a Library system that filed textbooks, facilitated borrowing processes, and also tracked membership attendance. Because this project was specifically object-oriented, we used Java. Nevertheless, I strongly believe that logic in another language can be translated to other languages –– it is for this reason that I am confident I will be able to offer valuable insight to the coding process. \n\nMy second choice is the Somerville Museum simply because of my ardent interest in museums and alternate forms of art. Having the opportunity to involve myself in such a field is something I am heavily interested in. Furthermore, the inventory system sounds like a very creative solution –– filtering through searches and other functions are all aspects I believe I would be able to significantly contribute to. 	\N	No I have not	Finrod Felagund	
	he/him	2025	Yes	I want to join JumboCode because I want to gain experience working more directly in the CS field. I have experience teaching programming professionally, but I still haven't ever had a real internship or gotten a sense of what working on more professional projects is like. On top of this. I also want to gain experience in web dev, and I feel like JumboCode would be great for both of these issues.	I've volunteered at food clinics on multiple occasions, I've always been very engaged with political activism and gone to multiple rallies in D.C., the past 2 summers I worked with a non-profit youth organization teaching programming, and in summer 2020 I helped organize a charity fundraising Smash tournament for BLM.	Yes	Yes	Sunday: 10AM-12PM, 2PM-5PM, 7PM-10PM\nMonday: 10AM-1:30PM, 6PM-10PM\nTuesday: 10:30AM-1:30PM, 3PM-4:30PM, 6PM-8PM\nWednesday: 10AM-1:30PM, 6PM-10PM\nThursday: No\nFriday: Anytime before 6PM\nSaturday: Anytime before 7PM\n	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 105: Programming Languages, CS 121: Software Engineering	C/C++, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python, Rust, Java	I've had an interest in coding since I was 9, I started tinkering with various modding tools and file explorers when I was 13, and I started teaching myself actual programming during my junior year of high school.	When I was 17, I became the first person in the Smash Ultimate modding scene to add a completely new character to the game. At the time, Ultimate modding consisted mostly of replacing files for stuff like minor parameter changes and model swaps, and writing Rust plugins to hook and overwrite functions from the base game, letting us reimplement major mechanics like how the game handles ground collision, how it reads inputs, and individual move scripts. This approach to code editing gave us a lot of power since it effectively let any function be an entry point, and I wanted to test just how far we could push that, so I started adding a character. The main limitation I was working under was the fact that we couldn't actually add new files, only replace existing ones, meaning that in order to load the assets for a new character, I needed to put those assets inside of an existing character's files, load the existing character, and tell the game to use parts of the file which corresponded to whichever character the user actually wanted to play as. Doing this meant I had to put a hook on any function responsible for playing audio, starting an animation, changing the visibility of a mesh (this one in particular was very frustrating since the function in question was unnamed and needed to be accessed by offset), checking the value of a parameter, spawning particle effects, and executing move scripts. Since I was still teaching myself Rust at the time, keeping this code organized was definitely a challenge, and the project as a whole definitely had a lot of spaghetti code in it, but in the end I was successful, and the final result can be seen here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ft-fLCeOBQU	The following repo is for an ongoing game design project I started from scratch 3 years ago using C++ and OpenGL: https://github.com/Ayerbe-Dev/ChampionFightingGame	2	11	4	1	7	12	8	5	6	10	9	3	I'd be happy to work on any of these, but I picked based on the areas the clients are tackling which are the most important to me.	\N	No	Fëanor	
