ice and help those in need while still enhancing my skills as a programmer. Overall, there are a lot of reasons why I would like to be a part of Jumbo Code.	Community service has always been extremely important to me. During COVID, my friend and I started a non profit organization that spread awareness on the use of ethical Artificial Intelligence issues and brought more STEM education to schools in the area. We partnered with Tufts Society of Women Engineers (actually how I learned about Tufts ironically), University of Michigan Society of Women Engineers, and other national organizations to discuss how we can use AI more ethically and bring those considerations into the development of new technologies. In addition, we partnered with local libraries to bring coding workshops to younger audiences. Our community had only offered coding lessons to high schoolers and we felt that middle and even elementary schoolers would be interested in learning to code and starting early. Through these programs we were able to foster interest in computer science in a lot of local kids. Additionally, I recently became a TA for Discrete Math. I was extremely excited about this, because Discrete was one of my favorite classes that I took, and though I found it challenging, it was also really cool to learn about the parallels between math and computer science. I also love math and had always tutored students in math in high school, so to get the opportunity to lead recitations and tutor students at the college level was extremely exciting for me. Finally, I worked as a Russian to English translator for some members of my community. I live in an area with a large population of Soviet immigrants, including my own family, and because of my high level of Russian that my parents passed on to me, I was able to serve as a translator for some elderly families in my area. Overall, community service has always been an integral part of my identity and I am hoping that I will be able to go back to the same amount of work I did for my community in high school, though Jumbo Code .\n	Yes	Yes	I am free on Mondays all day, Wednesdays after 7, and Thursdays after 6. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS61	C/C++, HTML/CSS, SQL, Python	My first introduction was AP Computer Science A in high school.	In High School, I was part of schools Debate Team, and I remember that it was always difficult for the coach to make the team pairings for the tournaments. So, for my final project for AP Computer Science, I chose to write a program that would make the pairings for her for each round. It was very difficult for me to write the code because I had only been coding for a couple of months at this point and I only had a basic understanding of how to write programs, but every day I would write out a small goal for myself and I would do my best to get that part done. Sometimes I succeeded and sometimes I had to come back the next day and try again. I had made a class to represent each team and a class to represent each round. The biggest challenge I faced was figuring out a ranking system, as when teams progress in tournaments they must face other teams who have the same win to loss ration as them. I.e. after round 3 if a team won once and lost once, they can only face teams who had done the same. I had googled different methods I could use and tried to think through the edge case of what if there was an odd amount of teams who had the same win to loss ratio. Finally, I found a way to assign point values to each team and instated a condition that would start at the very top of the leaderboard and pair teams up that way. In the end, I was able to get a basic implementation that my coach tried to use at the next tournament. Although there were some kinks I had to work out, she said it made her life easier, which made me very happy that my hard work paid off.  	\N	4	8	11	12	1	3	9	7	2	5	10	6	Being first generation American and coming from a town where a lot of 