nce, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 170: Computation Theory, CS 23: Game Design	C/C++, Git/GitHub, Python, C#	My first introduction to computer science was the AP Computer Science Principles class in my junior year of high school. It piqued my interest in the field and was a major part of the reason why I chose to pursue a degree in Computer Science.	A project that I'm proud of is a game I worked on with a few other classmates in the game design class here at Tufts called Lock and Key. Lock and Key is a 2D platformer in which there are two types of keys: keys for doors, and keys for abilities. Finding ability keys can aid the player or are necessary to find the keys for the doors. There are also boss fights at the end of each level sequence which adds variety to the level structure and difficulty. I built this project with four classmates using C# for scripting, Unity for assembling and building the game, Audacity for audio, Procreate and Photoshop for the art, and GitHub for storing the code and sharing it with each other. I worked primarily on coding the dungeon levels and UI, but also worked a bit on art, sound effects, and the player health and respawn systems. There were a variety of challenges that we faced along the way to building this game, but each challenge benefitted us in one way or another in the long run. For example, the scripts required to add certain functionality to obstacles and players became too difficult to reference with the tools we learned in class, so I had to learn C# scripting from scratch over the course of three weeks in order to be able to add the desired functionality. This made me feel much more comfortable with using C# and it allowed the game to have more complex features and functionality. In addition, some of the scripts provided to us from the class were not fully compatible with the design of our game. As a result, we had to edit a variety of the essential scripts in our game (such as player health, movement, etc.) or even rewrite these scripts entirely. While this was difficult, it helped tremendously in improving the performance of our game while also helping us further familiarize ourselves with C# and Unity. While these are only some of the problems we faced, these problems helped to improve and broaden our skill sets and come together into a final product that we were all proud of.	https://yvette-m.github.io/LockandKeySite/   (site for project mentioned above)	3	5	2	6	10	8	11	4	7	1	9	12	I admire the level of connection that is held between those who help those with food insecurity and those with food insecurity within Bread & Roses and due to this, I would love to aid in the development of the software they need to make their operations run more smoothly. In addition, I also admire how the Willy Project aims to brighten the days of fellow students around my age who may be having difficulties financially or emotionally. I would love to support this cause by aiding in their operations by developing the software they need.	Over the summer I strengthened my developer skills by completing three workplace simulations through Forage. This included:\nA Software Engineering Simulation with Electronic Arts, where I proposed a new feature for the Sims 4 and wrote a Feature Proposal describing it to other stakeholders, built a class diagram and created multiple classes in C++ for various game objects. I also patched a bugfix and optimized the Sims 4 codebase by implementing an improved data structure.\nI also completed a Cybersecurity Simulation with MasterCard, where I served as an analyst on Mastercard’s Security Awareness Team, and helped identify and report security threats such as phishing, and analyzed and identified which areas of the business needed more robust security training and implemented training courses and procedures for those teams.\nLastly, I completed a Cloud Platform Simulation with Verizon where I built a hypothetical new VPN product for Verizon’s Cloud Computing. This involved using command line Python to test whether Verizon’s VPN met the cloud-native traits, i.e. redundancy, resiliency and least-privilege and researching approaches to achieve application security and communicated insights in a PowerPoint Presentation. If I was accepted into JumboCode, I would be eager to continue to build on these skills, collaborate with others, and help serve the community.	No	Húrin	
