	He/Him	2028	Prefer not to answer	JumboCode is going to be a really important experience for me as I want to get into the Computer Science field and being with JumboCode is perfect as I can engage with the community and build projects for nonprofits. I'm mainly looking for the experience I can gain with JumboCode and how I can make my own projects from the ground up, from planning into action.	I volunteered a lot during my time in high school, helping out at community events, trash cleanups, and many other tutoring events around my area. My most important one was tutoring at an afterschool where I taught K-8 on mathematics and English with occasional HTML and CSS which led to students having interest in Scratch and making their own projects.	Yes	Yes	I can meet on Monday and Wednesday free all day until 6 and then free at 8 again. I'm free on Thursday after 5 as well.	\N	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Python, Java, Dart, Lua	The first ever introduction was my first ever computer science class in high school.	There was one project which I struggled, but I loved doing which was my strategic tic tac toe. The point of this game is just tic tac toe, but you have to get however much you decide to win. For example, you get to decide if you want to connect by 3 or connect by 5 to win and you can also decide how big the board is whether it is 3x3, 4x4, or 5x5. However, the catch is that once you placed more than the amount you need to connect and win, then the one you placed first disappears on the board. For instance, if you had 3 Xs on the board and you are about to put down another, and that is where the catch is as the first X you put down will disappear from the board and if you put another X then second X will disappear and so forth. The game ends when someone get a connection of however much decided by the player. The build of this was built in Java and it required quite a bit of understanding of data structures and algorithms. One of the challenges of this project was to build it so it had to fastest runtime which I think was around O (nlog n) and keep the space complexity as little as possible. I recalled building it with a map and having two digit system; ones place as x value while the tenths place value as y value. I do agree though that this was not the most efficient method, but I did keep the space complexity low and the runtime was still around O(nlog n). Luckily, in the end it worked out and it turned out to be really fun as me and my friend went head on against each other. The thing I figured out with this project was that the game tends to take very long to end when the board gets too big so maybe next time I'll stick to classic tic tac toe.	\N	1	4	2	8	6	9	12	5	10	7	3	11	My top choices are mostly related to educational and resource access. I chose these because I was a first year college student and I know the struggles of trying to find what resources were available around me so I thought it would be perfect for me to help community centers and nonprofit that focus on providing resources or networking of students and mentors. For instance, helping the West Medford Community Center and A2Empowerment are both very intriguing projects for me to work on as I can help give students resources to succeed. To me, bringing support to the helping cause of bridging people is very important to having educational breakthroughs in every field. 	\N	Nope	Turgon	
