t's a real thing) are all parts of Unity that I rarely use but enjoy the challenge of implementing during game jams. Another not as fun challenge was dealing with git conflicts on the few occasions where me and my friends miscommunicated and changed the same file. Having never dealt with manually choosing changes, correcting git conflicts was an absolute pain to learn and perform, but I powered through because I knew we didn't have time to undo local changes.	\N	5	4	6	12	1	8	9	3	7	2	10	11	I'm very interested in the Dillar Academy project! When it comes to their mission, I very firmly believe in the importance of education and it's ability to set people up in life. I think I was very fortunate because I was able to get a very strong education, and I would like to open that opportunity for other less fortunate people. Additionally, I have heard so many times about Uyghurs being mistreated (mistreated being an understatement, but I couldn't think of a better word) around the world, so I think it would be a good cause to help as a way to raise people up. On the programming side, I think there will be ample opportunity for me to get good experience in backend programming given the nature of a class registration system. I also can't deny that there will be quite a bit of catharsis in creating a better version of SIS.\n\nI am also very interest in Bread and Roses! Volunteering at a food pantry shows the issues of food inequality in a way you don't get normally, and I see a lot of value in providing food through a more quality and restaurant-like environment. I think the way Bread and Roses operates is a great way to lift up people struggling with food insecurity. Additionally, groups I've worked with in the past like Wellesley TOPs have had similar issues to the one they want to solve, where communication was not very good and volunteers would be away for a week without people knowing. I would love to help solve these problems for Bread and Roses. When it comes to programming, I think it will be very fun and interesting to create a scheduling website, and handling registration through emails is something I have no experience with, so I think it will be a fun challenge all around!	\N	Yes! I worked on Dress for Success last year! It was a lot of fun and I learned a lot, but I really want to round out my knowledge this year and get more experience in certain areas (namely backend).	Voronwë	
	she/her	2028	Yes	I want to join JumboCode to get experience in working in a team on a coding project. I realize that there is a whole new side to collaborating on code and making components fit together, so I think it would be a great learning opportunity for me. I hope to gain new skills in coding as the languages mentioned in the GIM slides are ones I have never used before. I also want to join Jumbocode so that I work on a project that will solve a problem in a community or for an organization. Lastly, I really want to meet other people who are interested in coding and CS!	Back in high school, I was the leader of the changemakers group, where we coordinated students around the world with social activists so that they could interview them one-on-one and foster mentorships so that students could start their own service groups in their own school communities. Additionally, about a year ago I went on a school service trip to Nepal for 10 days where we organized fun activities for kids at our sister school.	Yes	Yes	Mondays: I’m free between 10:15AM to 5PM, also free after 7:30PM except for 9-10PM. Tuesdays: I’m free 3PM to 7PM, and after 8:30PM. Wednesdays: free 12PM to 5PM, and after 9PM. Thursdays: free 3PM to 5:30PM. Fridays: free in the morning and after 3PM. Saturdays/Sundays I am generally free anytime.	\N	Java	Took AP CSA in junior year of high school and really liked it.	Back in junior year, my group and I competed in a competition to solve a problem in our local community. We decided to create a food tracker app that would warn the user X days before a food item would expire. At first, we were determined to make our solution as an iOS app on Xcode, but due to time constraints, we made it on Thunkable. First, we planned our UI design on Figma, and then coded it on Thunkable. One challenge we faced was time management, as although we theoretically had about 3 months, we wasted the first month with unfocused weekly team meetings. When coding the app, another challenge we faced was storing the user’s data externally in a Google Sheet, and creating a calculated property of days left before each food item expired. However, in the end we successfully managed to finish developing the app.	\N	7	1	4	6	3	10	8	11	2	5	9	12	My top choice is Village Food Hub because I think that having access to healthy food while promoting environmental sustainability is really important. A few months ago I watched 'You Are What You Eat' on Netflix, which opened my eyes to the impact on which the food we choose to eat has on the majority aspects of our lives.\n\nAccess to education is another cause that I really care about. For generations of families who are in a cycle of poverty, education can help them break out of it. In addition, education can make a difference in creating a more stable society where citizens are more informed about issues. I really want to help make a difference in this, so that is why my second and third choices are English at Large and Dillar Academy.	\N	No	Beren	
	He/Him	2027	No	JumboCode's community focus really caught my attention during the GIM slides. As someone who's passionate about CS, I'm always excited to connect with others who share that interest. The club's approach to web development is particularly appealing - it's not just about coding, but about making a real impact by helping non-profits while also giving developers hands-on experience with industry tools.\n\nI've seen how competitive the CS industry can get, so I appreciate JumboCode's efforts to create a more collaborative environment. It's refreshing to see a club that values the people behind the code as much as the projects themselves. From what I've heard, this community aspect is what sets JumboCode apart, and it's exactly what I'm looking forward to being a part of.\n\nThe combination of technical growth, meaningful projects, and a supportive community makes JumboCode stand out. I'm eager to contribute my skills and learn from others in this unique environment.	Over the years I've volunteered at a science museum, a homeless shelter, and the Smart Border Coalition, which is a coalition of business people and politicians in San Diego all aiming to improve efficiency at the US/Mex border.\n\nMy most significant volunteer experience was with the Smart Border Coalition, where I dedicated over 350 hours to a project aimed at improving efficiency at the San Diego/Tijuana border. The core of my work involved developing two key components: a comprehensive database and a real-time dashboard.\n\nInitially, I discovered there was no historical record of border wait times. To address this, I created a database from scratch and set up a cron job that automatically scraped data from Google Maps and the government website every 30 minutes. This automated system consistently updated our database, providing a robust foundation for analysis.\n\nBuilding on this, I developed a real-time dashboard to analyze and visualize the border wait times. This tool allowed stakeholders to easily interpret trends and patterns, facilitating more informed decision-making to improve border efficiency.\n\nThe impact of this project has extended beyond my initial involvement. Since its implementation, other developers have utilized the database for various analytical projects, which I find extremely awesome!!	Yes	Yes	I'm generally available in the evenings - I'll absolutely be able to fit in a one-hour weekly team meeting. 	CS 160: Algorithms, I transferred from Northeastern, the reason CS 160 but no pre-reqs.	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Swift, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	My first introduction to CS was when I was 12 - I built a website for my photography business because I couldn't afford the Wix fees	Choosing a standout project from the 30+ I've built isn't easy, but SolarUp, which I developed at HackHarvard, holds a special place in my heart. \n\nThe idea was to create software that helps business owners determine the cost-effectiveness of installing solar panels on parking lot rooftop canopies. We had to factor in the costs of canopy construction, solar panel installation, and potential revenue based on surface area and local solar irradiance.\n\nWe faced our share of challenges - I even had to implement Selenium on the cloud for one of our data sources, which was quite the task. But in just 24 hours, our team managed to integrate all the necessary APIs, algorithms, and UI to create a functional web app. Our efforts paid off when we won second place in the Earth & Space track.\n\nWhat makes me particularly proud is how well our team worked together to bring this complex idea to life. I'm also thrilled about the concept itself. After the hackathon, I reached out to the lead researcher in this field, who had proven that solar irradiance in the continental US could make any parking lot rooftop canopy profitable. Their support and feedback were incredibly encouraging.	https://sethmorton.xyz/	2	3	9	4	8	10	7	1	5	6	11	1