ajor in it.	I’ve worked on various projects through my CS11, CS15, and CS40 classes, but I’d like to highlight something different. In my game design class last year, I led a team to create Alien Traveler, a game where a trapped alien uses time travel—moving between the past and future—to find tools and escape a lab. The game’s mechanics are unique, as actions in one timeline affect the others. For instance, the alien could plant a seed in the past, jump to the future to pick the apple, and use it to solve a puzzle. While the game’s mechanics were fascinating, my main responsibility was the visual design. Using Procreate and Photoshop, I designed and animated characters in different timelines, created game environments, developed the game’s logo, and crafted interactive in-game objects. It was an exciting project.\n	my website is down but you can find "my_art_portfolio" in my Linkedin: https://www.linkedin.com/in/allen-wang-459125220/	1	9	7	10	8	5	11	2	4	3	12	6	West Medford Community Center: I chose this project because it focuses on enhancing the newsletter system for the West Medford community. I’m particularly drawn to it because it’s close to where I live and is a community I feel connected to. I always wanted to use what I’ve learned in the classroom to give back to the community I belong to. \n\nLCS Tutoring: \nI chose this because I believe it has the potential to significantly impact the lives of Tufts students, something I’ve personally experienced. I’d love to contribute to improving the tutoring experience, helping make it even more beneficial for my fellow students.\n\nBread & Roses:\nI chose this because it’s another project that connects with the local community.\n\nEnglish at Large:\nI chose this because of the team’s energy and vibes.\n	I'm looking forward to joining JumboCode this year and contribute to the team.	I haven't been in JumboCode before, but I've applied for multiple times.	Orophin	
