	He/Him/His	2027	No	For me, JumboCode is an exciting opportunity to create something meaningful with the skills and tools that I have been cultivating at Tufts. As a CS major who is also studying Science, Technology, and Society, I am fascinated by many of the projects that JumboCode supports, because they are a testament to how computing technologies can be paired with purpose-driven work to create real social impact, especially in our local communities. \n\nMy work with JumboCode's Theatre@First team last year was a fantastic experience. I had the chance to work with a strong team of people who share my interests, advance my development skills, and deliver a meaningful web app to a local non-profit (Side note, I even found out that one of my team members is practically my neighbor in NY). However, I felt that I had been thrown into the deep end, as I had joined JumboCode with minimal coding experience. While I learned a lot from my team, I still had lots of questions by the end of the year, so I put in some time this summer to improve my skills with HTML, CSS, and other tools. I hope that this year, I will have the chance to put these fresh abilities to use and develop them (along with other new skills) even further!	In high school, I was a member of the Tri-M Music Honor Society, in which I volunteered to bring musical events to life in my community through organization and education.​​ I was also involved with volunteering, fundraising, and meeting with various groups such as the National Honor Society, Black Student Union, and Students for Gender Equality. In my Junior year of high school, I was selected by school administration to serve as a student representative on Superintendent Focus Groups. Discussions covered various topics including the quality of student life, racial inequalities, accessibility, and staffing.\n\nI also spent three years volunteering with Fareground, a local nonprofit in NY focused on food justice for low-income communities especially hard-hit by the pandemic. With Fareground, I packed and distributed over 700 meal packages to families in need, a valuable experience that exposed me to hardship and unfamiliar perspectives.\n\nIn the spring I worked as a TA for CS-11, and I am excited to do so again this semester! Being a TA, especially for 11, has been both a rewarding and informative experience, as I have had the opportunity to introduce students to the fascinating capabilities of computing for the first time. Meanwhile, I get to put my code analysis and debugging abilities to the test in office hours, labs, and grading meetings.\n	Yes	Yes	I have late afternoon and evening availability every day of the week, not counting 1-hour TA responsibilities on Tuesday and Thursday evenings. I am also not very busy during the weekends. I am flexible, so overall will be happy to fit a 1-hour team meeting into my schedule.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Git/GitHub, Python	In my Junior and Senior years of high school, I took introductory classes in computer science, including AP CS Principles. However, CS-11 was my first real exposure to a challenging coding project.	In the summer of 2020, I fulfilled a long-time dream by spending my savings on a camera drone. I spent weeks capturing footage of the Adirondack Mountains and Lake Champlain. Using Adobe video editing software, I began splicing my footage together with music, matching video cuts with changes in the rhythm or key of the song. The process was very time consuming, as I had to teach myself how to use these highly complex recording and editing technologies. I nearly dropped my drone in the lake on a number of occasions, and looking back, I would probably have made some different editing choices. But being stuck in quarantine I had time to teach myself these things. In the end, I developed a short film that tells an audio-visual story while conveying the natural beauty of the region as well as the range of emotions that I associated with different parts of my visit. This video remains one of the artistic creations I am most proud of, although since its inception I have created other works involving drone videography. I have linked the video in the next section of the form.	https://youtu.be/L1IiSl7-jL8?si=GpKTOPgKTDgkAK7I	1	6	7	2	12	3	11	5	9	4	10	8	The West Medford Community Center is an exciting project option for me because I was involved in an effort in my hometown to allocate local government money for a community center where people could play music, host events, and provide community support. I would love to be a part of this project to support all of the important activities and work that WMCC does for our host community. I am also intrigued by the group's use of AWS in the tech stack, which is a technology I would like to learn more about.\n\nThe Somerville Museum Project is exciting to me because it resembles the work I did last year with Theater@First where we built an inventory system for another local somerville organization. I would love to apply this experience to a new and exciting project with a fun team. I also think that historical preservation is an awesome use of our skills as developers (and want to be in Night at the Museum 4).\n\nThe New England Innocence project interests me because computing technologies today are often used for greater surveillance and incarceration, like predictive policing. Also, living near a maximum security prison, I have seen the effects of mass incarceration on prison-adjacent communities. So it is exciting to see an opportunity to use these same computing technologies to combat wrongful convictions.\n\nMy volunteer work with Fareground to support local food access aligns with the missions of both Bread & Roses and Village Food Hub, and so I have put these nonprofits high on my list as well.\n\nOverall, I am impressed by the missions of all JumboCode projects this year, but these are the groups I would be most excited to work with.	So hyped for another year of JumboCode, thanks!	Yes! I worked on the Theatre@First project last year.	Elladan	
	she/her	2028	Yes	I want to get more industry experience in software engineering and also see if that type of career would be the right fit for me. Though I have prior experience with coding for projects in high school, I want to learn more about how these skills are used in a real world environment and gain experience with the modern frameworks that are being used in the industry. I also like the focus this club has for giving back to the community, since I believe that technology's main role should be to help the lives of others.	I contributed to a project where I led a team to design a alarm system for a care home, specifically for our client: a blind man who relies on a more unreliable system to let his caretakers know that he needs assistance with a task. This was the first time I used my technical experience to create something for a client, and I was the main contributor to the software portion of the device.\n\nOutside of that, I also have experience with tutoring and have spent 80+ hours volunteering at Community Servings, a non-profit that sends out tailor-made meals for people in the area with chronic or critical illnesses. I have also assisted with my high school's chapter of Society of Women Engineers NEXT, helping young women become more engaged with STEM through experience with robotics and CAD.	Yes	Yes	My current availability is a bit nebulous right now due to this being my first semester at Tufts. I'm still trying to see what clubs I want to participate in, so those times aren't set in stone yet. That being said, here are some times that I 100% *cannot* do this semester:\n\nSunday: all free\nMonday: 10:30 AM - 2:45 PM, 6 PM - 7:15 PM\nTuesday: 12 PM - 2:45 PM, 6 - 9 PM\nWednesday: 10:30 AM - 11:45 AM, 1:30 PM - 2:45 PM, 6 PM - 7:15 PM\nThursday: 12 PM - 1:15 PM\nFriday: 1:30 PM - 2:45 PM\nSaturday: 1 PM - 3 PM (probably)	Duel enrolled at WPI during high school senior year. Took Accelerated Intro to Program Design (CS1102), Accelerated Object-Oriented Programming (CS2022), and Algorithms (CS2223)	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python	In middle school, my first exposure was using Scratch in a club I was in. I got a Python book a few months after.	During my senior year in high school, I took many CS courses at Worcester Polytechnic Institute (WPI). In one of my courses, CS 1102, we had to use Racket, a language that was very different than I was used to. It was based on LISP, which meant that all iterative processes were done using recursion and everything was based on lists. One of the hardest projects that I had to do in this class was a Tic-Tac-Toe game. In it, my partner and I had to implement a computer for the player to play against that utilized a minimax algorithm. Because of a combination of lack of experience with the algorithm, working with a new language, and 8 layers of recursive insanity, this ended up being the hardest project of that class.\n\nImplementing player interaction was simple enough, but once we got to the minimax part of the project, we were at a roadblock. Every time we ran our code, it always seemed like we were one step off. The computer would suddenly fill the board with Os. We fixed that. Oops, the computer now played a whole entire game by itself. Ahh, silly computer, you have to only go one step in the tree, not all of them at once! \n\nAfter we were finally able to resolve what felt like countless bugs in our system and did our very last tests, we looked over our code again. What ultimately helped us finish this project was splitting everything into smaller chunks, making and constantly revising plans, constant testing, and sheer determination. It was absolutely not perfect, but with a combination of teamwork, 10 diagrams scrawled on lined paper, a function that was really 12 or so nested lists, and a 4 hour session in the library, we had done it. That feeling of satisfaction and pride we felt in our work was immense. \n\nExperiences like this are what have drawn me to engineering and computer science. The feeling I get when I've solved a puzzle like this one is always what keeping drawing me back. And now, I am excited to use those skills to help out this community!	https://annewu109.github.io/index.html	5	6	7	3	10	11	12	1	2	4	9	8	I mainly chose the projects that I would have a design that interested me the most and would give me more industry experience. For example, LCS Tutoring automates a matching process between a tutor and a tutee based on their preferences, which I felt would be the most interesting project algorithmically because a lot of factors go into matchmaking and trying to balance all of them seems like an interesting challenge. For English at Large, what appeals to me is being able to connect with a professional software engineer within the organization.\n\nOther projects, contribute to causes that I have personally engaged with in some way. For English at Large, my parents are immigrants that had to learn English largely on their own, so I really appreciate the cause this project is contributing to. For Bread & Roses, I have volunteered at another organization that helps against food insecurity that does have a web app to organize volunteer times.\n\nThat being said, all of the projects contribute to great causes and I would be fine with being part of any of them, but I lean towards my first 4 choices a bit more than the rest.	Good luck to the people who have to read like 100 or so applications and have to parse through who goes into what group, I salute you	First time in JumboCode.	Amroth	
