 through a maze without any user intervention/input. I thought it was fascinating. I had of course used tech to play video games and for school, but I had never consider a career in building those programs that I had used. After I took my first CS class in high school, which taught that very same OOP program and also taught Java, I realized that the language I was using was the same one to code Minecraft. After that, I was hooked.	I am really proud of the work that I did this summer at a non-profit school for children with autism, The New England Center for Children. I was a software developer for the ACE Software System, a software package that the school sold to other schools to accommodate students with autism. On the project, I worked with AI/ML models to build a hybrid search feature (semantic search, keyword search w/ stemming, cross encoder). I also fine-tuned and prompt engineered AI models to build a conversational agent for their website, focusing on tailoring the model to the ACE's needs. I used Retrieval-Augmented Generation to provide the AI model with relevant information before it generated a response, storing relevant documents (chunked into 150 word chunks) in a PostgreSQL database.	https://github.com/shaynesidman	5	3	12	1	2	4	9	8	7	6	11	10	I think that The Somerville Museum project is the most attractive to me because of its message and the passion that I saw from the PM and TL. I thought that Holden and Zack had a lively presentation that was light-hearted but did not diminish the purpose and mission of their project. Plus who doesn't love Night at the Museum (sequel is better)?\n\nI think that Dillar Academy has a great message. It would be really cool to work on something that we, as Tufts students, do not even have because SIS is so great... On a serious note, Dillar Academy, to me, provided one of the most unique yet applicable services of any of the projects. A class registration portal is both really cool and really useful for anyone using it.	\N	Yes, I was a part of the Emerald Necklace Conservancy last year (2023-2024) (miss you Roger and Ben)	Aegnor	
	He/Him	2027	Yes	As a sophomore in computer science I have to stand out in future applications for internships and jobs. However, doing JumboCode is also about how I can further apply myself and not stay so stagnant. I have always been a student who learns by getting hands on and applying what I know to problems. Coding is no different, and I want to find myself using the time I have free these next two semesters working towards a goal I can back confidently. The other aspect is being a part of a team, and working throughout this time simulating an environment in work, but most importantly gaining connections and lifelong peers/mentors. As someone currently taking CS40 I have been introduced to partner coding,  making the transition to a full scale team not hard at all and feeling even more rewarding when everything comes together at the end. But in general, coding in a classroom does not give the same feeling of making code that impacts people in such a positive way. I feel more connected in that regard, applying what I know to helping others in need.	Most of my previous works involve high school activities such as tutoring for math courses of all grades as well as volunteering for several clubs then. I helped students with their homework and finals prepping. I was also a part of Future Business Leaders of America and National Honor Society, volunteering for several activities such as marathons or at the local football stadiums. I do not have too much experience with community engagement on this level, but am very excited for the projects presented and especially with the depth of help I would be giving the community. 	Yes	Yes	Monday: 12 pm - 1 pm, 8 pm - 10 pm\nTuesday: 4 pm - 12 pm\nWednesday: 4 pm - 6 pm, 8 pm - 10 pm\nThursday: 4 pm - 6 pm, 8 - 12 pm\nFriday: 4 pm - 12 pm\nSaturday: 4 pm - 12 pm\nSunday: 12 pm - 12 pm\n\n\n\n\n\n	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript	I did a research project in high school for my internship. I used Rstudio there, so statistical coding. I made graphs and charts based on the data I was given.	The above project is still considered my pride and joy. I worked in a lab doing bioinformatics, namely predictive health genomics. I did gene research using gene association studies to mark if there were benefits to using a newer version. I learned R programming from a graduate mentor who helped me learnt the ropes, but mainly encouraged me to be more self-actualizing and learn on my own. Even with several hiccups in the implementation, I found the joys of coding for the first time and learning what it means to think out each step. I was able to make plenty of plots using Rstudio as well as an entire Rshiny web-app that helped display all of my work. The only thing I was mad about was that it did not win science fair. Predictive health genomics helps those that don't know what lifelong diseases they could develop in their lives and makes sure that they get the help they need as soon as possible.	https://sites.google.com/view/jeremiahlongino/about?authuser=0	8	6	11	12	5	7	9	2	1	4	3	10	I chose the first 8 mainly based off vibes of the presenters but also the projects. (I sort of wish there was like a tier list for these lol) \n\nMy top projects are English at Large and Dillar Academy because I feel strongly in regards to helping people learn English. I do not come from an immigrant background, however I saw the issues of emigrating here on other friends. This especially comes up for a lot of kids when it comes to applying to college, because they will most likely be the only ones with any knowledge of the academic systems as well as their parents not being able to communicate or understand anything going on. \n\nWomen's education is very important, especially in countries where women's rights have been and continue to be such an impact on their society. A2Empowerment is second because of this, as my sister was my main backing force throughout school and a big reason keeping me through college. Even if I am not a woman, I have been inspired by amazing educated women to be the person I am today.\n\nThe last few of my top 8 mainly involve food insecurity and tutoring. As someone from a marginalized community, my surroundings were always less glamourous than those from someone across the same city as me. A prevalent issue in Black communities is the immense amount of fast food restaurants, gas stations, and liquor stores next to our houses and families. These food deserts make acquiring fresh and healthy food expensive in the residents' eyes. In reality, a Trader Joe's  may be within someone's budget, just located in a very inconvenient place for them. Mind you, in Atlanta getting from the South to Downtown with all of its fancy places takes at least 30 minutes. And that is when traffic is good.\n\nThe Innocence Project I just feel overall strongly about due to being Black. The justice system is not infallible, and it is clear that there are a number of falsely accused individuals unjustly spending time in our prison system. This coupled with how said system allows for little rehabilitation options for actual offenders, makes the experience of spending jail for upwards of decades and lifetimes, more than just a wrong sentencing: it is choice between freedom or concrete walls.\n\nMy bottom options doesn't mean that I think they are boring, but that the presenters maybe did not fully explain the reasoning behind picking up the projects. These were also some of the first presentations, so they probably had unfortunate timing and maybe weren't ready.	I am really excited for this possibility! I hope my application impresses you all!	\N	Ecthelion of the Fountain	
	she/her	2027	Yes	I love being part of JumboCode because it’s an amazing group of students who share a passion for computer science, but who are also incredibly diverse in skills and interests. I want to continue being involved as it provides an opportunity to further my skill in web development and overall CS knowledge while also making meaningful contributions to the community. I hope that through JumboCode I am able to grow academically and professionally in addition to making connections with fellow students.	In high school, I was very involved in Key Club and STEM Club, doing community service projects such as organizing tree planting at a local park and running a science education event for younger students. I was also a member of the student government, where I helped manage fundraisers and organize school-wide events. Last year, I was also involved in JumboCode, working on the Theatre @ First project with Liam and Amitav, and this year I am working as a CS15 TA!	Yes	Yes	Tues: 2-4, 6-7\nWed: 1-4, 8-10\nThurs: 4-10\nFri: 6-8\nSat: 12-3\nSun: 12-8	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, Python, Java	APCSA junior year of high school (other than block coding in elementary school lol)	The final project for my APCSA class junior year was to code a card game. I decided to do poker, constructing a “casino” where users could play Five Card Stud, Five Card Draw, and Texas Hold’Em. Though I have since made more robust programs, this project particularly stands out to me because of how proud I felt to complete it. At the time, this was the largest piece of code I had ever written, spanning over a thousand lines across six files. Cards were represented by a Card class, dealing and scoring were handled in the Game class, each variant of poker had its own class, and the user interface was constructed in the Play class. Looking back on it now, I am shocked by how complex it was, considering I had been coding for less than a year at that point. It was very difficult though and I spent many hours working on the project. But I genuinely loved making it, I would prioritize improving it over my other responsibilities because it was so satisfying getting it to work. In the end there were still bugs, such as ties occurring in certain cases, so I’ve considered returning to it and making an improved version in C++. Nonetheless, I still remain extremely proud of that project and my success in that course in general.	\N	1	8	10	5	3	6	12	9	7	4	11	2	WMCC - You guys seem like such a fun group! Having more project leads is great as you can give a variety of perspectives and have different expertise. I think that by being part of this group, I will learn a lot about web development which is my biggest goal being a part of JumboCode.\n\nUniversity Counsel - Even though I am declared CS, I am very interested in data science. I also have experience in python, doing a machine learning internship this past summer and I am currently in CS30. This project seems really interesting and I would love to work on an important application of python!\n\nDillar Academy - As someone who has spent many (frustrating) hours on SIS, I am super intrigued by the idea of making a better system. I also think that what Dillar Academy is doing is extremely important for our community. You guys also seem so nice and I’d love to get boba with you!\n\nBread and Roses - Bread and Roses is such an amazing organization that is so beneficial to its community. This team seems like a lot of fun to be a part of! (Even the late night LeetCoding lol)\n\nSomerville Museum - Last year I worked on an inventory JumboCode project and really enjoyed it! You guys are also so funny and I’d love to work with you!\n\nNE Innocence Project - As someone interested in working with data, I find this project very interesting. The NEIP is doing really important work and I would love to help support them. I’m also extremely scared of CS40 and am so impressed that you guys TA it.	\N	Th