	she/her	2027	Yes	I’d love to join JumboCode because it’s a chance to use my coding skills in a way that genuinely helps people. I’ve always believed that tech has the power to create real change, and working with nonprofits to build apps feels like a way to make that happen. JumboCode’s focus on community-driven projects is something I really admire - working together to make a difference.\n\nI’m also excited about the chance to work with a team of like-minded people. Collaborating with designers, project managers, and developers on something meaningful sounds like a great way to grow both as a coder and a teammate. Plus, I’ve mostly worked on individual projects, so the idea of tackling a big, real-world app as part of a team is something I’m really looking forward to. \n\nI hope to walk away from this experience not only with stronger technical skills but also with a deeper understanding of how technology can support community efforts. It would be amazing to see something I helped build have a lasting impact on an organization, and I think that’s what excites me most about joining JumboCode.\n	My journey in community engagement has deepened my commitment to social good, and one of the most fulfilling projects I've been involved with is Mission She Power. Here, I trained underprivileged girls to play soccer. We grew from training just a few girls to now hosting weekly sessions for over ten participants, all free of charge. Each week, the girls eagerly gather at Azad Maidan and Oval Ground, excited to learn from experienced players and coaches. Our mission is to normalize the presence of girls in sports and break down the stigmas surrounding them.\n\nIn addition to my work with Mission She Power, I co-founded Empowering Youth21, which focuses on skill development and communication training. Partnering with the Birla Foundation's vocational training school, we provide ICT skill training for young adults and have implemented programs for women through the Rajasthani Female Organization to help them gain entry-level career skills.\n\nDuring the Covid-19 pandemic, I organized a fundraising campaign that raised 1.5 Lakh INR through Milaap. This initiative allowed us to vaccinate over 1,300 people in Mumbai's slums, partnering with Apollo Spectrum and the Ramesh Tulsiani Charitable Trust. Additionally, I led The Good Food Drive, where we prepared and delivered 10,000 meals to orphanages and street children during the lockdown.\n\nThese experiences have reinforced my belief in the importance of community-driven initiatives. By leveraging my organizational and leadership skills, I hope to continue contributing to meaningful projects like Mission She Power that empower and uplift underprivileged communities, especially young women in sports.\n	Yes	Yes	I would be able to adjust/move things around to make it to my team's weekly meeting	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++	I came into Tufts as an Applied Math/Econ major. However, I decided to take CS11 just for fun. I ended up enjoying it so much that I transferred to the SoE to major in Data Science.	This summer, I built a personal finance chatbot. My first year in college, I struggled to keep track of my finances, so I decided to built this so that I can use it to keep track of my expenses. 	\N	8	1	3	11	6	7	12	5	10	2	4	9	\nI find the Village Food Hub project really inspiring because it tackles some critical issues in our community. Coming from India, where this is an extremely common situation, I would love to be a part of this. And maybe apply what I learn here, back home.  Strengthening the food system and making healthy, nutritious food accessible to everyone is essential, especially in an era where food insecurity is a pressing concern. \nBeing involved in this initiative would not only allow me to contribute my skills but also be part of a meaningful change in how our community accesses food resources. \n	-	No	Ar-Pharazôn	
	He/Him	2028	No	I want to join JumboCode because I’m heavily interested in a career in software engineering while also wanting to help out my newfound community. I'm eager to dive into full-stack development and learn the process of creating a product from scratch, all the way to deploying it in the real world. While most of my past projects have been in Python, I’m excited to expand my skills by learning how to use React and TypeScript for building user interfaces, leveraging GitHub to manage code changes and handle pull requests, and working with Prisma and PostgreSQL to manage complex data types and queries. I also feel like JumboCode would allow me to apply the concepts I am learning in CS15 and deepen my understanding of how different data structures can be used to solve specific problems more efficiently. However, more than anything, I’m excited to meet all the new people in JumboCode who share a passion for using tech to help support the community. Being on a team sounds super fun to me, similar to the dynamics of a company-like environment, where everybody must contribute towards creating a cohesive product. I’m eager to find a place on campus with other aspiring techies, whether it’s through participating in hackathons, grinding through LeetCode challenges, or helping each other out with our CS classes. I can’t wait to join the JumboCode community!!	I’ve had a lot of experience with community engagement since a young age, always trying to give back to those less fortunate than me. From middle school, I used to volunteer at food banks, packing dry and fresh foods in boxes and hanging them out to the underprivileged in my community. In high school, I wanted to make a more significant impact in my community through something I was passionate about: cycling. During my freshman year, I embarked on a 500-mile bike ride from San Francisco to Los Angeles, raising $3,000 to address food disparities in my community. Furthermore, every year, my dad and I set a goal to participate in a century fundraiser ride, where we would cycle 100 miles to support various causes such as leukemia, multiple sclerosis, or fundraising for local schools. To continue contributing, I founded a cycling club at my school, where we volunteered monthly at a local bike repair shop.  We refurbished affordable, out-of-tune bikes and distributed them to underprivileged members of the community. Lastly, my friend and I started the club AI and Allgorithm at our high school, aiming to teach and inspire others about the fundamental principles of machine learning.	Yes	Yes	Free anytime after 5pm from Monday to Wednesday. Free anytime after 7 pm on Thursday. Free anytime after 12 pm on Friday. Free all day weekends(Saturday, Sunday). 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, Git/GitHub, Python	My first introduction to computer science was through a program called InspiritAI, where I had no coding experience, but learned theoretical concepts about Machine Learning and learned to apply those concepts through simple Python notebooks. I then took APCSA during my junior year of high school where I was able to learn Java, my first statically typed and compiled language. During that same year, I enhanced my machine learning and Python skills by building my own AI-generative jazz LSTM model by following the music21 documentation, MIT’s toolkit for computational musicology. That summer, I also attended a research program at UCSB where I worked on explainable AI(XAI), specifically leveraging XAI to help better diagnose malaria detection in malaria cell images. Lastly, during the summer of 2024, I took Tufts CS11 to just gain exposure to C++ before coming in this school year and taking CS15.	The project I am most proud of is my creation of an AI model that can learn how to improvise jazz music over a set of chord changes. This was my introduction to the world of generative music, which is an emerging field involving music created through algorithmic composition. In research of this topic, I discovered music21, a tool created for computer-aided musicology. The extensive documentation for music21 became my guide, but I soon realized the holes in my programming knowledge. Every line of code became a quest for answers as I sought to create a Markov model: a simple model that used probabilities to determine the next musical note in a generated composition. However, a major challenge was finding a dataset that my model could learn to train off of. With no musical jazz dataset online, I had to create my own dataset by transposing different jazz albums and converting them into a MIDI(a music file readable by programs). After doing this, I was able to build a simple Markov Model by following tutorials online that learned to improvise music based on a chain of probabilities. After successfully finishing the project, I explored more current models, such as neural networks, capable of crafting nuanced jazz improvisations and learned to construct my first deep learning model. After seeking help from a mentor who specialized in AI music generation, we were able to create a working LSTM model that learned to improvise jazz using Keras. As my curiosity grew in this topic, I created my own jazz metrics using theoretical concepts from my musical background to help better quantify how well our models were actually learning to create jazz. Based on my music theory knowledge of jazz, I was able to create two novel metrics which I wrote a paper on and eventually presented my work and got it published at an IEEE multimedia conference. \n	https://github.com/ConradHsu https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/10473480   https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=uG-EZR4AAAAJ&hl=en	11	7	6	8	10	2	12	5	4	1	9	3	While most of the projects essentially use the same techstack to tackle similar problems, Bread and Roses is my top choice because I’m deeply passionate about its mission: addressing food insecurity. ​​I’m particularly excited about the Bread & Roses project because it not only addresses a real-world problem but also provides an opportunity for me to learn and work with a modern tech stack. The current manual process for consolidating volunteer data and scheduling through email can be streamlined, and I’m eager to contribute to developing a scheduling web app that simplifies this process. I’m especially interested in learning how to build a website using technologies like React, Next.js, TypeScript, Prisma, MongoDB, and Tailwind CSS, as well as deploying the app on Vercel. This project aligns perfectly with my goal of gaining hands-on experience in full-stack development while addressing an important issue I’m passionate about. Lastly, eager to say, this is the only group that mentioned late night leet coding which honestly sounds awesome.\n\nMy second choice is the New England Innocence Project, as I’ve always been interested in the intersection of technology and the justice system. While there are ongoing debates about the use of AI in this field, I’m excited about the opportunity to take a step back and focus on building a database-driven web app that improves information accessibility, ultimately contributing to more equitable legislation. I’m really excited to expand my skill set by learning new languages and diving into full-stack development. I thrive on the challenge of problem-solving and am always eager to learn and explore new concepts. The community aspect of this club is especially appealing to me, as both the PM and designer are from the same area where I grew up (near San Francisco). Additionally, the activities, like trampoline night, and the fact that the project leads are 40 TAs (class I’m taking next semester) makes it even more exciting!\n\nMy third choice is the office of university counsel project, because it’s the only project that uses Python and it ties into healthcare. With the possibility of going to dental school lingering in the back of my mind, being able to create a project to help with transparency in endorsing Tufts Dental/Medical/Vet school with sponsorships from pharmaceutical companies. Again, the project uses Python which I’m comfortable in, and I’m also super interested in learning about FastApi, something also unique to this group. \n\nThe rest of my rankings are all totally equal, as I’m just super stoked about learning more about real-world web development and also equally important, client work. I am confident that I will be a valuable member in any team, as I learn things quickly and love to problem-solve and figure out new challenging things. Lastly, I’ve always grown up with the principle of helping those less fortunate, so joining a club like JumboCode that allows me to give back to the community while pursuing my interests would mean a lot to me.\n	I'm honestly fine being in any project as I just really want to join the JumboCode community. 	\N	Gwindor	
	he/him	2027	Yes	I want to join JumboCode because I would like to apply my interest in computer science and coding in an environment that is akin to a workplace. The bulk of my computer science experience has either come in a classroom or from personal projects. The most amount of coding I have done for work has been writing programs to make my spreadsheets go by fasters. So joining JumboCode, I will get to apply my coding experience in an environment that will prepare me for when I have a coding job in the workplace.	Starting this semester, I am a member of Tufts' STEM Ambassadors where I will teach high school students about STEM subjects and get them to explore their interests in STEM. Last year, I did FOCUS where I was in placemaking, and I participated in and discussed volunteer activities where we worked to make public spaces more accommodating to people. In high school, I was a volunteer at a branch of my local library where I helped develop different activities for after school and take home programs.	Yes	Yes	Thursday after 6pm, Friday after 4:30pm, Saturday after 5pm	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, JavaScript/TypeScript, SQL, Python	A computer class I took in 5th grade	I am proud of coding the backend for a robot website interface I developed for the final project of my EN-1 Simple Robotics course. I had to learn a lot about Google Firebase and connecting it to HTML and JS. The whole process took a lot of googling as doing this was very much out of the scope of the project, but our robot needed a lot of data, so we realized it would be easier to come up with an alternative backend than what was provided.\n\nTo do this, I had to take input from a user on an HTML page, send that data through Google Firebase, collect that data onto my computer to put the data in a format usable for the robot, and then through a serial connection, send that data to the robot.\n\nThe main challenge was learning a database system, I've never seen before in a quick amount of time, but I figured it out with a lot of looking at documentation and googling.	\N	4	7	2	12	10	1	11	5	9	8	6	3	To speak on my top choice, I have always wanted to work with the Innocence Project. One of my biggest fears is that someone close to me or even myself, will be falsely accused of a crime and be trapped in prison. What makes this issue so much worse is that there are so many cases of people serving massive sentences when it is publicly known that the evidence that sent them to prison is incredibly faulty (ranging from false accusations, police misconduct, or DNA testing exonerating people), but people can't be released from jail because there is tragically not the resources to do so. There are too many people across the country in this situation, and there are not enough defense attorneys to help these people get released. \n\nI am currently double majoring in computer science and human factors engineering, and a CS-based human-targeted volunteering project, no matter which project I end up working on I could experience the real life effects of the intersection I am interested in. Working on the Innocence Project would be one of the strongest ways I could make an impact using both of the majors I'm studying and would personally be very impactful to me.	\N	No	Huan	
	she/her	2028	Yes	I am interested in joining JumboCode because I believe it would provide an incredible opportunity to strengthen my technical skills while contributing meaningfully to the surrounding Medford/Tufts/Boston communities. As a freshman intent on majoring in Computer Science, I am eager to learn how to develop usable software interfaces, program using new languages, and get exposure to the full software development process. \n\nI started learning how to program during my junior year of high school. Since then, I have learned the basics in many programming languages such as Java, Python, C++, and HTML/CSS. I completed the SUPA (Syracuse University Project Advance) Cyber Security Course and the Girls Who Code Self-Paced Program. I am also currently in the process of taking Introduction to Computer Science (CS-0011) and the Emerging Scholars in Computer Science (CS-0014), where I am continuously learning C++ and exploring topics in computer science. I am very excited to explore new programming platforms such as Clerk, PostgreSQL, Vercel, etc. for backend, frontend, and general development. So while I may not be a professional programmer yet, I consider myself a very adaptable and driven person who is willing to invest the necessary time and energy to accomplish complex tasks.\n\nLastly, I have a strong desire to give back while, in-turn, strengthening my technical skills. I hope to note only become a more well-rounded programmer through JumboCode, but also to support local nonprofits, as they are essential pillars of every community. 	Throughout high school, I dedicated much of my free time to volunteering in various community service initiatives, and I am committed to continuing that work in my new community here at Tufts. One of my most meaningful leadership roles was serving as the community service chair for the New York State Council on Leadership and Student Activities (NYSCLSA) for two years. In this role, I helped organize conference events in partnership with nonprofits like Special Olympics and Syracuse University’s InclusiveU program. Together, we raised awareness about the importance of inclusivity, reaching over 600 conference attendees every year. Over those two years, we collectively raised $15,000 to sponsor Special Olympics athletes. \n\nIn total, I have volunteered over 150 hours, working with diverse community members such as elementary school students, senior citizens, children in hospitals, soup kitchens, and a couple non-profit organizations. I was also an active member in my town’s Interact Club and the treasurer for my school’s Key Club chapter. In these organizations, I collaborated with Rotary and Kiwanis members to plan/execute projects such as food drives, blood drives, clothing drives, and other community-focused events. These experiences have strengthened my commitment to service and taught me how essential it is to give back in meaningful ways.\n\nMost recently, as a part of the FOCUS pre-orientation program at Tufts, I gained insight into some of the needs of the Somerville/Medford communities through active service. I had the opportunity to work with programs like SCALE (Somerville Center for Adult Learning Experiences) and the BCGC (Boston Community Growing Center) to maintain community spaces, benefiting the people who rely on them. During the program, I engaged in more traditional, short-term service by aiding SCALE and BCGC with placemaking duties. However, I also learned about the importance of long-term, impactful service through a community-centered organization called MAMAS (Mutual Aid Medford and Somerville). Their work inspired me to seek more meaningful opportunities for service, which I hope to pursue by helping design user-friendly interfaces for local non-profits with JumboCode. 	Yes	Yes	Monday- 1:30-5:00 pm\nTuesday- 2:00-4:00pm, anytime after 7:30pm\nWednesday- any time after 7:30pm\nThursday- anytime after 2:00pm\nFriday- 12-7pm\nSaturday- any time\nSunday- any time	\N