	he/him	2027	No	Firstly, I would love to join JumboCode to advance my programming skills. I first got into programming in middle school and was extremely passionate about it. I was particularly interested in coding games, as playing Pokémon was what got me into the world of programming to begin with. However, I began to lose interest in my third year of high school, spending more time playing sports than coding. However, taking a programming class last year reignited my passion and I hope I can continue to grow my skills at JumboCode. \n\nAnother reason I want to join JumboCode is to be a part of a meaningful and impactful project. I've always used to code for my own interests, like programming games. However, I now want to combine my love for programming and put it into something larger, something that can be helpful to others. \n\nFinally, I want to join JumboCode to meet more people! I would love to join a community with people who share my interests in programming and making a difference.	In high school, I was an Admissions Prefect and Admissions Tour Guide, helping calm new students down and familiarizing them with campus. I was also captain of my school's Squash team. At my old university (I'm a transfer student), I was a leader in freshmen engineering and I had experience hosting events for fellow engineers. My favorite volunteer experience was in my senior year of high school. I went on a two-week journey to the edges of Costa Rica to help a nonprofit group preserve the population of leatherback sea turtles.	Yes	Yes	I am free every weekday after 7pm, but not too late. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, Git/GitHub, Python, Java	My first introduction to computer science was in sixth grade. My favorite videogame was Pokémon at the time and I became extremely curious about how it was made so I began self-teaching myself programming.	One of my favorite projects I worked on was in my second year of high school. For my AP Computer Science class's final project, I decided to create a Chess AI. To do this, I first had to program chess. Surprisingly, the game itself wasn't as complicated. I built the game by utilizing a 2x2 matrix, with the locations of each piece stored in the matrix. For each piece, I created a child class to define their unique movements. The hardest part of the project was the Chess AI. Since I had no experience with machine learning at the time, I didn't know how to train a model. Instead, my approach was to use recursion to loop through all possible moves for 3 layers of potential moves. The AI would then grade each move and then make the move with the highest grade. One of the hardest challenges I had with this was determining an algorithm for grading each move. I decided to implement a system where each piece had its own value. Pawn would have a value of 1, bishop a value of 3, etc, with the king having the highest value. Since the AI only looked 3 layers into the future, I prioritized the moves that could take the highest value piece from the opposing team while losing the least value. Although there are still some things I could improve on, it is still one of the projects I am most proud of as it is the first large-scale project that I coded.	I unfortunately lost my old GitHub account that I used in highschool	2	5	11	4	8	6	10	12	3	1	9	7	All the projects seemed extremely interesting and impactful so I just ranked them based on first-impressions of how much the solutions resonated with me and how fun they seem.	\N	\N	Gothmog	
