xcept instead of colors to represent right and wrong guesses, we used characters like "x" and "*". A big challenge was getting the formatting/spacing correct so that it was user friendly. Another challenge was having the characters go in the correct spaces, so for example having the "*" go in the space where a letter was correctly guessed. It was helpful to have a partner as we could bounce ideas off each other and eventually complete the project without too much difficulty.	\N	12	2	9	7	11	4	8	1	10	3	5	6	I am most interested in the LCS tutoring project because in high school I founded my schools chapter of Math NHS and part of Math NHS was creating the math peer tutoring program. Our tutoring program actually used the same system described in the LCS tutoring project. All the members of Math NHS filled out the tutor form, and high schoolers who needed tutoring filled out the tutee form. Pairs were matched in a spreadsheet, but the system because difficult to manage with semester class changes, changing sports seasons, etc. Because I understand the struggles of using Google Forms, I'd be interested in helping implement a more efficient pairing system. I'm also interested in the Village Hub Food and Bread and Roses projects because I have experience volunteering for a food pantry and would love to continue positively impacting the community by reducing food insecurity. Beyond that every project seems interesting and I would love to work on any one of them.	\N	No I have not.	Morgoth	
	She/her	2028	Yes	By joining JumboCode, I hope to learn more about the stages of software development, from planning to turning in the final product. Through this experience, I would get the chance to work firsthand with a team of developers to create a real solution to a real problem. It is also a chance to engage with a different community and to learn about their needs and perspective. It is also a unique opportunity to work in a project beyond the classroom; one where I can apply my skills to make a positive impact on another community.	During three years in high school, I volunteered in TuTutor, a program that connects tutors with kids from underfunded schools in Colombia, my home country. The aim of the program was to help these young children strengthen their math and English skills, as many of them had their education halted by the pandemic. I would have one-on-one online tutoring sessions twice a week, for which I created lesson plans based on games and interactive activities. It was an incredibly enriching experience, where I had the privilege of meeting the sweetest kids and hearing their stories and perspectives.\n\nDuring 4 years of high school, I participated in TOM (Tikkun Olam Makers), a program where innovators, engineers, designers, and problem solvers come together to enhance the lives of people with disabilities (Need-Knowers) by designing and building affordable assistive technology. During my first two years, I worked as part of the "Wellbeing Team", where my job was to accompany Need-Knowers and their families during the main event, which lasts three days. During this time, I had the chance to learn the stories of people who had very different lives from mine. It was incredibly inspiring to see how families are able to adapt and overcome challenges. It was also beautiful to see their joy after receiving the final product, which would help them participate more fully in society. The last two years, I participated as a Maker, meaning I was part of one of the teams that builds the assistive technology. I was first part of "Team Marlon", where I mostly helped with the graphic design aspect of a mobile app that reminded Marlon to complete his daily tasks in his job. The next year, I was part of a different brach of TOM, called TomAgro, that helps farmers optimize their processes. As part of "Team Yeison", I worked alongside other programmers to develop a WhatsApp chatbot.\n\nI also volunteered through Cancer Kids First and designed cards for children in hospitals. And, last year, I volunteered through "Fundación PAN" and helped organize a Christmas party for over 40 kids from a low-income neighborhood.	Yes	Yes	Monday (8:00am-1:15pm, 4:15pm-11pm), Tuesdays (4:30-7:00, 8:40pm-11pm), Wednesdays (12 pm - 2 pm, 7:30pm-11pm), Thursdays(4:30pm-11pm), Fridays and Saturdays (any time)	\N	C/C++, JavaScript/TypeScript, SQL, Python	In 9th grade, I started Harvard's CS50: Introduction to Computer Science.	Last year, I was part of "TomAgro", a branch of TOM (Tikkun Olam Makers) where our aim was to help farmers with small productions make their operations more productive, so they could better compete in the market. I was part of "Team Yeison", where we worked to develop a WhatsApp chatbot to help Yeison in processing orders from his clients, helping him optimize his current system. In this project, I was directly involved in the programming, which centered around using AWS and WhatsApp Business Management API to develop a chatbot that could take orders and store them in a database, making it easier for Yeison to process orders and organize his schedule for delivery. Costumer would be presented with a catalog, and they would be able to place orders directly within WhatsApp without the need of intervention from Yeison's end. The orders, along with the delivery location, would then be stored in a Google Sheets file, where Yeison could easily see the orders he needed to pack. This way, he could better plan his trips into the city and optimize his process. Additionally, the costumer had the option to get a weekly reminder to place an order.\n\nTomAgro consisted of a main event lasting three days during which the projects are to be built. Leading up to the event, my team and I met on several occasions with an expert in WhatsApp chatbots from the company Bonnet Analytics. We then learned the bases of AWS and WhatsApp Business Management API, as well as practiced coding with Python. We also learned to use Postman, as we would use it to send requests and test our bot. Learning to use new platforms was definitely of the biggest challenges I faced. The project took a while to begin, and I had very little time to thoroughly learn all the tools we would be using. When the main event started, I felt unprepared, and every task seemed a bit overwhelming. However, within the first hours I got much more familiar with how the tools worked together and the team soon fell into a rhythm. We got to learn about using message templates from the WhatsApp API, outputting data to a database, using AWS to run our code, etc. It was trial and error, and those three days were long, but by the end of the third day we had a product we were proud of. Fortunately, Yeison loved it.	\N	4	9	5	8	6	11	10	1	2	3	7	12	My first choice is LCS tutoring as I have had the experience of being a tutor and it is incredibly enriching for both the tutor and the tutee. Getting paired should be made easy, so that the tutoring sessions can start as soon as possible. I would also love to be part of English at Large, as the project would help immigrants get accustomed to life in the US. My parents were in that position when they first came to study in the US, as they didn't know the language very well, and it was challenging to adapt to a new life. For me, too, English is my second language and I often have trouble communicating my thoughts. Through this project, I would have a part in making a positive impact on many adults who could have easily been my parents. As for Bread and Roses, I believe also volunteering should be made easy. By facilitating volunteer sign-ups, Bread and Roses could increase their impact on the Merrimack Valley community. As for the other projects, I would be happy in any of them. They all give me a chance to learn about software development, as well as apply skills to benefit a community.	\N	No	Olwë	
