ularly proud of is the Bayesian Model for Sports Prediction that I worked on during the ACE++ program at Tufts. The project aimed to predict the Boston Celtics' probability of winning based on whether they played at home or away, using Bayesian analysis. We collected real-world data from the Celtics’ 2023-2024 season, including their win-loss record and home vs. away performance, and developed a model that adjusted prior beliefs based on new data. One of the biggest challenges we faced was incorporating multiple priors, such as coach experience and player performance, into the Bayesian equation. We tested different statistical distributions to fine-tune the model, and this process enhanced my understanding of Bayesian analysis and gave me valuable hands-on experience with data modeling and Python coding. It was an exciting project that allowed me to combine my passion for sports with my technical skills.	\N	9	1	6	5	12	3	2	8	10	11	7	4	I am most interested in projects that focus on community engagement, education, and social impact, as these align with my background and passion for using technology to solve real-world problems. My top choice is the Village Food Hub project, which addresses food sustainability and access, something I am particularly passionate about, given my experience with civic leadership initiatives. I am also drawn to the New England Innocence Project, as it tackles social justice issues, and the A2Empowerment project, where my experience in education and empowerment initiatives in Kenya would be valuable. Additionally, I ranked LGBTQ Senior Housing and The Wily Network highly because they provide essential support to underserved communities, and I am excited about the potential to contribute to such meaningful causes. While my lower preferences, like Somerville Museum and Tufts University Counsel, are still interesting, they are less aligned with my focus on civic engagement and education. However, I am eager to contribute to any project where I 