	He/him/his	2027	No	It is clear to me that being a part of JumboCode would encourage me to grow so much socially, emotionally, and professionally to the point that in my eyes, the offerings of JumboCode are unmatched campus-wide. It is everything I’ve been looking for in a CS club. To be able to work on a highly impactful project with a group of people who are eager to program and form friendships is an incredibly valuable opportunity for me. The welcoming and enthusiastic atmosphere I felt during the GIM really excites me as well, as the PMs all seemed enthusiastic to get to know their devs on a personal level and share their experiences.	- Volunteer trumpet player: performed for holiday events (Memorial Day services, Rotary club), charity walks, and local parades; organized music-related events for the community; 100+ hours\n\n- Local Jewish Community Center senior counselor (JCCNS); worked with special needs youth members; organized inclusive activities; ~280 hours\n\n- SAT and regional spelling bee tutor: mentored/trained students for school and regional level spelling bees; tutored students on SAT math section; 50+ hours\n\n- High school underclassmen mentor: consulted underclassmen for essay writing and geometry/calculus skills; ~20 hours\n\n- Homeless shelter volunteer at Lifebridge, Salem MA: served and prepared meals; ~50 hours\n\n- Food pantry volunteer at My Brother’s Table, Lynn MA, and local church; directed food drive; frequent fundraiser volunteer (car washes, door-to-door); distributed donations; prepared meals; ~70 hours\n\n- Community thrift shop volunteer at Magic Hat: collected, sorted, and distributed donations, ~60 hours\n	Yes	Yes	I am available:\n\nMonday 11am-2:30pm, 8-10pm\nTuesday 4-8pm\nWednesday 11am-2:30pm, 8-10pm\nThursday 2-3pm, 8-10pm\nFriday 10am-12pm, 7-10pm	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, JavaScript/TypeScript, Swift, Git/GitHub, SQL, Python, C#, Java	As a high school sophomore, my friend said he wanted to learn Python so he could program macros for a video game he played. That sounded fun, so I joined him.	A small group of friends and I participated in the GMTK 2024 Game Jam over the summer. It was a 96-hour competition to make a video game based on the theme "built to scale." The game we created was about a kid and a robot who solve puzzles and float around using a balloon that can puff up to get comedically huge. We used Unity, which uses C#, as our engine, and to collaborate we used GitHub Desktop and Git Bash. The game jam was tough, but ultimately very fun and memorable. I definitely came out as a stronger collaborator and programmer than how I came in. We also placed just outside of the top quartile out of 7,615 submissions.\n\nThis was my first time programming on a medium-sized team (5 people). The experience was super educational; I learned a lot about how to communicate, cooperate, and plan with a group of developers. I also worked alongside an artist and a musician who had little/no programming experience. Working with individuals with diverse skill sets in a fast-paced setting also helped me develop better communication skills, learn from other’s expertise, and enhance my ability to collaborate efficiently in a creative environment. Together we acknowledged each others' strengths and weaknesses, learned how to allocate our tasks rationally, and combined our skills to create a final product.\n\nIt was challenging to learn how to use GitHub Desktop 'on the job,' but it pushed me to adapt to a new and highly useful method of digital collaboration. Making a full-fledged game in a short timespan alongside people with unique abilities led me to learn other new things quickly (for example, various new Unity features and new algorithms for terrain generation) and evaluate important questions under pressure (should I implement this feature, how should I do it, how do my time constraints affect me, etc.).\n\nNow, after my experience working with developers in a group setting, I am excited for my next opportunity to do it again.	Game jam game link: https://jam-jar-games.itch.io/maurice-and-maximus	2	10	12	1	8	11	7	5	9	3	6	4	I love Sun Tzu and also recall his quote saying to join the Somerville Museum JumboCode project in his book The Art of War, which is why I had to place it first.\n\nIn all seriousness, I especially like the idea of impacting local communities because it is fulfilling to see the impact of our work firsthand. That being said, working on a project with far-reaching effects is also very exciting and fulfilling, and I would be happy working in either type of situation.	I am big into music production. If we need a group theme song, I have us covered.	I have not been a part of JumboCode before.	Lothíriel	
