	she/her	2026	Yes	I want to join JumboCode to support the Somerville and Medford community in a way that directly impacts it and gives way for sustainable long term changes. I grew up in Oregon and spending the last 2 years here in Medford has been really amazing, I'm so thankful for the area and all that it has given to me. I want to be able to reciprocate all that it has given me and I can think of no better way than through jumbo code. I recently decided to pursue the CS minor here, and I am eager to further develop my programming skills by also contributing to my surrounding community. 	I spent a few years in high school volunteering for Bags of Love, a local nonprofit which makes bags of clothes, toys, toiletries etc. for anyone in my town that was in need. I also volunteered for Food for Lane County which made and distributed meals. I also spent time as a tutor for elementary school age students during covid. In addition to these I helped my friend create a system of food pantries that were installed in and around my home community in Oregon. We worked with local non profits to build, install and stock wooden food pantries. We were chosen as representatives for the Columbia region to attend a national YMCA youth government conference. There we were recognized as National Finalists and received some support to build a website for the pantry system. This website was to create a map of all the pantries to make them more easily accessible and to create schedules for volunteers to stalk the pantries to ensure they always had food. I have not been involved much with community engagement here at Tufts but I am eager to get back into it. 	Yes	Studying remotely	That timing works for me!	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, Git/GitHub, Python, Praat Scripting 	My first introduction to computer science was CS11. It was a super challenging intro for me because I had no prior experience in coding before this. It was definitely one of the hardest classes I have taken at Tufts but it was also the most fulfilling. I developed so much as both a student and a person, and I'm so excited to continue on my CS journey.	One project that I am really proud of was a script that I and a lab PI created over the summer at a Linguistics lab I was working at. The project was to extract data to be statistically analyzed from speech audio samples through the program Praat. The goal of the project was to rework the language production model. The study was set up in a way that participants were given different phrases to read out loud in order to illicit speech errors. The data I was working in was in the form of audio clips that were transcribed and segmented through using features in Praat. Individual words were isolated as well as syllables and the frequency of each sound. There were over 60 participants and for each one we were looking at certain syllables that were targeted. Each participant individually had about 300 targeted syllables in their speech samples and 7 types of data needed to be extracted for each syllable that was targeted. We worked in Praat's scripting program to create the script and this proved to be very challenging. The language was very counterintuitive and was missing a lot of key features that python and C++ have which made the coding process very challenging. The language syntax was also incredibly bizarre, certain variables were in double quotes and some were in single quotes but they never established which one was meant for what in their scripting tutorials online. Safe to say it consisted of a lot of trial and error. To start we created a table that contained the participants speaker ID and the 7 types of data we were looking for from each individual syllable. From there we created a list to hold all of the text grids (a name for the files that have the audio samples in them). From here we iterated over the text grids one by one, retrieved the speaker ID and then determined which syllable we were looking at. This part proved to be the most challenging because of the way the experiment was set up. The specific syllables or sounds we were interested in were S and SH. The sentences participants were supposed to repeat were a word that started with neither S or SH, then a word starting with S then a word starting with the sound SH. For example peach seek chic. Originally our idea was to create a counter, so every time we encountered a syllable that was neither S or SH we started it at one. Then the next syllable was S and that was count 2, and then SH was three and then once it hit there it restarted and looked for the next syllable that was not S or SH. This worked at the beginning but then we came to realize that sometimes the participants were not completing the statements fully or repeating words when they shouldn't have. This made the count off and the statements were all jumbled when we tried to extract them and the target syllables were not maintained. So if someone had said peach seek seek chic this would be counted as a sentence twice, once with each seek. Ideally this sentence would not be counted tho as we did not want to include any sentences that had errors. We debated going through and fixing all of the data processing to throw out any instances where the participants messed up an utterance but that would've taken an incredibly long amount of time. We went through a lot of trial and error with this case but ultimately we created a for loop to iterate through the tier (similar to a list) of syllables. If a SH was found then we called a function that was able to extract the length of the syllable, the Center of gravity of it, and much more. From there we started counting back wards and went one interval back from the SH. Then we had a while loop that kept moving back one syllable until it found an S or it had gone back 4 syllables (if the participant had said the sentence correctly that's the max amount it would have been back from the SH syllable.) Then we called the same function as with the SH to extract the necessary data from it. We also had other checks to make sure the SH wasn't at the beginning of the list cause otherwise you couldn't start counting back from it, and to check that the pausing between the words were not too long (then the speech error would not be vaild). After this we had to extract the sentence that matched with each syllable we extracted data for. (if we were looking at the data from the S syllable you would want to know which sentence it came from so you would extract "peach seek chic" with it). For this we did something similar as looking for the syllables. We checked that the first syllable was not S or SH, if it was then we appended it to a table and then had a series of counters for repetitions to get the sequence extracted correctly. (I attached the link to the script below and it has comments explaining the process more indepth). Finally we appended all of the data into the same sheet. The whole process was pretty challenging, working in a new and very outdated programming language created a lot of issues. There were many times where we were wishing we were using python. One big challenge we had was the time and space the program took up. Praat requires you to create new objects every time you try and extract a piece of sound data (which we had to do for finding the center of gravity) so by the end of the program we had over 12,000 objects that were created in the Praat object list (user interface) so we had to add in code to delete these. If we would have known about it's memory and space issues we should have designed a more efficient program but we were on a deadline so we could not restart, and in the end the program did run and work perfectly.  The whole experience was super rewarding I really enjoyed having a real way to put my coding skills to use, it was my first coding project outside of CS11 or CS15. I also really loved getting to work with my lab PI the collaboration and brainstorming that coding projects require is so much fun. I really love working with other people on these types of assignments. 	https://github.com/stellaroering/Eshespraatscript/blob/main/Praatscript	11	1	8	7	2	4	12	3	6	10	9	5	My first choice is the Village hub food. As I talked about earlier in my application I worked on a very similar project in my home town with trying to organize food pantry volunteers and stocking. I unfortunately never got to see the website through after the YMCA National Youth government conference because I had just graduated and come to Tufts. I would love the opportunity to continue the same work that was so important to me in my home town and help another community with combatting food insecurity. I'm also eager to help develop the web-app to track the food pantries inventory, essentially the same work I would have done for my pantries back at home but never got to complete. \n\nMy second choice is the Dillar academy. I feel very passionate about education access and ensuring everyone has an equal right to their right to learning. I would be so interested in creating a web-app portal that would facilitate the browsing and enrollment of classes. Having had to deal with SIS it's clear how important a well designed and user friendly education portal is to making class registration easy. Having an easy access to classes is such an important step in helping Dillars mission in bridging the gap between Uyghurs educational disparities. \n\nMy third choice is LCS tutoring because I was a tutor for elementary school students in high school and the experience was very transformative for me. It is so important to have peers teaching peers to create collaboration, inclusivity and a drive towards shared knowledge. The LCS program at Tufts is so amazing and I would love to be able to contribute to the work it's doing here. After my tutoring experience it gave me a new perspective as a student, that learning is all about growing together and increasing collective knowledge about the world. 	\N	No I have not	Finwë	
