aw in 2023. I managed to find an AI tool called Mediapipe Pose to analyze 3D human movements using different joints. \nNew to machine learning, I worked with a mentor who introduced me to “K-Means Clustering,” an algorithm for image analysis. The recommended photos, however, did not match my stick figures at all. \nDisheartened, I tried to debug by comparing joint angles within images, but nothing worked. Inspired by trigonometry during a calculus class, I decided to develop my algorithm, which I later published in GitHub as “difference between angles.” By incorporating the trigonometry value of the angles and fine-tuning my model, I managed to achieve an accuracy rate of  87 percent for the matching results.\nTo provide artists worldwide easy access to my tool, I taught myself HTML and JavaScript to publish my tool online using a web user interface. \nA few months later, I was really happy to hear from people like me who wanted to learn drawing that they thought my tool was helpful.	https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oXvr1qQS3j0&t=3s	8	4	3	5	1	2	12	11	6	9	7	10	Dillar Academy is my first choice because Uyghurs are an important group in China. I like their cultures and dances, and I enjoyed traveling to Xinjiang before, Uyghurs are enthusiastic. I'm surprised Jumbocode has a program to help people there to learn English and connect with the world. \nThe New England Innocence Project is my Second choice because I was in a Forensic Science in high school, one project we did was to research false exoneration, this project made me learn about a lot of cases that were grievance, sorrow, but also joy when those people were released. Therefore, I want to join this program to help prevent wrongful convictions.	\N	\N	Ingwë	
