“one of my devs last year, ALWAYS IS PRESENT, ACTIVE, AND LIVENS THE ENVIRONMENT. i love her and have gotten to know her outside of being a dev (#my favorite)” -Sristi Panchu	she/her	2026	Yes	Last year, I had the privilege of being part of JumboCode, working on The Lantern. The experience was incredibly fulfilling, from working the first ticket to completing the final one as a team. It not only deepened my appreciation for web development but also strengthened the connections I built with my peers. As a CS major, collaboration and learning from others are vital, and JumboCode allowed me to not only grow as a computer scientist but make friendships that will last way beyond my college years.\n\nInitially, I joined JumboCode to do more to volunteer work and to gain exposure to the “CS world.” I quickly realized that this club was the perfect intersection of both. After a year, the experience exceeded all my expectations, and I would be honored to continue being part of this incredible club.	My experience with social good activism started in high school. I went to a small all girls school that focused on making well rounded students. Because of that I joined pretty much every club I could and I got to learn about and help a lot of different groups of people. I was a part of my school's Environmental Board starting in freshman year and became Vice President senior year. We worked on keeping our school green and educating the community on environmental issues. \n\nAnother group I was the Co-President of my school's Asian Student Alliance where we did many presentations on different topics like hypersexualization and fetishization of Asian women in media or harmful stereotypes and their effects on Asian Americans. I also was a two year Co-President of my school’s Empowering Women in STEM club. This club partnered with our elementary and middle school by doing workshops with the goal of inspiring young girls to pursue and love STEM. Outside of high school I helped lead a group of 30+ members to raise money/awareness for Leukemia & Lymphoma Society. This program was called “Student of the Year” and we had seven weeks to raise as much money as we could. The group ended up raising over $26K. I also volunteered at soup kitchens and community events throughout high school. \n\nNow in college, I am a freshmen RA in Miller Hall and I love it! Last year I also was in Miller as an RA, and I really enjoyed connecting with my residents, being the go to person for all their questions and concerns, and being a role model for them. I am still friends with over half of my residents now!\n\nAll of these experiences were and are very rewarding to me and I want to continue to do similar things in other communities I am interested in.	Yes	Studying remotely	Monday: 10pm-4pm, 6pm-9pm\nTuesday: 4pm-6pm, 8pm-9pm\nWednesday: NA\nThursday: 8pm-10pm\nFriday: 12pm-2pm, 4pm-6pm\nSaturday: NA\nSunday: any time until 10pm	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 121: Software Engineering	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, Python	My first introduction to computer science was in middle school during an Hour of Code. Since then I have done everything my high school has offered related to CS. I took three computer science classes in high school and became the main coder of our school’s robotics team for all four years. Now I’m a junior in CS170, CS160, and Database systems, and I’m excited to learn more!	My high school did a competition every year called the Hallway Competition. Based on the grade’s class color (ours was green) you decked out a hallway in that color with a theme. Each grade went all out to decorate their hallways to reflect their unique personality and spirit. In freshman year we really did not know what we were doing and ended up coming in last place because we had no leadership or a solid plan. \n\nThe next year I was elected Co-Lead of my class' planning committee. Unfortunately, my classmates were deeply divided on the idea of a jungle theme, and I knew we wouldn't be successful without wide agreement. Convincing everyone to trust a singular vision was difficult and tiring but absolutely required. It allowed us to pull off the most realistic fake jungle I have ever seen. We won by a landslide, but more importantly the whole grade worked together towards a common goal, and learned to truly collaborate.\n\nIn junior year, mid-way through the competition, I was unexpectedly sent home due to a COVID-19 exposure. While I was disappointed that I could not physically be with them, they demonstrated that great things can happen when everyone works together. It became an opportunity to unite and for new leadership to emerge. Students called me for design input and virtually showed me progress. Witnessing my peers work together to make an amazing hallway was exactly what I wanted. I was proud of our grade's enthusiasm and amazing teamwork. This collective work resulted in another victory!\n\nBy the time senior year rolled around practically everyone in the grade wanted to help and we ended up with an amazingly colorful hallway (complete with a 10 foot high foam cave). Even though the contest might have been trivial and just a fun way to get into school spirit, it was truly an amazing experience. It helped me become a better leader and learn how to collaborate while also having fun and doing what I love.	\N	7	2	8	11	9	4	10	3	1	5	6	12	English at Large: This project really hit home for me as someone with a Korean immigrant father and immigrant grandparents. Growing up in a bilingual household, there was always and is somewhat of a language barrier between me and some member of my family. I would love to work on a team that would have an impact on my family and to help others. I also thought that the project itself was interesting and as someone who loves reading and being organized this project sounds right up my alley!\n\nVillage Food Hub: In high school I always had a passion for food pantries and outreach programs similar to Village Food Hub. I recently joined a club on campus called HUG (Helping unhoused groups) with a similar mission to raise awareness and also help with food insecurity. I would love to be in a group that is focused on helping a good cause that closely relates to my morals.	\N	Yes! I was on The Lantern	Angrod	
	she/her	2026	Yes	I’m interested in joining JumboCode because this club would provide me the unique opportunity to gain valuable experience relevant to my future career, help my local community, and connect with my peers. I love the logic puzzles, creativity, and learning integral to the coding process and I enjoy using code to address real-world challenges. I’m majoring in Data Science and minoring in Computer Science and Math. I have taken various computer science classes at Tufts such as CS 20, CS 15, CS 11, and CS 40 and I’m currently taking CS 121 and CS 135. Projects in CS 40 taught me how to confidently tackle intimidating problems and debug programs handling large amounts of information, while CS 20 taught me many new languages, how to use multiple languages to complete a task, and how to design a visually appealing and user-friendly website. These classes gave me technical skills and I would love the chance to apply these skills to real world situations. Over the summer, I completed research at the MIT Koch Institute and applied machine learning to aiding the discovery of an effective tuberculosis vaccine. This experience taught me how exciting and informative real-life applications of computer science are. With JumboCode, not only would I learn how to create a product for a client from scratch, but I would also learn from the strategies, advice, and knowledge of my fellow group members. Through my work in several community service clubs throughout high school, I enjoyed connecting with and helping others. JumboCode is the perfect opportunity for me to apply my passion for computer science in supporting the Somerville-Medford community while connecting with my computer science classmates. I always look forward to partner and group work in my technical classes. Collaboration and communication make tasks more fun and allow everyone to enhance their learning, creativity and productivity. Aside from connecting with my peers through our project, I look forward to bonding with my team through the fun team building activities described during the presentation!	Throughout high school, I was a chief coordinator and teacher for Kickstart STEM, an LLC that teaches elementary school students in my hometown how to code in Python and Scratch while exposing the students to a variety of engineering topics. This role required teamwork, organization, and patience. I worked with fellow leaders to create curricula, determine schedules for classes and camps, train teachers, advertise the program, and communicate with parents and teachers. I taught these classes and developed various teaching strategies. I learned how to make the class fun, connect the material to students’ interests, and keep students engaged for extended periods of time by making the assignments manageable. Helping young students, especially young girls, gain confidence in computer science was especially rewarding. My role for this LLC extended beyond running meetings, planning, and teaching classes. I made myself available for teachers whenever they needed advice, needed someone to fill in at the last minute, and fielded parent questions. In high school I also served as Communications Manager for Key Club. In this role, I oversaw and participated in community service events, managed all social media, and photographed events. I also participated in a mentorship program. As a mentor I visited an elementary school weekly to connect with students, forge friendships, offer support, and engage students in art and sport activities.	Yes	Yes	On Monday and Wednesday I am available after 4:15. On Tuesday and Thursday I’m available after 1:15. On Friday I’m available after 12. I’m available through the weekend other than Sunday from 5-6.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python	In elementary school I participated in a program called Girls Who Code. In elementary and middle school I was a member of a First Lego League team in which I helped to code and operate a robot. During high school I took two computer science classes (a Python class at the University of Vermont and AP CS A).	I worked with a team for the final project in my Web Programming class last fall. Our goal was to create  an interactive and transactional website using HTML, CSS, Javascript, jQuery, an external database, server side technologies, and an API. Our team decided to create TravelWise, a website that helps the user plan trips on a budget. TravelWise includes a currency rate calculator, travel packages, budgeting hacks, and a Smart Search feature that displays countries the user can travel to based on their budget and interests. My main roles were to create the database, write the PHP code that connects our website to the database, and complete the Smart Search feature. The database made the code more efficient, less repetitive, and easier to update. Late in our project development process we hit a roadblock. Our database was corrupt and required extensive debugging. Our team worked closely together on project elements such as overall debugging and making the website mobile friendly. My team’s excellent group dynamics were strengthened by the fact that our skills were complementary and we prioritized communication. For each of our major tasks we discussed how we would meet the requirements, what role each person would play, and our new timeline for completion. We used Figma to create a detailed plan for the website’s design, which was essential in connecting the work of the coders to the work of the designers. I really enjoyed working with this team because we were all equally passionate about the project, did our parts, helped teammates with their tasks when they struggled, and kept each other on track with schedules and to-do lists. This project taught me about the importance of a diverse and well-rounded team with a  communicative and supportive culture in which team members can speak up, set goals, ask for help, and step up to lend assistance when needed.	\N	3	8	1	4	7	6	10	2	11	9	5	12	The Willy Network is one of my favorite projects because I’m passionate about helping Boston students who lack emotional and financial support from their families. This project has a great cause and would teach me a lot of new frameworks. I’m excited by the LCS Tutoring project because making a web-app that collects tutor-tutee preferences and facilitates the matching process seems like a very valuable skill. It would also be cool to work for an organization at Tufts! I’m also interested in contributing to the organization of newsletters for the West Medford Community Center. This project would give me experience with new databases, libraries, and languages. All these groups have bonding activities I’d love to participate in and people that I would love to work with!	\N	\N	Beorn's Bees	
