and Stroke Foundation. In terms of hands-on experience, I’ve volunteered at shelters, helped create care packages on Christmas to hand out at community centres, and run toy drives for homeless children. I was the service lead at my school and represented the service council in conversations with the school administration. This responsibility also included planning and executing all school service events. The best experience I’ve had, though, was on a two-week trip to Project Somos, a school for Indigenous students in rural Guatemala. They recognize that service trips are often performative and create harmful dependency, and adjust volunteer’s duties as such. They prevent us from getting too close to the students while simultaneously enlisting us for some of the hard labour that comes with running a school. About half my time was spent teaching science, math, and English, and the other half was spent raking leaves, laying bricks, and cooking. Lastly, I make an extra effort to contribute to my local community. At my mosque and in my religious community, I’ve been involved in delivering food and other goods to the elderly, food service at mosque, setup and takedown, and various other volunteer activities. 	Yes	Yes	Mondays after 8pm\nWednesdays after 6\nThursdays after 9\nFriday all day\nSaturday all day\nSunday all day	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, Python	Although my first formal introduction to computer science was CS11 at Tufts, I would be lying if I said that’s what piqued my interest. In my senior year of high school, I had to complete a 50-page thesis as a part of the Global Studies program. I was originally set on researching an International Relations topic, since that had been my interest for years. However, I was inspired by a previous student’s choice to write about baseball stats despite his similar area of study. I chose to complete my thesis on the impact drafting has on an NHL team’s success, and spent the next five months diving deep into a world of statistics, spreadsheets, and t-tests. I found myself working on even the most boring parts of the paper at 11pm when I didn’t even need to, thinking about it in my free time, and looking forward to the next time I could open my computer and contribute to the Excel sheet again. This project sparked my interest in data and computer science and is the true reason I chose to begin taking CS courses at Tufts and eventually decided to major in computer science. 	At the beginning of this school year, I overheard a friend of mine, Jake Flosi, talking about how much time he and his fellow JumboCasters spend looking for statistics for their games. I began thinking about ways to automate it but immediately ran into a wall when I realized I needed to learn web scraping. I sat down the next morning for half an hour and watched a couple of YouTube videos, then, with increasingly difficult sites, I practiced my skills until I got up to the Tufts Athletics site, starting with the volleyball section. It had much more complicated HTML and the data points I was scraping were all stored in different ways, but eventually, I was able to create a basic Pandas data frame with the volleyball player information. From there, I got to work building different pathways of data to grab, starting with defensive volleyball stats, moving on to separating overall and conference stats, and then adding soccer. After that, I added a functionality where you could choose which year you wanted to look at and different ways to parse the data (per player, per stat, with certain conditions, etc). I returned to Jake with what I had, and he asked me two important questions. First, can I access other schools, and second, how do I work with this? I realized that I hadn’t implemented any kind of interface a user could use to pull all the data I’d scraped. After building that, I looked into the HTML of other NESCAC schools’ sports pages and realized that every website was built the same, so my code already worked on it. Lastly, I looked over my code and noticed it was incredibly inefficient, so I condensed much of the code into modular functions that could work with multiple sports. I invested many hours into this project, but it never felt like work due to my passion for sports. Although I was unfortunately never able to fully complete it, I enjoyed every moment of the experience and learned a lot from it. 	https://github.com/akassa01, https://www.linkedin.com/in/arman-kassam-87ba19248/	11	3	1	9	2	8	10	6	4	5	7	12	I took a two part approach to evaluating which projects I’d prefer. First, areas of impact, and second, areas of expertise. I want to feel like I’m really making a difference, and the best way for me to do that is through seeing exactly how I’m helping those I meant to help, but also through having the necessary skill base for a given project. For the first criterion, I looked for projects that have the most direct impact on people in need. For these, I found that the Wily Network, Dillar Academy, Village Hub Food, English at Large, LGBTQ Senior Housing, and NEIP are most directly linked to impact, while others serve purposes such as helping spread the word to volunteers, store data, and create archives, all important functions, but now what I’m passionate about. Additionally, as someone with little background interest and/or knowledge in American law and the LGBTQ community, I didn’t feel that my skillsets properly fit those two projects. However, as someone who’s done lots of tutoring and food distribution, I found I had more vested interest in the previous four projects. I also have a specific identity interest in the Dillar Academy, given that it is geared towards Uyghurs and I’m also part of the Muslim community. With all that said, I also felt that I had an affinity for the Wily Network project, since I’ve seen the economic disparity here at Tufts, even in my own friend group, where some will receive smartphones for christmas and some get nothing. Even 