	She/her	2028	Yes	I’m drawn to JumboCode's initiative of applying the skills we learn in class to real-world projects that create meaningful social impact. While I learned a lot of concepts and practical skills in taking multiple classes every semester, I also easily lost sight of why we are learning all these skills and their applications. JumboCode provides a purpose behind all the things I learned that are not just mere formulas -we can apply things we learned to improve the world around us. Most importantly, I have always been involved in volunteering work throughout high school ranging from education to healthcare. JumboCode represents an opportunity to do volunteer work in a professional format that directly collaborates with the organizations. \n\nBy joining JumboCode, I wish to gain hands-on practical skills in coding relating to software development. Even though I am a BME major, skills such as coding and product development are also heavily used in making medical instrumentation. JumboCode’s structure also mirrors the corporate environment, and I am interested in learning about how different roles can put together a project. Additionally, I also hope to develop interdisciplinary thinking, which I believe can be applied to my future profession. JumboCodes projects are nonprofits that require a team to think beyond just the technical aspects. Similar to BME, it is also crucial to think about the social and ethical sides of medical instrumentation, and how a product can be safe and accessible to all the users. Overall, JumboCode prepares me well to transition into my profession. 	Volunteering has been an integral part of my life, especially throughout my high school career. As president of my high school Red Cross club, I frequently communicated with the local state chapter to organize and manage various initiatives, including four blood drives, fundraisers, and house fire prevention lessons for children at daycares. In addition to my role in the Red Cross, I served as a tutor, helping ESL students from countries such as Malaysia and Cameroon prepare for daily quizzes, tests, and state standardized exams. I was dedicated to providing them mentorship, socially and academically, in transitioning to the States and high school. During the past two summers, I also worked as a Teaching Assistant at the Dale Carnegie Training Center in Taiwan. There, I modeled public speaking techniques, led group activities to practice communication and teamwork skills, and provided personalized guidance to over 35 students. My responsibilities included managing after-class one-on-one sessions via phone calls, addressing questions and concerns, and keeping parents updated on their children’s progress. Lastly, my friends and I also work on a project aiming to solve gender disparity in STEM education and the workplace. We created STEM content to encourage female representation in the field of science and provide accessible learning opportunities on social media platforms. To make information in science and technology more accessible to teenagers, we summarized scientific research papers and updated them regularly on social media. 	Yes	Yes	I am available for a one-hour weekly team meeting basically every day, especially after 8 pm. 	\N	Python	 My first introduction to computer science is my AP Computer Science Principles in high school. Even though the AP Test does not require coding skills, my teacher still introduced us to coding with Python. Later on in the summer, I also got a certification in Python from the National Taiwan University where I studied the basics of Python in algorithms, function, and control flow. I also researched Python Crawlers and coded some fun games such as number guessing, nAnB, 20 questions, and Madlibs to master techniques. 	For my Python course at National Taiwan University, I coded a game number guessing game. This project is a great introduction to coding concepts in a fun and interactive way. The users were to feed into numbers and to keep track of the attempts. I started the project by listing out the game rules and making sure my programs can reach these goals. This involves generating random numbers and providing feedback based on the guesses. Thinking from the users’ perspectives, I make sure the feedback I provide can guide the users in the right direction and give hints about how close they are. I also make sure the game responds appropriately when the input is invalid (non-numeric value or exceeding the number limit). One challenge I have met was thinking from the users’ perspective when coding the games and giving clear instructions. There are many situations where users can input unexpected answers into the program, however, I found that asking other people to test out the program can find things I never considered. Overall, I found the project very rewarding because it helped me practice the foundation of Python by programming a game that I am most familiar with. 	\N	1	5	7	6	8	9	10	11	3	4	12	2	My priority is the West Medford Community Center because this project is related to my experience with the “Females in Science” project, and this is something that I think I could have improved in my past project in terms of providing science information to underrepresented groups or teenagers overall. I want to participate in this project to learn how newsletter creation and publication can be done. My second priority is Tufts General Counsel. I hope to go into the pharmaceuticals/healthcare industry in the future, and I want to learn about the legal issues with health information and how we can protect them. My third priority is English at Large because I feel this project is most connected to my identity. As a first-generation immigrant myself, I understand the struggles among immigrants in learning a new language and living in a completely different environment. In high school, I have been dedicated to being a mentor and creating a space for international students, and 