 generates a C++ client and server pair. The client initiates a Remote Procedure Call of a function by sending the function information to the server, the server then executes the function and sends back the result, which the client then reports, all via generated code. The project required a lot of pre-planning to figure out how we were going to generate the code and how we were going to set up our generation system to be adaptable for all different kinds of data types, including nested structs and arrays. I had also never previously coded in Python, so I was learning on the fly. The project taught me a lot about how to plan, specifically plan in a way that will be scaleable. Sometimes you have to do something in a "less efficient" way in the beginning in order to make it more efficient and elegant later on. Also, I learned how to learn a language while coding in it, which was then useful in my internship this past summer where I was working in a language I had never coded in before and had to learn on the job, while I was working on the project. 	\N	9	12	10	5	6	1	3	8	4	7	2	11	I am very excited about both the New England Innocence Project because it relates to work I did in high school relating to the Raise the Age campaign in New York, which changed the law to prevent 16- and 17-year olds from being tried as adults in the New York State court system. I went to Albany to lobby my local legislators on that legislation, which eventually passed and became law. I also worked with Reclaim our Vote to make phone calls to voters who had been purged from the voter rolls, for some because of felon disenfranchisement, to let them know that they were in fact still eligible to vote, either because of a change in legislation, or because they were eligible the whole time but were told they weren't when leaving prison. I am also very excited about A2Empowerment. As a TA expanding access to education is very important to me. In the computer science field there are such disparities in who has access to resources that make it easier to succeed, and I love helping break those down as a TA. A2Empowerment aligns with those values for me. I am also really drawn to the project by the fact it has local Cameroonian partners; so many organizations just do work without actually consulting the people they claim to help, and having local partners is such a good way to actually ensure the work you do is truly beneficial. LGBTQ Senior housing is also such an important issue. As a member of the LGBT community I think it's a necessity to look out for our elders. I am excited about the opportunity to use my technical skills to help any of these organizations.	\N	I have not	Bëor	
