	she/her	2027	Yes	Over the years, I’ve worked on a variety of projects—from creating a platform game in JavaScript and HTML to developing a triage system using priority queues in Java. These experiences taught me that programming is more than just writing code; it’s about approaching problems with creativity and determination to find solutions for real-world applications. To me, JumboCode is an opportunity to test and apply my programming skills to real-world projects, to hopefully help someone out and make their life just a little easier. By joining the club, I hope to gain experience working on large-scale projects, to implement the whole package (front end, back end, etc.). I’m excited to build something from beginning to end with a team of capable programmers, to be a part of the entire process starting from nothing. 	A few summers through the pandemic, I spent my time teaching English to children in rural China who had little or no access to English education. Through this program, I realized that there are many children around the world who were not as privileged as I was, to receive such an amazing education growing up, and it really made me want to help others. So, I continued pursuing local opportunities to help others throughout my high school experience. During the school year, I volunteered for the Ronald McDonald House Charity, supporting families with sick children in the hospital, cooking food and hosting activities on the weekends for the children and their families. I spent my hours after school at a local non-profit thrift store, helping the store managers by sorting and racking all donated items on the floor. Volunteering for my local community has always been a part of who I am and what I love to do, and it would be awesome to be able to continue this through the JumboCode club. 	Yes	Yes	Mondays - after 3 pm\nWednesday - after 6 pm\nThursdays - flexible\nFridays - after noon\nSaturday/Sunday - flexible	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python	When I was younger, I would sit in my dad’s office, and watch him type lines of code on the terminal to find the root cause of an Office 365 mailbox outage. My own introduction to computer science, independent of my father, began in elementary school through the Hour of Code program. There was one week per school year (Week of Code) that we would dedicate to learning how to code in block code, through Scratch or the Hour of Code program. Then, I went on to expand my coding toolbox throughout elementary school, in FLL Robotics (also block coding) and later in programming classes through middle/high school.	One of the more recent projects that I’m proud of is my work in EWB (Engineers Without Borders). Last year, we constructed a greenhouse, and I was part of the construction group that focused on interior design, which meant that we were building tables, plant beds, etc. from scratch. We started by planning out the design of the tables – given minimal instruction, it was up to us to optimize for structural soundness. Upon building the first prototype, we realized that our initial ideas may not have been the best ideas. These initial stages created a lot of challenges for us from a design perspective, and ultimately, we had to pivot directions in the design of our table. Another challenge we faced further along was in coordinating our plans for the greenhouse with other subgroups of the greenhouse project. We later learned of new plans from the agriculture/water distribution groups that forced us to adjust the size/placement of our table within the greenhouse. Despite these struggles, we were able to adapt and figure out a new plan that would satisfy everyone’s needs, and we continued to build as such. Now, the greenhouse sits behind Harleston (almost complete!). Throughout the process, I think that our biggest challenge was in coordinating one project between all of the groups, but with enough communication and open-mindedness, I think large-scale projects like this are very feasible.	\N	10	3	2	5	8	4	1	7	9	6	11	12	As a fellow member of the LGBTQ+ community, I think it would be really awesome to work on a project to benefit this community, especially a project that makes the community feel more welcoming for seniors. I am also very interested in working on the Wily Project - as I've mentioned in the previous questions, having volunteered for children before, I love helping children/students, and to be able to do something good for students who haven't had an easy college journey would be amazing. 	\N	No	Aerin	
“works well in a group, one of my most present devs, good person to rely on, GETS TO KNOW EVERYONE, SO INSPIRATIONAL” -Sristi Panchu	he/him	2026	No	My interest in web development stems from a desire to solve complex problems and make a tangible impact. JumboCode stands out to me because of its unique focus on social change and its commitment to empowering non-profits and social organizations, which would help build the foundations for a potential career in a client-facing role.\n\nDeveloping a project this year in JumboCode would allow me to get a balance between gaining technical experience and working with a client whose mission aligns with my personal values and goals. As someone who would like to pursue a client-facing career in the future, being able to work on a team and provide a deliverable that would benefit a client is something that I find fulfilling. \n\nWhat stands out to me about the projects is that there is no “one size fits all.” Being able to understand a client’s needs and developing a tailored solution for them would allow me to develop the soft skills that would be critical to the success of any team moving forward. \n\nIn essence, JumboCode offers the perfect combination of breaking down complex challenges, creating solutions, and developing the skills to drive positive change. I am eager to immerse myself in this agile environment and develop the necessary tools to meaningfully contribute to the field of social impact. \n	The past year, I worked on developing a website for The Lantern, and being able to use technology to bridge the gap between different communities and the information they need has been rewarding. I enjoyed the aspect of working on a team whose values aligned with that of the client, and I believe that we were able to develop good chemistry to efficiently develop a deliverable for them. \nAdditionally, this past year, I worked as an advising fellow with a nonprofit organization that aimed to provide academic and career advising to high-achieving, low-income high school students. As a part of the advising team, my primary role involved providing one-on-one academic and career advising to students. This included reviewing their academic plans and assisting them in setting meaningful educational and career goals. One of the most rewarding aspects of my role was building and nurturing positive relationships with the students I was privileged to advise. Through regular check-ins and follow-up meetings, I ensured that my advisees were progressing toward their academic and career goals.\nI'm eager to bring these valuable experiences to JumboCode and contribute to scaling projects in the nonprofit and social change field while maximizing their impact. My dedication to educational empowerment and social change drives me to seek innovative solutions and collaborative opportunities to create a brighter future for both individuals and communities.\n	Yes	Studying remotely	Free at nights M, W, Th, F, free saturday and sunday	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 160: Algorithms, CS30	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	My first introduction to computer science occurred during my involvement in a research project focused on coral microbiomes and their response to thermal stress. At the time, I was unaware of the applications of computation at the intersection of science and technology.  However, there was a steep learning curve in the realm of computational biology- getting a good grasp of R required practice with syntaxes of various packages. However, after several weeks of struggling and watching tutorials, I was able to generate plots and perform statistical tests. I gradually began to appreciate the impact of computer science at the intersection of science and technology. Initially, I was unaware of the extent to which computational tools could advance our understanding of complex biological systems. It was a rewarding process, not just because I was obtaining findings through data analysis, but because I was able to transform the data into tangible evidence to support my hypothesis. As I gradually understood the potential of computation in the field of research, it furthered my goals of pursuing work that combines the realms of science and technology, driven by the idea that innovation in this space can lead to impactful discoveries.	One project that I am proud of was the app that I had built during my summer internship at Bristol Myers Squibb. After visualizing the workflows for one of the team’s business processes, I realized that there were several bottlenecks characterized by a lot of manual work, which led to a lot of time being wasted. \nI had proposed developing an app to reduce manual data entry, but my idea was initially rejected as the team did not want to adjust their workflow due to the technical learning curve. My goal was to gather data and assess how much time was being spent on manual processes like managing documents and inputting data so that I could highlight the potential benefits of an app that could automate much of their tasks.\nTo tackle this, I gathered data that measured the inefficiencies of the current workflow, tracking key performance indicators such as the time spent on document processing and delays in information retrieval. I also worked closely with team members to get a better understanding of their workflow and the pain points they faced. After collecting and analyzing the data, I created visualizations to specifically show the team members where the bottlenecks were. Based on my research, I was able to present my findings to the team, who ultimately agreed that an app would be helpful to address some of their pain points. I was able to build the app for them, which would allow them to handle shipments from external vendors as well as manage their data that had been manually entered into their other systems, ultimately reducing up to 10 hours per week of manual work. \nOne of the most rewarding aspects of this project was seeing how analytical and empirical research could be leveraged to convince a group to change their mindset. I think that one of the biggest challenges in working in a team is to convince someone to accept a new way of doing things, and being able to let the team see that there was an alternative that would benefit them was something I found fulfilling. This project not only allowed me to apply and expand my technical skills but also gave me a sense of accomplishment in contributing to a solution that could benefit business and reduce complexity. \n	https://github.com/jonchung1205	9	2	3	8	6	7	12	4	1	10	5	11	I am most interested in working with English at Large because their mission is something that resonates with my personal experiences. Growing up with immigrant parents, I witnessed firsthand the importance of developing English skills in a new country. I understand how important it is to have the necessary support and resources to develop English proficiency because of the amount of opportunities that become more accessible. Furthermore, because of my experience working with educational nonprofit organizations and developing websites, I believe that I can meaningfully contribute to a project, both in my technical and work experience. By contributing to this project, I hope to develop something that would advance English at Large’s efforts to allow tutors to access the materials they need and play my part in supporting immigrants and helping them adjust adequately.\n\nMy second choice is Village Hub Food because I am also passionate about minimizing environmental harm, and addressing food insecurity is a great way to promote sustainability and raise awareness. The opportunity to develop a system that not only tracks pantry inventory but also collects demographic data about volunteers and recipients is exciting, as it will help the organization make data-driven decisions to better serve the community and the environment. My background in data science makes this project particularly appealing, as I can contribute to creating a solution that helps reduce waste and enhance the organization’s understanding of its impact. \n\nMy next choice is The Wily Network because of my experience working with students to help clarify their educational and career goals. The project structure of addressing social issues in a data-focused nature aligns with my background and interest in data science. I believe that I can bring valuable experience and a data-centric background that could benefit the development of the app to ultimately benefit the students served by The Wily Network. By streamlining the gift-giving process through automation, I can help reduce the manual workload for staff and ensure a more efficient, reliable system for students to receive gifts that celebrate their milestones.\n\nMy fourth choice is LCS Tutoring because it would allow me to apply my nonprofit experience from a technical view and aligns with my passion for education and mentorship, particularly in supporting younger students in their academic growth. I appreciate the opportunity to work on a project that directly impacts students in the Medford/Somerville community by improving the tutoring experience. Automating the matching process will save time and effort for both tutors and coordinators, allowing more focus on the quality of the tutoring itself. My technical background and interest in building streamlined solutions can contribute to making this process more efficient, ultimately benefiting both the students and the Tufts volunteers involved.\n\nA2Empowerment is my fifth choice because I strongly support their mission of empowering through education. The opportunity to contribute to a project that impacts students in Cameroon is meaningful to me, as it combines both my interest in global education and my experience working with organizations that aimed to expand resources and education in Africa. \n\n	\N	Yes, Lantern Club	Wulf	
