t end, Node.js for my backend, selenium in Python for web scraping the academic calendars, and then GitHub and Vercel for organization and deployment. A big challenge that I faced was web scrapping the academic calendars, especially in bulk. Websites have all different variable names, organization, and other features that make it very hard to create a generalized webscrapping script. What I did was I created a JSON file with specifics to each website such as span class with the proper info, paths to find buttons, etc. and then I created a Python script that went through the JSON file and took the necessary data for webscrapping the given urls inside the JSON file. I also tried other methods such as scrapping basically all of the information from the website and then filtering it. Right now I am in the middle of trying to find the best and easiest method of doing this before I go and try to get 1000 universities or so. 	Portfolio website: https://jonahpflasterportfolio.vercel.app/	12	5	11	4	2	6	10	3	7	1	9	8	I mainly ranked based off the projects stack and on impacts that I feel relate to my own life/interests.	Although I am a MechE, I am doing a cs minor and am super interested in web/app development! 	No	Hunthor	
	he/him	2026	No	For as long as I can remember, I’ve loved building websites. When I was 8 years old, I created my first website using iWeb on an iBook G4 for a small snack restaurant I started for my family, aptly named Dan’s Snack Shack. Although that site was as simple as a static menu page, I became obsessed with the possibilities of creatively using technology for a purpose. Since then, my fascination with technology has continued to shape my interests. \nMy motivation for rejoining JumboCode is driven by two main reasons: I want to be a part of a community that aligns with my passion for building websites and exploring a wider range of technology, while also supporting organizations dedicated to improving the community by delivering products that enhance their impact. JumboCode is the perfect combination of these two interests. In fact, it was one of the highlights I mentioned in my "Why Tufts?" essay as a major reason I wanted to attend Tufts in the first place.\nLast year, I had an incredible experience as a developer in JumboCode, where I learned new technologies, gained hands-on experience in web development, and met amazing people who shared my interests. Experiencing firsthand the sense of community and purpose that comes from working on a team was one of the most fulfilling parts of my time at Tufts. \nBeing part of something that not only challenges and educates me but also directly benefits the community is why I’m excited to be involved again this year and am eager to contribute to a team that will allow me to continue growing both personally and professionally.	I have a strong background and passion for volunteering and social engagement within my community, with a particular focus on benefiting non-profit organizations. Over the past four years, I’ve dedicated more than 800 hours to various programs and initiatives aimed at making a positive impact, and I hold the record for the most community service hours in my high school’s history.\nOne of my significant roles has been leading the Food Insecurity Committee at my high school, supporting our local food pantry, Loaves & Fishes, which serves over 1,000 families in surrounding towns. As part of my role, I organized and executed weekly food collections from students at my high school, community members, and local restaurants, ensuring a consistent supply of essential items for the food pantry. Additionally, I’ve volunteered at my town’s Council on Aging weekly senior meal site for several years as part of my commitment to fighting food insecurity.\nI’ve also supported a Community Farm that feeds people in my community and have participated in community trash pickup committees, working to maintain a clean and environmentally-responsible neighborhood. Beyond these roles, I’ve been heavily involved in the operations, logistics, and provision of technical assistance for my high school’s drama department, contributing to community engagement and enhancing the arts within my community.	Yes	Yes	Yes, I will be able to attend the all-club meetings. I am also available almost every evening and can be flexible with my schedule during the day. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 116: Introduction to Security	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python, Tailwind CSS, Vercel, Neon, Clerk, Laravel, Astro, Framer, Drizzle ORM, npm/pnpm	My first official computer science project was in middle school computer class where I created an interactive tourist guide for Morocco in JavaScript. Prior to that, I taught myself Java in 4th grade back when I used to code custom Minecraft plugins for my home-hosted server. 	One project I'm really proud of is the fully-customized e-commerce store I built and maintain for my dad's small technology business (batteryeliminatorstore.com). For over 20 years, my dad has been selling homemade technology gadgets from our basement. About 5 years ago, his outdated PrestaShop site finally gave out, and it was time for an upgrade. Having already built several websites for my own interests and hobbies, I stepped up to the challenge.\nThough initially daunting, with thousands of customers and over 200 products in the database, I spent that summer building a brand-new site from scratch. One major obstacle was that the old site had failed so unexpectedly that we couldn't extract all the product images and data. I scoured old files and web archives, managing to piece together the content for the new site. Another challenge was the need to remap the site for search engine indexing since we had no record of the old URLs. I created URL redirects for the new product pages, ensuring we didn’t lose traffic.\nThe new site ended up performing better than we had anticipated. I added flexibility for product customization at an upcharge, integrated a blog to feature customer testimonials and product ideas, and even created a subdivision for my grandfather’s ham radio accessory business. As the webmaster for both stores, I continually optimize SEO, add new products, and work to boost traffic—challenges I find both fun and rewarding.\nThis past summer, I continued to improve the site while also building five web apps of my own to sharpen my skills in preparation for JumboCode this year.	https://github.com/danglorioso, https://danglorioso.com/	4	8	7	1	3	5	9	2	10	6	11	12	Somerville Museum is my top pick for a few reasons. First, I genuinely love museums. This past summer, a few friends and I went on a museum tour across Massachusetts, visiting a new museum each week, so it's super exciting to have the opportunity to build a tool to help a museum manage its huge inventory. Additionally, Holden and Zach’s lively and entertaining presentation really stood out to me—it seems like it’s going to be a fun and collaborative team that I’d love to be a part of.From a technical standpoint, I have experience using the tools they’re using