	she/her	2026	Yes	I hope to gain more technical skills and make more friends	I have volunteered at an art education community before. The community mostly provides an online platform for students interested in art to connect with people who share similar interests and learn more about art history and art making. 	Yes	Yes	I'm mostly available on Friday, Wednesday and Thursday after 5pm, and the weekend	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 160: Algorithms	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, Node.js/Express, Python, Redux-toolkit, Next, AWS, Firebase, MongoDB, express, MUI, Tailwind, Flowbite	my first introduction to computer science was CS11	During the last summer, I had worked on a personal project with my 3 other friends. The motivation was: we are all in a Vietnamese Tech Community. Everyone in the community are all very supportive and willing to give out referrals to help people in need. Usually, people who can be a referrer will comment or make a post to notify others about that. Then, candidates (people who need referrals) will inbox the referrers, hoping that they would get a respond soon. This process is very time-consuming and frustrating for both party: the referrers have to go through all of their messages and read through all of the resumes sent to them, then set up the meeting with people who they think could be qualified, etc.; the candidates also have to wait to until the referrers get to their message, or maybe will never get a response since the referrers have filled up their referral slots. So, my friends and I decided to create a web app that can automatically match candidates with the opened referring slots, which fastens the referring process as well as reduce the frustration for both parties.\nMy responsibility then was in both front end and back end. In particular, I mainly worked on file storage solution, notification microservice, and some other front end components.\nTo be honest, I was really struggled, especially in the first few weeks, as I never had any experience building a web application before that. I remember myself spending hours reading the documents and watching multiple youtube videos just to understand how I could use OAuth and LinkedIn API for authorization (we wanted to connect the user's account with their linkedin!). Things actually got better as I understood more about how everything works. I gradually got myself bigger tickets like implementing both front end and back end of a user form, storing user files to S3 bucket; designing and implementing the notification microservice utilizing AWS Lambda, SQS, SES, Firebase; writing reducers for front end logic, etc. (Have to credit my awesome teammates here -- they helped me a lot! always opened for discussion and gave me so many advices, which helped me learn wayssss fasterrr!!)	https://github.com/lnguyen2693?tab=overview&from=2024-08-01&to=2024-08-31	5	1	9	12	7	4	11	8	2	3	6	10	At the place where I'm from, there are a lot of homeless kids who are taken advantage of. Usually, they are forced to sell things to people on the street, then at the end of the day, they have to give in all the money they made, which means they can't afford food even if they work for the whole day. To help them, my family usually bring them to a nearby restaurant or buy them food. That's why the Village Hub Food stood out to me. If a similar project existed in my hometown, a lot more kids will have access to food. I also think this is a perfect opportunity for me to apply my knowledge and contribute to a meaningful and impactful project, which is what I have been searching for.	\N	No	Saeros	
