	she/her	2026	Yes	I’m really excited about joining JumboCode and I was very inspired by all of the awesome presentations at the GIM. All the projects are working towards great causes, and I would love the opportunity to be a part of making changes in the world, no matter how big or small. All the project leads seemed knowledgeable and experienced, and learning from them would be a great way for me to learn and grow as a programmer while also gaining experience working in a team environment. I really value working in team environments because everyone has a different skill set that they are bringing to the table.  I also loved that all the project leads were excited about creating a team bond because I would love to get to know my team on a deeper level and hopefully come out of the project with new friends. This is a great opportunity to get to know other people that share similar interests and passions as me.	I have always liked working with kids, and during the pandemic I started a beginner coding class for kids, which I held outside in my backyard. I made the class free to attend because I wanted it to be accessible to anyone who was interested. Teaching this class was really rewarding, and I loved getting to introduce so many young kids to coding and seeing how inspired it made them. They were so creative and passionate about the games they made, and I hope that some of them will continue their coding journey in the future. Also, I was a leader for the Tufts CREATE pre-orientation program last summer. I had a group of first-years that I was in charge of, and it was a really unique experience getting to welcome them to Tufts. Some of them were pretty nervous and closed off in the beginning, but I made sure to create a safe and welcoming environment where they could feel comfortable talking to me about how they were feeling. I learned a lot from this experience about leadership and working in groups.	Yes	Yes	Monday/Wednesday before 3 or after 6, Tuesday/Thursday after 6, Friday any time, weekends generally pretty open	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 105: Programming Languages	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Swift, Git/GitHub, SQL, Python	My first introduction to computer science was through an app called Hopscotch, which is similar to Scratch but it’s an iOS app. I was around 9 years old when I was scrolling through the app store and thought that it seemed interesting, so I downloaded it. I had no idea that this app would be the reason that I realized I wanted to study computer science. The first time I got an object to move across the screen I was so excited, and the possibilities for what I could make seemed endless. The idea that I could turn anything from my imagination into a real game/project was so inspiring. I was instantly hooked on this app and I continued making games with Hopscotch for years. Hopscotch taught me how to think both logically and creatively, and it showed me the endless possibilities that computer science has to offer. 	This summer, I worked on creating a Python program for a professor at Tufts. The goal of the project was to create an interface for an existing semi-working Python program that analyzes data from an input file to predict what areas certain bird species will be present. This project really excited me because I have an interest in ecology and I have always loved bird-watching. I faced some challenges with this project because I had never worked with Python GUIs before, so I did a lot of research before getting started. I watched some YouTube videos, read documentation, and created simple test projects until I felt like I had a good grasp on how Python GUIs work. Another challenge was interpreting the original project script, because it had a lot of complex math-heavy functions. I worked through it slowly, reading the documentation and the given pseudocode, and adding print statements throughout to help myself understand what was going on. I’m proud of the end result because I was able to create a user-friendly, intuitive interface design. I love graphic design and UX/UI, so this project was a great opportunity for me to practice those skills. 	\N	8	7	12	3	11	2	4	9	6	5	1	10	I'm really inspired by all of these projects, and love that they all have such an impact on their respective communities, especially the projects that are helping people in disadvantages situations. Women empowerment and access to education are important to me -- I was part of Girls Who Code in high school, where we taught coding to young girls. A2Empowerment would be my top pick. My other 3 top picks would be New England Innocence Project, Somerville Museum, and LGBTQ Senior Housing because I love the missions that they are accomplishing. Although I would love to be a part of any project!!	My favorite CS joke is... (get ready for it): why don’t CS majors like nature? (drumroll please) it has too many bugs!!!	No	Ceorl	
