n I am interested in joining JumboCode is to learn more about and help different communities. \n\nI am primarily interested in working with Bread & Roses since I resonate with their mission of addressing food insecurity while promoting self empowerment. I think fostering a sense of community and self-empowerment is important in making people feel heard and inspired to transform their lives. \n\nI am also very interested in working with English at Large. My parents are immigrants, and I think retaining a sense of our own culture while living in America has been important to my family. I think providing adult immigrants with English language resources is important for giving them the confidence to live in American communities. \n\nI am interested in working with Dillar Academy. I think it is important to bridge the educational disparity gap among Uyghurs by providing them with English learning resources at no cost. Supporting Uyghurs through Dillar Academy is important in supporting the lives of future Uyghur communities.\n\nI am interested in working on the projects and learning about the communities they support!	\N	\N	Anárion	
	He/Him	2027	No	I want to join JumboCode because of my deep-rooted passion for computer science and the opportunity to collaborate with others who share that same enthusiasm. I aspire to work in the tech industry, and I know that joining JumboCode will expose me to valuable real-world experiences in full-stack development while helping me grow my technical skills. I strongly believe that the best way to solidify what you learn is by applying it in meaningful, real-world projects—JumboCode provides the perfect platform for that.\n\nThe fact that JumboCode works with non-profits is especially exciting to me, as it means my contributions will have a tangible, positive impact on the world. That’s a major motivator for me.\n\nI am interested in the West Medford Community Center project, because although I have engaged in the community at home, I feel that I am missing out on working with non-profits near Tufts. This project would be a great way for me to give back to Medford.\n\nI’m particularly drawn to projects like English at Large and Dillar Academy. Having moved to the U.S. from Hong Kong at age 13, I experienced the challenges of adjusting to a new culture and language firsthand. I deeply understand the importance of English proficiency and cultural integration for immigrants who are pursuing the American Dream, and I’d love to help them in that journey.\n\nAnother project that resonates with me is LCS Tutoring. I have extensive tutoring experience, and supporting students is something close to my heart. I believe my skills and experiences would align well with the mission of LCS, and I would really enjoy contributing to that initiative.	In the summer of 2022, I volunteered at SquashBusters, a program that uses squash as a vehicle to teach at-risk teens important life skills. On the first day, the students were hesitant, staring at the floor and reluctant to engage. Squash is not an easy sport to pick up, and their fear of failure was palpable. I pushed them just beyond their comfort zones, carefully adjusting my pace to challenge them without overwhelming them. Initially, they were frustrated, but soon, they began to realize that my challenge was a sign of belief in their abilities. Over time, I watched them grow more determined, and by the end of the program, they were not only more skilled but also brimming with confidence—a transformation that was inspiring to witness.\n\nMy experience at SquashBusters showed me the immense power of confidence and self-belief. Since then, I’ve also worked as an instructor for middle school students in computer science through iD Tech Camps. I saw a similar transformation in those students, many of whom started out nervous but finished the camp with newfound skills and pride in their achievements. Helping these students succeed, despite their initial doubts, is what made the hard work worthwhile.	Yes	Yes	Mostly available on weekends, Tuesday and Thursday mornings\nFlexible schedule. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, currently taking cs40	C/C++, SQL, Python	My first introduction to computer science was Scratch when I was in elementary school. I have been coding ever since. 	One project I’m particularly proud of is a baseball analytics project I worked on at Tufts University with a team of five students from the Baseball Analytics Team at Tufts. The project was part of a hackathon administered by the Cincinnati Reds, a MLB team. The goal was to choose MLB pitchers that would excel in a different role. Roles included starters, long relievers, closers, and platoon pitchers.  \n\nWorking with the team presented both collaborative and technical challenges, especially as we navigated complex, interdependent baseball statistics. We divided tasks effectively, but ensuring the model’s accuracy without overfitting required close coordination and frequent code reviews. The project allowed me to combine data analysis with teamwork to solve real-world problems in sports analytics, which was enjoyable.\n\nAfter presenting our results to top level executives at the Cincinnati Reds, we were presented with 3rd place out of 30+ college teams, a great accomplishment I am very proud of. \n\nThe project is in my Github if you all are interested!	https://github.com/emerson-w	1	6	10	5	3	8	12	4	2	9	11	7	I have talked about the projects I am interested in in the first page of responses.	No.	No	Ohtar	
	she/her	2027	Yes	What attracts me most to JumboCode is its dedication to community service, and each project’s decision to work with local organizations. I have always been drawn to volunteer work, and care deeply about making a real, tangible difference to the communities I work with. With JumboCode, I hope to integrate my interest in computer science with my passion for community service.\n\nAs I progress through my computer science classes, I have realized that although I have gained coding skills, I have not learned how to apply them in a real-world context. This summer, my best friend’s mom launched her own company, The Crafty Lounge, offering crocheting and knitting lessons. When she was having trouble with her booking system, I offered to take a look, hoping to fix the problem. However, I realized that I had no idea how to approach the problem and was unable to contribute to a cause that I really cared about.\n\nI hope that by joining JumboCode, under the guidance of team managers, I can learn how to apply my knowledge of computer science to real world projects that benefit communities that I care about. \n	Conversations to Remember: Starting from 2020, I have worked with Conversations to Remember, an organization that connects students with patients living with dementia in senior care facilities across the US. As a volunteer, I engage in two zoom calls a week with two separate patients, one of whom I have been speaking to for almost 3 years. As the Student Outreach Coordinator for the organization, I schedule virtual meetings, oversee the matching process, liaise with senior living communities, and troubleshoot technical issues. I also onboard roughly 4 new volunteers per month, recruiting members by holding zoom meetings with interested high school and college students. \n\nConcerts in Motion: I have been volunteering since 2019, playing violin/guitar at senior care facilities in New York City. I became a Youth Ambassador for the program in 2021, working on expanding the program to colleges and high schools. I arranged and led monthly interactive sessions at 6 different nursing homes, and coordinated with senior centers to match musicians to specific locations. I also successfully expanded the program by starting a branch of Concerts in Motion to The New School’s Parsons School of Music, attracting over 50 new volunteers.\n\nSunnyside Community Services: Starting in 2018, I have volunteered for Sunnyside Community Services in Sunnyside, Queens. At home, I volunteer twice a week for their Social Adult Day program, which offers an alternative to nursing home placement for elderly residents in Sunnyside. My tasks include aiding in preparing meals, organizing therapeutic activities, and hosting emotional support sessions. I also fostered a partnership between Sunnyside Community Center and Concerts in Motion, hosting group music therapy sessions twice a month.\n\nLove Violin: Volunteered as an Assistant Teacher, providing free violin instruction to 8 students aged 5-12 from underserved communities who could not afford violin lessons. During my time as an assistant teacher, I led two group classes of four students each, designing personalized and creative lesson plans tailored to different learning styles and skill levels. I also monitored student progress through practice charts and consistently communicated with parents, delivering their child’s updates in weekly emails.\n\nFood For Thought Festival: At Tufts, I worked as a part of the programming team for the Food For Thought Festival in collaboration with Harvard’s Alternate Protein Project. I collaborated closely with my team to organize and execute the programming for the conference, which attracted over 100 attendees from 60 universities. We studied upcoming work in cellular agriculture, and connected with lead researchers in the field to attend the festival. 	Yes	Yes	All day saturday and sundays, Mondays: 12-4, 6-8, Tuesdays: 3-8, Wednesday: 12-4, 6-8, Thursday: 6-8, Friday 3-9	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, Python	CS11 was my first official introduction to computer science! Coming to Tufts, I knew that I wanted to learn programming, but I had no idea what I was getting myself into.  I realized that I was interested in computer science during my junior year of high school, where I worked on a project for Conversations To Remember. As the Student Outreach Coordinator, I was tasked with improving the volunteer sign-up system, and updating the website to integrate the new system. Working with web developers, I felt completely out of my depth, and heavily on their experience to complete the project. Watching the developers inspired me to pursue a degree in CS, and introduced me to the possibilities of what I could do with programming knowledge. 	The project that I am most proud of is the one I worked on with Conversations to Remember during my junior year of high school. Though I lacked programming skills at the time, I was heavily involved in the UI-UX and design side of the project, drawing from my personal experiences as a volunteer to identify the things that would make the new features more user-friendly. Though this project does not reflect my skills as an actual programmer, it is by far the most rewarding because I get to see the impact it makes on a community I care about, and continues to be used today by Conversations to Remember.\n\nAs the Student Outreach Coordinator for the program, I was responsible for overseeing new and returning volunteer sign-ups, matching students with specific patients, and scheduling calls with specific facilities. Similar to the problem LCS Tutoring currently faces, we were managing all of our sign-ups via google form but as the program expanded, coordinating all the information became unmanageable. As an eager high school junior, I was ready to jump into the project with virtually no technical experience. Paired with two (actually experienced) developers, we worked as a team to improve the sign-up system. Because the developers were not volunteers themselves, it was up to me to clearly identify and communicate the necessary features to implement. \n\nBased on the feedback I had received from my trainees, having a single form for both new and returning volunteers was both confusing for the students and coordinators. To solve this problem, we created two separate forms, integrating the new-volunteer form in the website, while sending out the returning volunteer form in a separate email.\n\nThe biggest challenge of our project was the transition from Zoom to Jitsi, a smaller online meeting platform. When we test-launched the new platform, students were struggling to download the Jitsi app and had a hard time signing into their calls. To combat this, we got rid of the need to download the app by integrating the meeting platform into the website, where volunteers could log in directly with their personalized meeting code. However, after relaunching, we discovered that volunteers (especially Zoom users) were struggling with the transition to a web browser and had difficulty finding the login portal. Finally, after much reworking, we realized that the best way would be to sidestep the login process entirely, and just allow volunteers to connect directly from the home page of the website.\n\nAlthough I was not heavily involved with the actual programming of this project, it taught me valuable lessons on how to not only identify problems, but also how to effectively address them through communication, teamwork, and critical thinking. I hope that through JumboCode, I will be able to get the opportunity to apply my newfound knowledge of CS to similarly impactful projects.	\N	1	3	10	11	5	6	9	4	12	2	7	8	My first choice is the West Medford Community Center. At home, I have been a long time volunteer at the Sunnyside Community Center in Queens, a community-based nonprofit with very similar missions to the West Medford Community Center. In 2018, my grandparents permanently moved to Queens from China, and the Community Center has served as a place of safety, comfort, and socialization for them. Through my grandparents, I have seen first-hand the positive impact the Center has had on the residents of Sunnyside, and feel very passionate about helping the West Medford Community Center here in Medford so that it can reach more people. (also just as an added bonus: Neya was my CS11 Lab TA and got me through some crazy times lol!)\n\nMy second choices are Bread and Roses and the Village Food Hub because I am strongly aligned with the missions of these two organizations. At the Sunnyside Community Center, I volunteer as a part of the Social Adult Day Program, which offers an alternative to nursing home placement for elderly residents in Sunnyside. A big part of my work there is preparing meals for residents and stocking pantries. A lot of people who come in for meals do not have the physical strength to cook for themselves at home, or cannot afford meals regularly. Understanding the importance of these meals to so many lives, I really want to work for a project that supports this cause. 	\N	No	Bandobras Took	
