	she/her	2027	Yes	While I've participated in short-term service, which limited my capability in producing meaningful change, I aspire to collaborate with peers to achieve purposeful, longer-term changes to serve community organisations. By developing mechanisms that advance efficiency and increase visibility for organizations, I believe that I will amplify their impact and accomplish meaningful improvements towards our community. This is why I want to join JumboCode. I am dedicated to supporting the needs of the organization through software development projects, which will significantly reinforce their missions in a durable and impactful manner. JumboCode will be a bridge between the community's issues, my passion towards strengthening the relationship between Tufts and its host communities, and my coding experience. Additionally, I want to gain experience in applying my coding knowledge to real-world problems. Although I have explored and enjoyed CS in an academic environment, I wish to utilize my knowledge into tangible, professional skills that I can use in my career. I hope to gain practical experience through the specialized instruction of my leads and workshops. Lastly, I want to make connections with like-minded peers from different academic backgrounds. Being a part of a team that shares the common goal for serving the community with technology will be a special experience. I wish to learn more about different topics of software development through the leads and other members of JumboCode. By collaborating with peers who are also passionate about social activism, I strongly believe that we will grow and learn from each other. 	I participated and worked as a member of the support staff for the FOCUS pre-orientation program, which aims to connect first-years to community service learning. As a support staff member, I went to various service sites such as Theater @ First, the SCALE school for adults, the Emerald Necklace Conservancy, Trans Resistance MA, and the East Somerville Elementary School. I worked with the site representatives to instruct and encourage first-years on what they can do to help. By facilitating direction from the site representatives to the first-years, I not only improved my leadership skills but felt fulfilled by taking on a larger role in service. I felt that my dedication and eagerness towards service, combined with my role as a leader, was communicated to the first-years, strengthening their spirit towards service. Additionally, I was able to learn more about and support the needs identified by Tufts' host communities. I understood and connected to issues plaguing communities in Medford-Somerville, and felt that I contributed to strengthening the relationship between Tufts and its neighboring communities. Furthermore, I resonated with the concept of critical service learning, which is a service approach aimed at dismantling the larger structure that reinforces communities' need for service. Rather than traditional service learning, which aims to fulfill the student's feeling of satisfaction or educational requirements, critical service learning's objective is to dismantle the inequalities that create and sustain them. Although the service I've done more traditional and short-term, I am eager to participate in critical service. 	Yes	Yes	Monday 7-9pm, Tuesday 7-8 pm, Wednesday Thursday after 8pm, any time Saturday and Sunday	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 160: Algorithms	C/C++, Python	I took a physics class in high school that incorporates coding with Python to model experiments. This class helped me realize that I enjoy the problem-solving aspect of coding, as well as figuring out how to apply concepts to code.	The Gerp project for CS 15 encouraged me to pursue CS as a major. For the project, my partner and I had to build a hash that stored the words of the files in a given directory. This was the first project for which I had to work with a partner, and it was challenging at first to coordinate schedules, accommodate to their work approach, and voice my thoughts on how to formulate the code. However, we made a project timeline and set out to start and finish early. Coding and debugging each step of the project meticulously helped us approach the project calmly, and finish with ample time to test every case. I'm particularly proud of this project because I learned to collaborate to code. Expressing my thoughts was difficult, especially when I myself wasn't sure of a secure plan on what to do. However, I realized that talking through solutions was helpful. In addition to collaborating for the first time, I was proud of my partner and I for working through each step, making sure the different parts worked by itself and with each other. Without this methodical approach and a corresponding schedule, I don't think we would have been able to succeed in finishing our project with consideration to many edge cases. Overall, this project has instilled confidence in me that I can work with others to code longer, more demanding projects. 	\N	5	8	1	7	12	4	10	2	3	11	9	6	I resonate with the mission of the Wily Network. I hope to learn more about the specific issues that college students without familial support face, and help peers of my age. For a similar reason, I want to work for LCS tutoring. I am eager to work with clubs at Tufts, and connect with them to support their mission. As someone who is privileged to have access to education, I want to give back by helping LCS connect tutors to students. 	\N	\N	Paladin Took II	
	he/him	2028	No	One day, I asked my dad what all the funky characters on his screen really meant.  He sat me down in my room and taught me HTML. My mind was completely blown. I hyped up all my friends, and my dad got them all together in my dining room. He brought out a big monitor and taught all my friends the magic of web development. Our first project was called “MovieMatcher”, pairing the user to a recommended movie based on their preferences. What followed was a year-long class on PHP, Javascript, NodeJS, SQL, MongoDB, and a whole new world of programming languages. \n\nSince then, I have presented an app using the LinkedIn API to a potential investor, created an app for the Chrome Web Store known as FlowTab, and dabbled in programming every day on Google Meet with my friend calls trying to make our own programming language or our own version of TailwindCSS, called FlowshadeUI on the npmjs website ("flowshadeui").\n\nOkay, a long introduction into my world of programming experience. But why do I want to join JumboCode? Because JumboCode allows me to use what I know not just for my personal fun (which I've had a lot of!) but for real impact in our communities of Somerville, Medford. That's why I love the idea of projects like "English at Large", which will help immigrant communities (like those of my parents) learn English, which is crucial to their survival in America. My mother and father struggled to learn English when he came to this country and services like English at Large are what helped them become successful. \n\nI hope to gain from JumboCode a community of people who love coding just like me and can drown their thoughts on the keyboard to create something meaningful and impactful. I hope to learn and appreciate the power of technology and the knowledge of coding in the development and betterment of our world. I hope to indulge in the creation of an app with its full tech stack, adding on to my experience with tailwind and frameworks such as React. The fact that this is all happening at Tufts, my university, makes it all too special!  	My first volunteering experience was at the food pantry, Bread of Life in Malden, Massachusetts. My preconceived notions about food pantries were simple: people donated cans of beans or fruit, and people in need took those cans. But, as I soon realized, the process is extremely difficult and requires extensive planning and organization. My time at the Bread of Life involved setting up bags of weekly food meticulously planned to be nutritious and weigh the perfect amount for healthy living. I carried heavy boxes into trucks and one worker took me with him to deliver them to people’s homes. I was even able to participate when the organization was moving to their new, larger warehouse. Of course, while I did learn the value of appreciating what I was given in life, I also learned the harsh reality that others were living under.\n\nSince the fourth grade, I was playing tennis under the ‘Tennis Skills and Drills’ program at my high school. This once-small program at the school has turned into two varsity teams, one coeducational and one boys. This program has given me endless memories and bonds, with me and my teammates playing all throughout the summer and going to the Flaming Grill and Buffet together. As I graduated to the varsity team, I was given the opportunity to volunteer as a coach for the Tennis Skills and Drills program, teaching lower schoolers and high schoolers alike. Placed in the position of a teacher, I had an increased respect for the amount of patience that is needed in the profession. It was a proud moment to come full circle, going from being taught tennis by a coach that I have become good friends with, to helping that coach teach more future varsity tennis players.\n\nOne of the volunteering experiences I am most proud of is at an organization known as Bike to the Sea, that runs a rail-to-trail bike path from Everett and Boston to Lynn. I believe that the path allows for a beautiful pedestrian and bike-centered connection of these Boston North Shore communities. This path has been a great source of enjoyment and natural connection for me, and as I live near the path, I frequently ride on it on my bike. Before finding out about the path, I contacted my town’s local committee member and we sat together at our local Dunkin’ Donuts talking about my ideas for the future of the abandoned schools in our town—I was thinking about parks and bike-friendly areas. She redirected me to the Bike to the Sea organization, and from there, I was able to set up a stand to advertise their organization’s membership at the local fair, participate in trail cleanups, and go on group rides with other members. This experience of a pollution-free and open space for physical activity was eye-opening, especially living near a major highway.	Yes	Yes	I am available on Wednesdays, Saturdays, and Sundays after 6pm. On Thursdays I am available after 3pm and on Fridays I am available after 12pm.	\N	HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python, Vue, Vite, Next, Nuxt, PHP, SASS	My father introduced me after the summer of 8th grade and I am self taught ever since.	https://youtu.be/O3hIvuKCSIs?feature=shared\nThis project was the result of many years of experience coding with my best friend Sameera Murthy, as part of the Congressional App Challenge for our district, MA06 with Representative Seth Moulton. \n\nClimate change is a really important issue to me. Looking back at my Google Photos from years ago, I see videos of my younger self playing in the Massachusetts snow in December, making snow angels and laughing my heart out. There are pictures of me with my parents in New York City, enjoying the vibes of the snowy city during winter break. The arrival of spring is hard-earned and unexpected. Nowadays, New England is warming faster than any other region in the United States, and spring comes faster with no snow to enjoy. This is not the life I want to be living.\n\nMy solution? Create an app that, even in our busy day-to-day schedules, allows users to take small steps to keep track of their carbon emissions in order to reduce our global carbon footprint. This app was intended for anyone, young or old, who wanted to make an impact to stop the climate crisis.\n\nThis project was made with months upon months of hard work, planning, creating a design on Figma, and arguing. Our biggest difficulty that we spent a while on was finding the right framework to use for our code. Progressive Web Apps are websites that are compatible to be apps on both iPhone and android. We tried using React at first, as well as Nuxt, but these frameworks only complicated the process. Then, we bumped into Svelte, which allowed us to easily make reusable components while keeping simplicity in our code. It also allowed us to integrate the Progressive Web App system with no difficulties. On a personal level, me and my friend disagreed very often on how the coding structure would work and how the app would even function. We had arguments for hours on how the buttons should be placed and how even something as simple as an svg should appear.\n\nIn the end, our app won first place for our district for the Congressional App Challenge and we were presented with a Congressional Recognition certificate in addition to an invitation to the prestigious #HouseofCode event in the U.S. Congress this year. We were able to meet our state representative Seth Moulton, and our app is currently being framed in the U.S. Capitol. This was an experience I will never forget in my life, and I am really proud of how my coding experience helped me create something truly impactful, even recognized on a congressional level.	FlowTab: https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/flowtab/ainabceojclhldicflamfgghhkmmcnlo   CarbonQuest:https://carbonquest.vercel.app/  NPJSapp, FlowshadeUI: https://www.npmjs.com/package/flowshadeui  Flowshade GitHub: https://github.com/flowshade 	4	2	12	5	9	10	7	3	1	11	6	8	Languages are really important to me—they are the bridge for people into the world. Sanskrit is an important language in the Hindu religion, but my lack of knowledge of the language prevents me from understanding what the religion I follow is even about. The chants seem like gibberish and I feel as if I am unable to follow Hinduism properly.\n\nMy father and mother's experiences immigrating to the United States are similar to the issues the "English at Large" program is seeking to solve. They had limited knowledge of English, coming from South India, and struggled to find footing. They were scammed in many ways as people assumed they were vulnerable due to their English knowledge, especially when trying to find an apartment to live in. That's why "English at Large" resonates with me the most, as I would love to assist in the creation of an easy-to-use, understandable catalog system for English students. \n\nJust like how learning Sanskrit helps me understand my religion properly, helping others learn the crucial language of English will help them participate in greater American society culturally, socially, economically, and all of these to the best of their ability.\n\nIn addition, the group at the "English at Large" seemed the most charismatic at the General Interest Meeting, and I vibed with them so much (I love karaoke and I want life advice!!)\n\nMy second option is the "Village Food Hub", which my experience at the Bread of Life in Malden makes all to important. I have seen first-hand the needs of those unable to get the food they need to survive, and the power of food pantries to provide to the community. Working to distribute and deliver food for Bread of Life, I have faced a lot of difficulty reading papers and printed-out spreadsheets to find and deliver the proper food to those who needed it. It was a long, and painstaking process. Digitizing inventories would make the process all the more easier, and would expand the impact that food pantries have on our local communities of Medford, Somerville, and beyond.	\N	\N	Pervinca Took	
	he/him	2028	Yes	I want to join JumboCode because I am passionate about using technology to make a positive impact on communities. During my gap year, I was part of a movement to digitize records for our local government and teach computer literacy to entrepreneurs in my village in Kilimanjaro. This experience opened my eyes to how technology can empower people and improve their lives. Similarly, when I built a personal web application to track my workouts, I faced many challenges, but I also saw the potential of technology to solve real-world problems.\n\nJoining JumboCode would allow me to continue this journey by applying my growing skills in computer science to meaningful projects that benefit others. I am eager to collaborate with like-minded peers on non-profit organization projects, tackle new challenges, and learn from real-world software development experiences. Being part of a team that creates technology for social good aligns perfectly with my values and aspirations as a computer science student\n\nAdditionally, I am eager to build connections and bond with other students. I’m also looking forward to participating in fun activities like cooking, karaoke nights, and enjoying downtime together after long hours of coding; and as a freshman, I'd be excited to work alongside experienced individuals who will mentor me on navigating CS and beyond. \nJumboCode will be my new home away from home!!	Before my high school graduation, I founded the "Arise and Shine" support group, which started as an emotional outlet and evolved into a thriving center for mental well-being. During my time with "Arise and Shine," I met Naila, a 12-year-old girl whose dreams of education were stifled by her tribe's entrenched tradition of early marriages for girls. \n\nTo help Naila and other children in her village, I initiated an outreach-tutoring program with support from dedicated teachers and fellow students. We made bi-weekly visits to Naila's village, offering vital academic support to the local children.  \n\nInitially, we faced resistance from parents in the village. However, with the assistance of group members from the same tribe, we worked to change their perceptions of our program. We emphasized how education could empower Naila to support herself and her family, offering an alternative to the traditional bride price of 20 cows. Eventually, we obtained permission for Naila to resume her primary education.  \n\nNaila's graduation and acceptance into a nearby secondary boarding school filled me with immense pride and gratitude. Her journey has become a powerful symbol of the transformative potential of education. Her success, alongside other talented, marginalized girls I've had the privilege to support, has further fueled my determination to make a difference in the lives of those facing similar challenges.  \n\nJumbocode's mission,  which emphasizes community service, feels like my new home  away from "Arise and Shine." My goal is to continue advocating for education, empowerment, and mental well-being in underserved communities. Together with like-minded individuals, I'm committed to leaving a lasting impact and ensuring everyone has a chance to rise, shine, and fulfill their potential.\n	Yes	Yes	I am very available.	\N	HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, SQL	My first introduction to computer science was during my gap year last year. I had the privilege of being part of a movement aimed at digitizing the records of our local government and teaching computer literacy to young, uneducated entrepreneurs and other community members who were unfamiliar with technology. During this period, It was when I began learning JavaScript, CSS and HTML.	I created a personal web application to track my workouts while I was back home in Tanzania. The app features a map, which I implemented using a map API, and it uses HTML and JavaScript to create interactive and user-friendly interfaces. Users (me) would input data through an HTML form, specifying the type of activity (running or cycling), the distance covered, the duration, and the cadence. The app then stores this information and displays it visually on the map. This provided me with a clear overview of each workout session that i'd do.\n\nBuilding this project was quite challenging, especially since it was my first major project after I started learning programming this year. Implementing the map API was particularly difficult—I had to learn how to integrate external APIs into a web application, handle potential errors, and ensure that the data was accurately plotted on the map. Additionally, making the interface both interactive and intuitive required a solid understanding of JavaScript. Despite these challenges, this project significantly improved my coding skills, gave me practical experience in web development, and fueled my passion learn more about CS.	\N	11	4	10	8	3	9	7	2	12	6	1	5	 A2Empowerment (PM: Rofeeah, TL: Will)\n\nI am eager to join the A2Empowerment project because of my personal connection to its mission. Growing up, I witnessed my sister’s dreams of continuing her education being cut short due to the financial constraints of my family. She was unaware of the external resources that could have helped her, largely because many organizations lacked accessible platforms like web applications. Therefore, the A2Empowerment project's mission resonates with me deeply, especially when I think of communities back home in Tanzania.\n\nBy joining A2Empowerment, I hope to not only refine my skills, expand my network, and gain valuable experience, but also to contribute to making educational opportunities more accessible for marginalized communities, such as those in Cameroon. My goal is to help bridge the gap that too often prevents bright and deserving students from reaching their full potential.\n\nAdditionally, I am very excited to participate in any project, from LCS Tutoring, Dillar Academy,\nVillage Hub Food, tufts General Counsel, Bread & Roses, LGB