	he/him	2028	No	I've been coding since I was in grade school and it is probably my favorite activity. I would love to join JumboCode because it seems like an incredible club use my passion to help the community. I've never really done web development, so I think JumboCode is a really great learning opportunity to participate in a web development project from start to finish. I also don't have much experience programming for a group project and I would love to develop this industry essential skill. And on top of it all, JumboCode just seems like such a fun way to spend my time.	I've done community service projects for the community garden in my hometown to expand the planting plots. I volunteered at my local church, and have participated in church-led humanitarian expeditions. I've also participated in a few charity 5k runs. I was also a TA for a chemistry class.	Yes	Yes	Monday: 9-13 & 18-22, Tuesday & Thursday: 16:30-22:, Wednesday: 15-16 & 18-19, Friday:17-22, Saturday: all day except 1, Sunday: all day except 12-14	\N	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python	I've been coding since elementary school showed me Scratch.	Over the past summer, I had an internship with a material science company where I worked on a project that fused atomic physics and computer science. My project was to create a program that could take an input gamma spectrum (distribution of gamma ray energies) that was gathered using a scintillator (a detector that can create gamma emission spectrum) near a radioactive isotope (Isotope that naturally decays, emitting alpha, beta, and gamma radiation), and identify the radioactive isotope which created the spectrum (I'm sorry to whoever is reading this, the physics is hard for me to explain). One big challenge of the project was actually getting to see spectra of some rare and military isotopes such as Weapons Grade Plutonium. Because our company did not have access to many of the dangerous isotopes, I had to spend a lot of time creating an accurate model of the radioactive decay of isotopes. This modeling involved using GEANT4, a powerful C++ physics simulation toolkit, which was pretty complex and difficult to handle.	https://github.com/WoodchuckXL, https://woodchuckxl.itch.io	5	7	10	3	9	6	12	4	1	2	11	8	I would prefer to work on a project that is relatively local, because I think it would be very helpful to be able to meet the client directly to get to know their needs better and just to know who our team is working to help. My first choice is English at Large because of the aforementioned proximity, along with my personal interest in learning more about creating and manipulating large databases.	\N	No	Guthlaf	
	they/he	Masters Student (Tufts undergrad)	Yes	Programming has always been magical to me. I remember the first time I transformed an abstract idea into working code, and the first I transitioned from solving leetcode problems to developing software for real-world problems. It brought me a lot of joy, knowing I could use my education to make a change in the world. I want to join JumboCode so I can volunteer my computer science skills as a student, make a positive difference to the local community, collaborate with passionate Tufts students, and sharpen my technical skills through meaningful projects.\n\nMy personal goals and value align with JumboCode's mission to support underserved groups in the local community. As someone who has volunteered before, and taught at Somerville High School, I am particularly drawn to opportunities related to educational equity and English learning. I strongly believe that students of all backgrounds, ages and language level, deserve high quality education, and I know from experience how fulfilling it is to work towards bettering the community. The chance to make a visible, positive change excites me, and I know the impact I can make with JumboCode would fuel my passion and persistence to contribute meaningfully.\n\nBy supporting JumboCode in creating meaningful, long-term solutions for local communities, I hope to connect more deeply to the field of computer science, build strong bonds with Tufts students and serve the Greater Boston community. Ultimately, I'm eager to combine my technical skills with my passion for service, and I know JumboCode offers a unique and meaningful way to do that.	I have experience working as a teaching fellow for Breakthrough Greater Boston, a non-profit  which prepares low income-students for success in college. This role was very hands-on and fast-paced, and I enjoyed engaging with the Somerville community through its students, teachers and parents. As a math teaching fellow, I developed curriculum, held one-on-one with students, and co-ran the computer science club. This experience has prepared me with the patience and flexibility needed to best serve local communities.\n\nI was a Data Structures TA Spring 2024, where I hosted office hours and guided students on how to store, organize and access their data using C++. As someone who took the class previously, I found satisfaction in returning as a mentor. I would love to support, and be supported by other Tufts students again through JumboCode.\n\nI also have experience leading a volunteer group at Hong Kong's Red Cross Princess Alexandra School. This school's mission is to care for students with physical disabilities, and provide an adaptive learning environment, curriculum and career life plan. As volunteer leader, I would plan weekly activities, recruit and train volunteers, coordinate with school faculty, and translate from Cantonese and English for volunteers and students.	Yes	Yes	Free on Weekends. Free on weekdays except Monday (1-2 and 3-4:15), Tuesday (10:30-12:00), Wednesday (3-4:15) and Friday (10:30-12, 12:30-1:15).	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 115: Database Systems, CS 160: Algorithms, CS 170: Computation Theory, Game Design, Machine Learning, Intro to LLMs, Computer Vison, Big Data	C/C++, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Python	My first introduction to computer science was EN1: Introduction to computational design! I started as a biomedical engineering major, so MATLAB was my first language.	I am proud of my research with the Hassoun Lab, where I led a project on leveraging pre-trained, search-enabled LLMs to automate and improve biological data retrieval. I independently built an end-to-end retriever using Python, gained experience using packages such as Pandas, Numpy, Langchain, OpenAI and RDkit, and learned a new tech-stack (primarily docker, git, RESTful APIs and jupyter). Through my research project, I uncovered that although there is potential for search-enabled LLMs to outperform manual searches, there is a long way to go. Rather than seeing this as a setback, I saw this as an opportunity to continue my research. I continued my work and developed a QA generator for KEGG in one day. I built it using Python, leveraging tools such as BioPython, OpenAI API, Pandas, dotenv, CSV and JSON to handle biological text data processing. Planning what data to use, how to extract it from KEGG, and accessing it via the Entrez API was a big challenge. There was a vast amount of data, and much of it was was missing or irrelevant. I had to utilize big data management and data structures strategies to store, organize, and access compressed data through streaming. In addition, I learned to use the natural language processing tools needed to preprocess this textual data. My work thus far has led to my first major achievement: the submission of an article, Evaluation of search-enabled Pre-trained Large Language Models on retrieval tasks for the PubChem Database, to the JCIM. I always read research papers for class, but never imagined I would be the one writing them. The reward of being able to help medical researchers better access biological data inspires me to keep coding for good, which I know I can do with JumboCode.	https://github.com/beepoduck	10	9	4	12	3	7	1	5	2	8	6	11	I would be grateful to work as a developer on any JumboCode project, but I am particularly interested in contributing to LGBTQ+ senior housing. As someone who is queer, this project resonates with me personally. It would allow me to connect with LGBTQ+ individuals outside of college and support older queer people in the larger Boston community, something that brings me a deep sense of joy and purpose.\n\nI am also interested in LCS tutoring, English at Large, and Dillar Academy since it aligns with my passion for educational equity. My existing experience in education means I already have a solid understanding of the field, and I’m excited to build on my passion while continuing my contributions. 	Since this is the last year of my time at Tufts, I really look forward for the opportunity to contribute to JumboCode. There is a lot of passion and ideas I want to bring, and thank you for considering my application!	\N	Haldir	
