	he/him	2026	No	I honestly think coding is super fun. I hope I can meet other people who enjoy it as much as me, and we can work together on projects for some really cool causes!	Back home, I frequently volunteered at a medical office scanning records and working with their database. At tufts, I was on the Quiz Bowl e-board last year as the Communications director, and I try to find random volunteering events to do frequently (such as helping with varying department and Proctor events, for example).	Yes	Yes	All late afternoons/nights except Monday would work well, and I would have a lot of free time during the day on Mondays and Wednesdays as well to meet. Weekends I could probably do almost any time.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++	CS11	I'm probably most proud of the first personal project I worked on, which was a database containing various information about different tennis players. I'm a pretty big tennis fan, so it was a fun project to get an initial feel for different parts of programs that I didn't experience outside of cs classes. I learned how to do a lot of error checking and data validation to ensure that everything I imported from the official websites came across correctly and was in a readable format.\n\nFor the actual project, I got some hands on experience with C++ and data structures in general. For getting the information, I tried some various web scraping techniques, but I got most of the information by manually inputted information into a file to read in from. I used a hash table to both insert and search for players with the key being the player or tournament name, depending on the information I wanted. I used a different class as a value for things like their height, weight, whether they used a one or two handed backhand, etc, and a second hash table for tracking their head to head matchups against other players. I kept track of their matches and head to heads against opposing players, and other information like their ranking or prize money earned. I kept some other information like the tournaments played in a simple vector in order of when in the calendar year they are played. I faced a lot of challenges related to data validation and updating it with more recent information, but I'm mainly happy because it was a great experience to really get a better understanding of different ways to do the same thing and parsing through a lot of data. I had a lot of trouble figuring out how to store the information outside the running program, so I had to export it to various files and try to read it in again to see what could work.\n\nIt was honestly a super fun experience to just learn coding outside of a structured environment with no stakes and at my own pace, especially about something I am really interested in. It was also probably the first project that I really planned out and debugged alone outside of class homeworks, so that was another cool part of it for me personally.	\N	8	7	10	5	9	2	11	3	4	6	12	1	As somebody who is very interested in law and legal studies as well, the Tufts general counsel and New England Innocence project sound particularly compelling to me. Honestly, they all sound cool though!	\N	No	Fengel	
	He/him	2028	No	When I watched the GIM video archive, I was impressed by the tech stack that each team was using! I have a fair amount of coding experience, but haven't learned how to use things like AWS, Prism, mongoDB, and Vercel. By joining a JC team, I'd have to opportunity to explore these foreign languages, and simultaneously under the mentorship of experienced team leads and upperclassmen. This way, I'll be putting in the work to learn these myself, but always have multiple mentors that I can ask questions with and discuss ideas with all year round—all in all, that's an invaluable resource for me because I have a long way to go in my coding journey. I also enjoy how these aren't projects contained in their own bubble—they're going to be used for real purposes, will interact with other people, and I'll be apart of having a positive impact. JC's values align with my own values. \n\nFurthermore, I'm drawn in by how each and every team seemed so energetic and excited about their project. Compared to all the other academic GIMs I've attended as a freshman so far, JC stood out because I genuinely felt a "homey" sense. Each project cracked jokes and emphasized the importance of team-unity. Honestly, I was surprised when teams told their plans for fun team bonding experiences like rock climbing, baking, Mario-Kart, and more! In high school, my clubs were all strictly business—that's completely unlike JC. So I'm really excited to be apart of a JC team, make friends, and learn together with everyone!	In 10th grade, I started my own educational outreach program to promote STEM to my community. I won a $500 grant from my district's Education Foundation to fund materials and supplies for my program, and became sponsored by them and the public library. During my events, I would teach visitors (of all ages) all about physics, chemistry, and astronomy and answer any questions they'd have! Sometimes it was an energetic kindergartener asking about the physics of black holes—other times, it would be a curious grandfather who wanted advice on what telescope to buy his grandson. I held events at the public library, county arboretum, and local schools. My program has collaborated with the NJ flagship state observatory and the local Boyscouts/Cubscouts. In total, I've reached over 500 visitors from all across the county. \n\nI was also the Educational Outreach Director of my school's robotics club for 4 years. In that position, I designed and organized monthly volunteer outreach programs with all types of people in my community—other high schools, local elementary and middle schools, cubscouts, and more. They were all tailor made for different experience levels. My programs with the elementary schools and Cubscouts were introductory programming—for example, they had to block-code a pre-made robot to maneuver through a cardboard maze I built. With the local middle school, I organized 15+ students from my own high school to volunteer as referees for the middle school robotics competitions. We also demonstrate our robot to the team, explaining both the code (eg. Android Studio, Java) and our mechanical design (eg. mecanum drive train, 4-bar lifts, gear ratios).\n\nI've had a blast sharing with others the things I care about and enjoy learning, and I'm excited to continue that endeavor here at JC!	Yes	Yes	12:00 on Mon, Wed; 4:30 on Tue, Thu; anytime after 1:30 on Fri. Free all day on weekends.	\N	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python	Learning to make indie games using Unity with C# during middle school!	I built a website for the New Jersey Astronomical Association to help them better plan their public events! The problem I solved for them is very hard to illustrate, so in short: the observatory has a telescope whose vision is blocked at certain angles and at certain times. They wanted me to create a system calculates what angles work or don't work so that they know what to use during demos with the public! \n\nTo solve this, I first took a manual data points that corresponded to when exactly the vision got blocked. I made two excel sheets with all of this data—it took about two months to collect. Secondly, I found a best fit line and extrapolated an equation. I then coded my first website from scratch, hosted on Glitch.com, to utilize this equation. Languages I used include CSS, Java, JS, HTML. Essentially, it prompts the user for their desired telescope angle, and then returns how long to wait until the telescope is usable at that angle.\n\nSome issues I encountered was learning how to make the UI interface friendly. Though it's still not perfect, it's much improved. I solved this by making many text size changes, adding color contrast, and adding borders to buttons/inputs for better readability. I found that neatly placing/organizing my website elements via centering/alignment was especially hard and is something I'm looking to improve.\n\nAll in all it was a great first project and showed me how fun coding is, especially when you're creating it to solve a problem for someone else! When I presented it to them I felt really fulfilled and I hope to extend that concept here at JC.  	https://njaacalculator.glitch.me/normal_orientation.html	1	4	10	3	9	6	12	5	7	2	11	8	1) This group uniquely uses AWS which is something I'm interested in learning! I also think this project has a lot of impact potential because of the reach of the emails. \n\n2) Bread and Roses is a very large organization I feel like I would have a ton of impact by creating a scheduling app—that would help more volunteers get involved and help even more people. \n\n3) I love art on top of STEM, and I think the museum project is a great combination between these two! I also think creating a catalog would be very interesting and is a unique project idea. \n\n4) Similar idea to 3, I think creating database to track inventory is really interesting and I'd love to learn how to do it. \n\n5) Since I did educational outreach in high