	he/him	2027	No	I want to join JumboCode to apply my technical skills to directly benefit non-profit organizations, which I believe is a powerful way to contribute to the community. JumboCode offers the unique opportunity for me to learn more about real-world web development while doing social good in helping these organizations achieve their goals.	I have been a research assistant working on AI solutions to communication inequities and also have experience volunteering by helping to collect donations for my community	Yes	Yes	12-4p  M/W; after 8 any day; any time Fr/Sat/Sun	CS 15: Data Structures, CS 185: Computing for Developing Regions	HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	Making game mods in middle school	One project I’m particularly proud of is a research application designed to foster empathy between autistic and neurotypical individuals with diverse communication styles through LLM interventions. I’m drawn to this project because it opened my eyes to the potential of web applications in addressing complex social issues. It combined my interest in technology with a desire to make a positive impact and make communication more equitable. The project used a React frontend that was connected to a FastAPI backend. One of the biggest challenges was building a robust system that could handle unexpected behavior. This was necessary as we were relying on LLMs, which are highly sensitive and stochastic, so we had to put a lot of work into making the system reliable.	https://github.com/nvdaz	5	8	11	4	1	9	12	2	7	3	10	6	I am particularly interested in projects like Dillar Academy's portal web app and LCS Tutoring's matching system because I am personally interested in using technology to enhance educational access and efficiency. By developing user-friendly, sustainable platforms for these organizations, I would be able to contribute directly to bridging educational gaps and empowering both learners and tutors. This type of work excites me because it would allow me to apply technological to real-world challenges that have a meaningful impact on communities and making learning more equitable.\n\nI'm also drawn to projects such as Bread & Roses' volunteer scheduling app and the Somerville Museum's inventory management system, as they mix tech and community service. These projects offer the opportunity to create scalable solutions that not only streamline operations but also enable these organizations to focus more on their core missions. I'm particularly excited about the prospect of developing systems that can support long-term goals and make a lasting difference in how these non-profits serve their communities.	\N	\N	Idril Celebrindal	
	she/her	2027	Yes	While I enjoy studying CS at Tufts, I don’t necessarily enjoy the competitive environment that surrounds the CS community. It’s a challenging industry, especially knowing that you have to start really early to get a “head start”. \nAfter getting to know the JumboCode people, I felt really excited to finally have a welcoming community. A community of CS people that actually wasn’t aiming to “polish my resume” but instead to actually have fun, drink boba, go on little trips together, work on a meaningful project together, and so on. I appreciate the emphasis many of the project managers, tech leads, designers, and the e-board put on being friends before being co-workers. I mean, I can develop a website on my own, I’m sure anyone that joins JumboCode can do that if they put in the hours. However, what I really hope to gain is a supportive community of CS people. That is what makes JumboCode valuable to me. \n\n\nI hope to make friends to produce something together with. Friends to feel the satisfaction of having helped a small business - together. \n	Since 14, I have been involved in a FIRST Robotics Competition Team named ACI 35, from my high school. Our emphasis was on using tech for good and spreading STEM and robotics - not only making a robot. Under the roof of this team, as their captain, I initiated a movement that involved working with nonprofits to 1. Teach kids in need robotics and basic coding, 2. Recruit more young girls/women to robotics 3. Reach underprivileged schools/villages in the east of Turkey (where I am from) to help them start their own robotics team, own coding workshops etc. This project (aka my baby) did not only win multiple awards as a team but allowed us to actually do meaningful work with a lot of local nonprofits that focused on children with leukemia, orphan children, and girls of underdeveloped villages. My team, ACI 35, had only 3 girls when I got in. Starting with an embarrassing f/m ratio of 3/24, I managed to get our team to a 25/25 ratio full of young women passionate about coding, robotics, and community work. (Just so you know, for all these years of being the captain of this robotics team that won Nationals and went to world championships and so on, I never once wrote one single line of code, and frankly, really never knew or  learned how to code either. \n\n\nSomething else that I’m proud of is my work as a piano teacher for 2 years to orphan children. (I’ve been involved with children way too much now that I realize haha)\nI'm also a Discrete Math TA this term!	Yes	Yes	I am available early in the mornings and evening/late nights (except for Tuesday)	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, Git/GitHub	My first introduction to computer science was when I started crying in my CS 11 Lab and a TA had to pull me aside and give me a pep talk. I mean… I had definitely been introduced to CS before by the guys at my robotics team who were annoyed at me for bossing them around even though I didn’t know mechanics/coding at all. But my most sincere introduction to CS was on a september wednesday in 2023, my CS11 Lab. That was my introduction to how hard CS could be, how impossible it would look sometimes to me, how tiring it could get, and how inadequate I’d feel. But that was also my introduction to how people could actually grow into a field, how you could actually become competent enough to solve those problems you thought you could never solve, and how a random TA you found unapproachable would speak to you in a way that would keep you motivated for the next whole year. I love CS. I am proud of the work I’m doing and the improvement I’ve made. (shoutout to all the CS11 TAs! (if there’s any in this e-board lol))	One of the achievements I’m most proud of is leading the development of an Online Charging System (OCS) during my internship at i2i Systems. When I first took on the project, I was both excited and a bit overwhelmed. I was responsible for managing a team of 15 engineers, many of whom were using complex technologies like Volt Active Data, Apache Kafka, and Hazelcast for the first time—technologies that were also new to me.\nAt times, I felt the weight of this responsibility deeply. I had moments of doubt, wondering if I was equipped to lead a team through such a technically demanding project. But instead of shying away, I leaned into the challenge with curiosity. I asked countless questions, sought out learning opportunities, and made a point to grow alongside my team. Together, we navigated the steep learning curve, and I found that as I helped my teammates overcome obstacles, I was also overcoming my own.\nThe end result was a system that could process up to 5000 transactions per second with high availability and real-time balance management—something I never would have imagined I could help create at the start of my internship. But what I’m most proud of isn’t just the technical success; it’s the personal growth I experienced. I learned that even in moments of uncertainty, embracing the opportunity to learn and support others can lead to remarkable outcomes.\n	https://github.com/begumugurlu	4	1	8	11	6	5	12	3	2	7	9	10	1) My grandfather was a farmer. Every summer (and some winters - though it wouldn’t be the season), we’d go to my paternal grandparents’ house in a small city in east Turkey. My grandfather had a small piece of land that he inherited from his own father, where he (and my dad and uncles when they were children) harvested apples and apricots from a few trees. These fruits would later be divided into two groups: those to go to juice factories (i.e. those fruits that weren’t pretty enough to sell) and those to go to the farmers’ market. I’d then go with my grandfather and my cousin to yell “1kg for only 5 lira!’ at the market and look cute to elderly ladies to sell some apples and apricots. However, most of our work involved keeping track of all the trucks and boxes coming in and out, understanding who gave us what and what we gave back in return. While keeping track of all these, we’d also continue holding tight to the cash money on our small hands. We’d save all the money in little boxes and my grandfather, with his limited education and even more limited math, would try to understand how much he actually sold. My cousin and I then would use our 4th grade star-earning math skills to multiply, subtract, and divide to find out the exact kilos and exact liras we sold, lended, or left aside. He’d then carry all these boxes in his small car where we’d barely fit as two small children. It was a really messy work with (looking back) a great loss of efficiency and profit but to me, at the time, all this work seemed like the most innovative and efficient of them all. I was very happy to put my 11-year-old-skills to use to help my grandfather.\n\nI never had the opportunity to use my highschool math skills, or my finance skills, or my marketing skills that I developed over the years to help my grandfather - as we lost him the year I started highschool. \n\nSo I’d really love to work for Village Food Hub. I finally want to put my skills to good use.\n\n\n2) English is my second language. I learned English over the course of MANY years and still don’t feel like I’ve mastered it at all. I understand the struggle, I understand the challenge, and I want to be a part of the solution with the experience I’ve had so far. That’s why I would love to work for English At Large. (Plus, I’m a HUGE library nerd, in my senior year of high school I reorganized the book stacks (there were 52 of them) and their respective orders in the database. I’d love to work on keeping track of books and playing around with the library databases - it feels like entering a sacred space to me)\n\n3) I've been tutoring since 14. I've tutored middle school math to SAT math classes. I've tutored Autistic middle schoolers in learning English and orphan girls in Lego sets. I worked (and volunteered) as a piano teacher for 2 years. Here at Tufts, I am a tutor under STAAR Center for some CS and Math classes. I feel like I've established that I'm "in" the tutoring business. However it is definitely hard to keep track of the sessions, individuals' needs and the tutors that'd match these the best. I think I'd work very well for the project LCS Tutoring.\n\n\n	\N	Sadly, no	Valandil	
