d on over the summer. As an ACE++ student, I got the privilege to learn Bayesian statistics and probabilistic programming with PyMC. As a group of three, my teammates and I used our newly founded skills to make a program that assessed the probability of misinformation in stock market investing. We used dummy data to propose our idea but we hope to develop this further and use actual test scores soon!	https://github.com/tammittran	9	4	6	10	11	7	3	5	1	2	8	12	I am very passionate about student advocacy and food insecurity. As a student, I will always relate to feeling trapped and helpless especially when it comes down to studying and being a better student. I actually recently started finding tutors on the Tufts Tutor Finder website to get more help on Calculus III and had a hard time finding a tutor for my subject, connecting with them, and receiving help from them. I think there is a lot to be done to improve a student's experience towards tutoring, and I believe that the more we improve this community the easier it becomes for a student to reach out for more help. On the other hand, I want to work on projects such as food projects in general to make others who are not as fortunate as me more comfortable receiving help. I've taken vegetables and fruits from my family's garden and placed them in community fridges alongside the bike roads in Malden but there are many times my produce would go bad before anyone has taken them. I understand what it is like to feel like I don't know enough ways to help my community and I can sympathize with people who feel like they don't know where and when to get help. I want to help people know when they can receive help and when they can offer help!	None, just excited to join!	I have never been part of JumboCode	Thorongil	
	She/Her/Hers	2027	Yes	I want to join JumboCode because I am looking for a way to improve my programming skills while contributing to a greater purpose. As a Computer Science major hoping to work in the tech industry in the future, it’s my responsibility to understand the ethical issues surrounding technology and tech businesses and to use my skills for a cause that uplifts communities. When I was in high school, I attended a program that taught high schoolers how to build an AI program, and the program directors included an ethics lesson in the curriculum. I learned about how AI data from systemic and historical biases  can affect the results the AI produces, which can be catastrophic when considering the choices that an automated AI program makes for a company, such as new employee hiring. Through JumboCode, I’m hoping to contribute to a project that I know will be used to benefit a community.\n\nI am also hoping to learn from my project team and have the opportunity to work on new tools. Working with a team has taught me valuable organizational, syntactic, and algorithmic skills that I either didn’t learn or didn’t understand well from teachers. My CS-15 GERP partner really helped me understand how our choices would affect the runtime of our program. If I hadn’t done the project with her before our final exam, I might have totally fumbled the exam. I’ve greatly enjoyed team programming in the past, so I’d love to have the opportunity to learn, at JumboCode, from a bigger team with more varied levels of experience than me.	At Tufts, I'm in the Giving Camp Club, where I volunteer to organize and participate in events for disabled and neurodivergent adults. Last year, we organized a Davis Square bowling event, Thanksgiving crafts session, Special Olympics, and info sessions about disabilities and neurodivergency for community education. The Giving Camp Club members are usually paired with a “camper” who attends our events, and there is one non-verbal camper who I’ve been pairing up with since I first volunteered with Giving Camp during my pre-orientation. We have formed a special bond doing crafts and taking walks together! We've worked together to find a common mode of communication.\n\nI'm also the current logistics chair for Women in Computer Science (WiCS). My responsibilities include managing and updating the WiCS website and reserving rooms for meetings and social and professional events. I also contribute to planning events for WiCS to help female and non-binary Computer Science students at Tufts form a community and prepare for their careers. Through WiCS last year, I was able to meet so many other women who were in my CS classes and helped me out when I needed it, so I’m excited to contribute to the community that was so welcoming to me last year.\n\nIn high school, I co-founded a New Jersey chapter of SmashHigh, a tutoring organization that provides free tutoring to under-privileged school districts. I recruited and organized over a dozen tutors from my and surrounding high schools and worked with a local school district to match tutors with students. I tutored two elementary students twice a week. Though I was supposed to be teaching them, they also taught me a lot too. One of my kids was always trying to teach me Spanish to get out of doing the work I was assigning her, so we compromised and she taught me Spanish for the first 10 minutes of the lesson, and I taught her math and English for the rest of our time. My other kid loved Roblox and computers, and I always wanted to talk to me about it because he knew I was studying computers at school. He taught me so many Roblox and video game hacks and then created Kahoots to test me on them when we were done with our lessons. Before tutoring, I had always heard teachers talk about engaging students in lessons, but I only truly learned how important that was when I started teaching kids myself.\n	Yes	Yes	I am available for the all-club meeting on Sundays, and I am also available all mornings before 10:30 AM on Mondays and Wednesdays and before 12:00 PM on Tuesdays and Thursdays, Monday and Wednesday afternoons, Thursday evenings, all day on Fridays and Saturdays, and before 5:00 PM on Sundays.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	My first introduction to Computer Science was at my high school, which was a vocational technical school for Information Technology and Business. Every year, we had one required vocational course dedicated to computer science topics. Even though my Computer Science classes’ homework always took me the longest amount of time, I grew to love them as I watched my programs turn from a mess of bugs to a working program.	During my senior year of high school, I participated in a team of three as a developer for a video game project that we submitted to the Future Business Leaders of America state competition. The prompt that year was a "word-based" video game, so my team and I first discussed what kind of game and what the game mechanics would look like. We came up with an idea that amalgamated a lot of ideas from previous games we had all played. Our game's premise ended up being that a player would collect letters as they appeared and moved on a track in front of the player. Then, the player would have to spell words with those letters they collected to earn points. For the project, our one teammate created original art for the game graphics and assets. My other teammate and I used the Godot game engine to organize the game into scenes and to implement the game mechanics. \n\nThe first challenge I faced while participating in this project is that I had never coded a video game before or used Godot. There was definitely a learning curve for me while creating the game. I started out just adding background music to levels, then moved on to placing buttons and creating new scenes, and then eventually moved on to creating and editing algorithms for the game. When we presented the project at the Future Business Leaders of America NJ State Leadership Conference, we won first place for the project and moved on to the National competition! It felt so surreal, realizing that we had taken our project from ideas and thoughts on a page to a playable product that won a competition.\n\nThough we didn't win first place at Nationals, I've taken the skills I learned from this project, namely development processes and team programming, and applied them to my college Computer Science classes.	https://github.com/Gooey64/fbla-babey	10	9	12	4	7	6	5	3	2	8	1	11	My first choice is A2Empowerment because I strongly believe that education empowers people to achieve their goals in life, and that everyone deserves the chance to do so. My grandmother was uneducated in China, but she moved to the United States and worked hard so that my mother could have an education at an American college. My mother works hard so that my brother and I can focus on our studies without worrying about our family’s finances. As a woman who has had the privilege to learn in an academic setting and choose my own career path, I care deeply about the rights of other women to have the tools and opportunities for education. I also feel that my background as a tutor, through which I was always sending feedback to and receiving evaluations from my students and their parents, will allow me to contribute to the user experience of the web app that A2Empowerment is developing.\n\nEnglish At Large is my second choice because I have seen how difficult it is for immigrants to learn the English language on their own. Spanish was the first language for both the students I tutored in high school, and English was their second language. None of their parents spoke English, and I communicated with them with my limited Spanish skills. After every lesson, I emailed my students’ parents a report of what we reviewed that day, the students’ progress, and any homework I assigned. The parents were always grateful to hear that I was assigning them English work, since the kids were in a school that taught only half the classes in English and the other half in Spanish. Sometimes my kids would ask me questions about English, and, as a native English speaker, it would be difficult for me to explain the answer to them. I wish I had access to a library like English At Large when I was teaching them, so I would love the opportunity to contribute to this project! I also think my background as a tutor will allow me to contribute more meaningfully to English At Large’s catalog system because I will understand the program as both a developer and a user.	\N	\N	Cirion	
