dside stories for children whose parents are far away from home to help bring warmth and comfort. \nThese experiences have made me a better communicator and collaborator, I have built deep connections with children whom I have learned from and contributed in a meaningful way to my community. 	Yes	Yes	I am available Mondays, Wednesdays, and Thursdays after 5pm; weekends 9am-2pm (flexible).	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++	CS11! I originally was a social sciences student with no interest in computer science. Hence, I went into the course anxious about not being able to keep up due to my inexperience. Yet when I managed to get my first program to compile and output silly messages based on the results of computation, it brought me an immense feeling of joy and excitement. Constructing a program using the most fundamental algorithms to enable a machine operations beyond a human’s ability is beautifully logical. I also became interested in the connection between a human mind and a machine’s intelligence. Through computer science, I get to blend creativity with logic while bridging my understanding of human cognition.	This summer, while scratching my head trying to figure out what my schedule would look like, I decided to implement a topological sorting program to help me plan out my academic career. This was my first ever personal project; I still remember the thrill of completing it, suddenly realizing that I have a range of programming toolkits at my disposal to assist me through hardship. \nI went into the planning stage only knowing the general features of a topological sort algorithm. One of the first struggles I encountered was how to establish the graph – since to the human eye, nodes with pointers was the best representation, I attempted to use a complicated linked list. This soon failed, as this approach was messy and the program had to cluelessly jump between nodes trying to find the starting point. After some research I chose an adjacency matrix holding Boolean values as the final representation. Another difficulty was finding a data structure to help the computer decide which node to go next in the sort, and queues was the best solution. \nDuring the testing phase, I had accidentally put in a cyclical dependency in my graph, causing me infinite agony. This experience taught me the importance of using a debugger and tracing through code by setting stop points. Overall, this project had gave me a boost in confidence and desire to create more practical programs. I became familiar with a branch of algorithms I originally had no experience with and inspired me to continue exploring other programs (I am currently trying to implement a pet quokka class).	\N	1	10	4	3	2	6	9	12	11	7	8	5	I am most interested in projects that allows me to get to know and engage the Medford and Greater Boston community (West Medford Community Center, Somerville Museum). My second choice is Dillar Academy because I have been to Kashgar and deeply want to assist in their lives. I am also interested in supporting fellow university students in any mental and emotional hardships they may encounter. Further, as someone with an interest in the social sciences, I am happy to work in projects related to the justice system or counseling. 	\N	\N	Fram	
