earn to navigate Figma, which was new to me, but ultimately, I managed to create a design that resonated with users.\n\nThis project excited me because it combined problem-solving with user-centered design, and I gained valuable experience working in a team and adapting to new tools and challenges.	\N	11	6	7	12	3	4	8	1	9	5	2	10	My top choice is LCS Tutoring because I’m passionate about education and want to support students and the learning community through technology. A2Empowerment is my second choice, as its mission to empower women through education resonates with me, and I’m excited to streamline processes for their scholarship programs. Dillar Academy is my third choice, where I see an opportunity to enhance educational access. New England Innocence Project, my fourth choice, aligns with my interest in criminal justice reform, and I’m eager to support their work in preventing wrongful convictions. Finally, Bread & Roses is my fifth choice because I’m committed to addressing food insecurity and look forward to contributing to their volunteer scheduling system.	I would like to add that I am currently studying data science, which equips me with a strong analytical background that I’m excited to apply to real-world projects. My experiences working with nonprofits like Front Porch and my involvement as a Tisch Scholar have deepened my commitment to using technology for social good. I’m eager to contribute my skills to JumboCode, where I can continue learning, collaborating, and making a positive impact. I believe my combination of technical skills, community engagement, and passion for meaningful work makes me a great fit for this opportunity.	No	Léod	
	she/her	2027	Yes	I hope to join JumboCode because it would be an amazing opportunity to apply my coding skills and learn more about web development while gaining a community and supporting a good cause. What stood out to me at the GIM was how excited all of the project managers, tech leads, designers, and executive board members were to introduce JumboCode and the organizations they would be helping. Not only that, but every single project mentioned how they were looking forward to bonding with their team members and would answer any questions we had, whether it be about the project itself or homework for the classes that they were TAs for. \n\nDeveloping my CS community was a goal of mine coming into my sophomore year, and seeing how determined the PMs, TLs, and designers were to build a family as much as create web-apps that will help non-profits really resonated with me. In fact, Tufts' value of collaboration over competition was one of my main reasons for choosing Tufts. I would love to join the JumboCode family and help to foster that supportive culture.\n\nAdditionally, I'm excited by the prospect of giving back to the local community through something that I'm passionate about--computer science! I would learn so much about working with a team, an extremely important career skill, and about the paths I could take within the CS field from all the mentors I would gain as a JumboCode developer. From there, I could take what was given to me and pay it forward to the community. Community service has always been a value of mine, and it would be incredibly meaningful to be able to make an impact through coding.\n\nI've heard nothing but praise about JumboCode from past members, and I would be and honor to join the organization.	Throughout middle school and high school, I was a part of the National Charity League with my mom, which brought together mothers and daughters to practice philanthropy. My mom and I would volunteer at non-profits based in mine and neighboring towns. This would range from driving meals to locals who were unable to leave their homes for Meals on Wheels to one of my favorites, manning the pretzel stand at the Oktoberfest event held by Founders Hall, an education and recreation center for seniors in my town (many of whom were drunk).\n\nMy senior year, I was also the secretary for my high school's National Honor Society chapter, and helped organize events such as a Red Cross blood drive and food drives for local pantries. But my favorite event we did by far was our "Senior" Senior Prom at Ridgefield Crossings, a senior living home in my town. We had live music, food, garden decor, and of course, lots of dancing.\n\nIn high school I was a member of the Youth Against Cancer Society (YACS). We mainly volunteered at Ann's Place, an organization a short walk from my house that provides services for cancer patients and their families such as counseling, yoga, and kitten therapy. We would often help them clean up and garden, raking leaves in the fall and weeding their gardens in the spring. 	Yes	Yes	Monday 7:30-9:30pm | Tuesday 3-10pm | Wednesday 7:30-9:30pm | Thursday 9am-12pm 4:30-8 pm | Friday 1:30-9pm | Saturday 9am-10pm | Sunday 9am-1pm 4-6pm 8-10pm	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, Python	Growing up, I dabbled in Codeacademy, but my first real introduction to CS was AP CS my senior year of high school.	Last semester, for my ES2 final project we were prompted to choose any topic and a dataset(s) about that topic, then analyze and visualize it. For this project, I utilized the Python libraries pandas, NumPy, and Matplotlib for data manipulation and visualization. \n\nFirst, I chose my topic and datasets. My focus was healthcare accessibility across racial groups, specifically in my lovely home state of Connecticut. I compiled demographic and health-care related data from the Connecticut Health Care Survey and the American Community Survey from 2012-2013, and added all the information I was going to use in a dataframe, which was divided into separate dataframes by race. Then, I isolated and calculated key metrics, such as the percentage of individuals unable to afford prescription medications, and visualized these alongside median income estimates by race using grouped bar charts. I repeated this process to visualize the percent of people who did not receive health care due to cost worries by race. \n\nA challenge that I faced was assessing bias in my analysis. My analysis could be biased in the sense that starting out, I had expectations for what results I would find. This could have caused confirmation bias by influencing which information and questions I chose to include in the analysis. Perhaps if I decided to investigate responses to different questions from the Connecticut Health Care Survey, I would have found my predictions proven wrong. In part to prevent confirmation bias and in part to ensure that there was enough information to draw valid conclusions from, I chose which information from the survey to explore by looking at whether there were adequate responses to the question. To further prevent this I could have randomly chosen which questions I looked into. \n\nAfter my analysis I concluded that race and median income does play a role in the disparity within health care accessibility. For example, race groups with a higher income had a lower percentage of individuals unable to afford prescription medications. Overall, it was really interesting to focus on something that I consider important and in my home state. I really enjoyed creating something mostly of my own accord without strict requirements, like those characterizing many of the projects we do in CS classes.	\N	11	9	4	2	6	10	8	3	1	7	5	12	1. I really connected with the English at Large mission. As a daughter of two first-generation immigrants from China who came to the U.S. knowing only a few words of English, I've heard stories about the struggle to assimilate and build 