solidified my passion for teaching and helping others grow, especially those who lack access to quality resources.\n\nThoroughly enjoying this work, I spent a summer in high school teaching elementary students English and basic computer skills, and later joined my school’s Social Service League, where I taught Math to middle and high school students at an underprivileged school every Saturday for a year. These experiences not only left me with a deep sense of fulfillment but also sparked a lasting passion for education and mentorship.\n\nTherefore, I am drawn to projects like "LCS Tutoring" at JumboCode. They align with my commitment to education and provide an opportunity to use technology to create meaningful change. I am excited to bring together my teaching experience and technical skills to work on projects that uplift and impact the community in positive ways.\n	Yes	Yes	Available at any time as long as it doesn't clash with my classes!	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	Python	I took computer science as a subject in high school! It was introductory level Python. 	During my internship at PwC, I worked on a project that I’m particularly proud of—automating the Black-Scholes model and its associated volatility parameter for option pricing using Python. Previously, the process relied on manual Excel work, but my automation reduced analysis time by 40%. By web scraping historical stock data from the National Stock Exchange, I built a model that calculated volatility and Black-Scholes values for user-defined companies.\n\nThis experience was particularly exciting for me as I learned new skills at every step and discovered a deep interest in coding.	\N	7	8	11	2	6	9	10	1	5	3	4	12	LCS tutoring particularly aligns with a non-profit cause I'm very passionate about. However, I'm happy working on any of the projects!	-	Not been a part of JumboCode	Haleth	
	she/her	2028	Yes	I really liked JumboCode's dedication to helping the community both local and global in return. I was worried that taking up a major in CS would force me to take up a job that could not give back to the community, but I was happy to see a tech club at Tufts that focuses on the community by utilizing tech skills. I also was happy to see the gender diversity of this club as there were some entirely female-led projects. I'm not exactly sure which area of CS I would like pursue, so I hope this club can provide me with opportunities to see what software engineering is like and learning how to use different tools used in the industry. I also appreciate how each project has some sort of bonding activities, including the chance to meet with the client and see progress, making the task more meaningful at hand. 	In high school I did a lot of volunteering events, such as tutoring students, managing eco initiatives at my school, to creating a fundraiser which took people's old shoes and converted them into money for third-world countries by collaborating with a non-profit. We ended up collecting about 170 pairs of shoes and $1,500 to donate. I even volunteered this summer to help out some biologists compile images of a Petri dish into a Timelapse video of a bacteria growing with the use of programming (python)! I also did a community-service based pre-orientation program (FOCUS) which familiarized me with the social/community issues of the Medford/Somerville area.	Yes	Yes	Monday: After 6 pm\nTuesday: After 12 pm\nWednesday: After 6 pm\nThursday: 12 pm - 5:30 pm\nFriday: After 3 pm\nSaturday: Not available \nSunday: After 6 pm	\N	Git/GitHub, Python	I took a robotics class in 9th grade, however since I was doing remote-learning (covid), I ended up learning the basics of Python. I wanted to continue learning this, so I self-taught more of the basics of python by watching tutorials through freecodecaamp.org on YouTube and geeksforgeeks.org. I then took AP Computer Science Principles in 12th grade as it was the only CS course offered in my high school.	This summer, I assisted biologists at Tufts in creating time-lapse videos of bacterial growth on Petri dishes with the help of a computer scientist. A scanner was used to capture images of six dishes over time, but I needed to segment each image so that the videos showed just one dish in its place as the bacteria grew. To do this, I developed a program using OpenCV to detect circles, remove the background outside the Petri dish, rotate the dishes, and organize the frames. The main challenge was the sensitivity of the circle detection, which identified too many false circles, so I researched image processing techniques and adjusted the parameters to improve accuracy until I got one circle.	My GitHub: https://github.com/SPal28   (My bacteria growing project is not included in the GitHub because the research it was used in is still confidential, if you would like to see it, I can request for it to be shown)	2	9	4	8	12	6	7	3	11	1	5	10	Some of my top choices were those that supported non-english speakers with their journey. Their messages really resonated with me as my mother was an immigrant and needed my help with learning English as well. I also appreciated how I could gain advice on CS classes and internships from groups like Dillar Academy and Tufts General Counsel. I also tried to select projects that had some tech stack that I was familiar with, especially with Tufts General Counsel as they were the only ones who used Python. I also really appreciated Clarence's reassurance that the group will care about others and offer life advice because I think feeling acknowledged and comfortable with others is the best way to make close connections with your team and being successful. 	\N	No	Halbarad	
“super willing to learn” -Lillian Tran	she/her	2028	Yes	I want to join JumboCode because I want to work on an impactful project with a passionate team. I feel like most of the programming I have done has been for myself, so I want to be able to use my skills to make a positive impact on people's lives, something that can be hard to do on my own. I also hope to meet of lot of new people and make good friends, both within the team and the client. I also hope to gain valuable experience working with a team on a real-world project and take advantage of the pre-professional opportunities. Furthermore, I hope to learn a lot about software development and project design in a welcoming environment, as I have found it a bit hard to learn it on my own.	I volunteered with a community fridge for a year (weekly during summer and the school year). We would collect food that our partner stores were planning to throw out (food that was near the best buy date) and distribute it to elderly residents of a building, or just anyone in the area who needed it. We would put the remaining in a community fridge that anyone could take out of. Overall, although it could be frustrating when something went wrong, it was a very rewarding experience and I met a lot of nice people.	Yes	Yes	I am available all days after 8pm except for Tuesdays and Thursdays. On Tuesdays and Thursdays I am free after 5pm (to 9pm). On Fridays I am free after 6pm. On weekends, I am free most of the day.	\N	HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python	My dad introduced me to Scratch when I was in elementary school, and I experimented with it for a little.	In my junior year, I worked on a task-management web app using Express and HTML/CSS with two of my friends and a mentor in a program that my school hosted. The final project had a user login and allowed the user to create tasks they could check off and add a due date and priority for. We all started off with very little coding experience, so we had to learn a lot about how to design a project that we could feasibly make. Although our mentor helped us a little, we had to do most of the coding on our own, which was quite difficult and included a lot of googling. We also had trouble scheduling meetings and sticking to due dates, since we were all busy and underestimated how much time it would take us, but we were able to adapt by the end. Overall, although we made a smaller project than some of the other teams, we were still proud of what we did and learned a lot.	\N	4	2	11	10	1	8	9	5	6	3	7	12	My first choice is Dillar Academy because I believe their mission is very important, the persecution of Uyghurs has not received the proper attention, so I hope to help them achieve it. Additionally, educational disparity among ethnic minorities is a big issue, especially where I am from, so I also want to work on this project to help combat that issue. (The schools I have gone to have chosen to ignore it). Also I have always had bad experiences with class-management web apps, so I would like to see if its possible to make a better one.\n\nMy second choice is Village Hub Food because the group I volunteered with faced a similar problem with not being able to track when volunteers were doing pickups and what food was in the fridge, which caused a lot of confusion and sometimes resulted in people not being able to receive the food they needed. I hope that by working on this project I will be able to prevent another organization from experiencing this problem, and allow everyone who needs food to get it.\n\nMy third choice is Bread and Roses because it's clear from the number of meals served and supplies given that they have been making a big impact on the community, so I hope that by working with them, they will be able to expand even further. The scheduling web-app is also similar to the project I worked on in high school, so I hope I can improve on what I did before with this project.\n\nMy fourth choice is West Medford Community Center because we used a similar web-app to upload and store issues while I worked on my school's newspaper, and while I thought it was really cool, I was never able to figure out how it was created. By working on this project I would be able to make something similar, which would be cool. The newspaper was also an important way I was able to connect with other people and be aware of what was happening at my school, so I would like to facilitate the creation of that in a different community through this project.	\N	\N	Arador	
“I know her from a separate club - she's so sweet and hard-working!!” -Stephanie Kim	She/Her	2027	Yes	The most important reason I want to join JumboCode is that I feel genuinely passionate about the mission and personally excited about the work required to carry out that mission. I want to use CS, something I am lucky enough to be trained to do, for the purpose of helping people or bettering their lives in some way. Having a good website is so important to making a business or project look professional. If users get the wrong message, are confused about what services are offered, or whether they are the right fit for those services, the organization loses credibility and, in some cases, the ability to help all the people they could have.\n\nAdditionally, I learned the core concepts of some web development frameworks/technologies during my summer internship and I was inspired to get more involved with projects that push me out of the “college-class coding bubble” and into working with more real-world tools.\n\nI am also interested in getting more involved with the Medford/Somerville/Boston community. I am not from the area and I think this would be a great opportunity to become more engaged in the community here.	My most recent experience working with non-profits/doing mission-driven work was this summer, working as a TA at a full-stack software development bootcamp that teaches veterans transitioning out of the military to code. Additionally, I am volunteering as the outreach chair for the Tufts Women in Tech conference because I am passionate about cultivating more diversity within the tech field. I am also a TA for CS10 at Tufts where I care a lot about making people feel confident and excited about coding. \n\nI have done other non-tech related work in the past such as coordinating a school supply drive, volunteering with an organization that houses and prepares meals for families experiencing homelessness, and tutoring kids who didn’t have access to academic support in an underserved area of Chicago.	Yes	Yes	If I were to get this role it would be a priority for me so I could make changes to some things if needed. I don’t have class until 3:00pm on Mondays and Wednesdays so I have large blocks of time then. I’m also available most evenings and almost any time on Saturday and Sunday.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Python, Django, PostgreSQL, Flask, Vite, Tailwind, Bootstrap	I’ve always been interested in technology, but I started coding in middle school with things like “Hour of Code” etc. In 8th grade (and then 9th/10th) I took a coding class and have been hooked since!	Two summers ago, I worked as a barista at a local cafe and saw firsthand the kind of problems that having a bad website could cause. People came in confused about an outdated menu and an obsolete online ordering system. I offered to remake the website for them and help them transition to Squarespace. I worked on the project for about a month, photographing the space, composing the layout, and writing/editing copy. I also got the experience of working with the cafe owner to create a website that best met her objectives and could help her business grow.\n\nOne of the challenges I faced was working with the cafe managers who were often slow to provide me the info I needed to move forward (new menu, rental info, etc.). This was frustrating at times but I was able to work with them to make sure I ended up getting all the info I needed to make their website as good as possible. I’m proud of the work I did for them and that it has made a noticeable impact on the business.\n\nHere is the link to the Squarespace: https://www.tagscafeevanston.com/	\N	7	9	12	3	8	2	4	1	6	5	10	11	All of the projects look wonderful and I would feel motivated and excited to work on any of them. \n\nI would especially love to be part of the LCS tutoring project. One of the reasons I was drawn to this project was because I think it could benefit substantially from having a clear and professional online presence/registration process. With something like LCS tutoring, it’s so important for potential clients (likely the parents of potential tutees) to feel comfortable and confident that their kids would be involved with something safe and well-managed. A new, professional matching system would be a great way to strengthen trust in the organization. \n\nI am also compelled by the mission of pairing college students with local children. It’s a mutually beneficial relationship where kids who may not otherwise get academic support, are able to get the help of older, more experienced students while the tutors get the opportunity to hone their leadership and communication skills. \n\nAdditionally, the new system would take pressure off those who had to manually manage matching in the past, opening up opportunities for those people to help the community in new ways and allowing the organization to operate at a greater scale.	I would be really excited to be a part of JumboCode!!	No	Arathorn II	
