“such a sweet soul and has been wanting to be in jumbocode for very long, she learns well and will be super passionate about the project!!!” -Sristi Panchu	she/her	2027	Yes	As someone deeply passionate about both technological innovation and community service, I find the opportunity to combine the two incredibly exciting. JumboCode provides the perfect platform to merge these interests—using technology to create meaningful solutions to real-world problems while contributing to the greater good. \nMy journey in computer science began when I took CS11. From the very start, I was struck by the strong sense of community I found in the CS department. I found myself going to office hours regularly, not only to ask questions but because I genuinely enjoyed the collaborative environment. I appreciated how students supported each other, whether by solving tough problems together or explaining tricky concepts. That spirit of teamwork and shared learning is something I have come to value deeply, and it is what I am confident that JumboCode offers. \nWhat excites me even more about JumboCode is the opportunity to apply my skills in a real-world setting while working on impactful projects. As a sophomore, I am eager to gain hands-on experience, simulating the dynamics of a professional development team. This is a chance to not only grow technically as a developer but also to learn how to effectively collaborate on a product that has tangible outcomes.\nBeyond the technical work, I’m excited to get to know my teammates on a personal level. I believe that a team’s success isn’t just about the code we write but about the relationships we build along the way. There is so much to learn from each other’s experiences, and I am looking forward to sharing my own perspectives while gaining new insights from other team members. Working with like-minded people who are equally passionate about using technology to make a positive impact is something that I find both inspiring and motivating.	CS11 Teaching Assistant: The most rewarding part of being a CS11 TA is being able to support students as they begin their CS journeys. It's fulfilling to help them navigate challenges I once faced and see their progress. Being a TA also reminds me not to doubt myself—I went from having no programming experience to doing very well in the class to TAing it in a relatively short time. Seeing current CS11 students’ growth helps me reflect on my journey as well!\n\nStAAR Center, Peer Tutor: As a peer tutor at Tufts, I currently tutor CS11, CS15, Calc 1, and Arabic. Back in high school, I also tutored math and French. Tutoring has always been a rewarding way for me to share my knowledge and help others succeed in their academic journeys.\n\nAILEM App, Volunteer: In high school, I collaborated with a group of students to develop an app called AILEM, which aims to help refugees and asylum seekers learn the languages of the countries they are seeking asylum in and integrate into their new societies. I wasn’t a developer at the time, as I didn’t have programming experience. I contributed by designing English and French language curriculums by providing translations to Arabic. \n\nTufts Arab Student Association (ASA), Event Coordinator\n• Coordinated diverse cultural events welcoming both Arab students and those eager to explore the culture.\n\nUWC Atlantic's Peace Council, Chair\n• Organized school-wide events: notable events include TEDxYouth@UWCAtlantic, themed The Day After Tomorrow, which included themes about Future Technological Developments.\n• Planned fundraisers, raising £1700+ for earthquake relief in Syria and Türkiye, and £600+ for combating period poverty in Bangladesh.\n\nSyria Trust for Development, Volunteer\n• Organized national debate tournaments for teams representing different states in the country.\n• Drafted debate curricula for Syrian students and mentored younger debaters.	Yes	Yes	I'm pretty flexible generally and happy to discuss availabilities, but I am free on Wednesday and Thursday at any point after 1:30pm!	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, I have some experience with Java as I'm currently taking CS86 (OOP for Graphical User Interface)	My first introduction to computer science was in CS11. Before that, I had no formal coding experience, just the occasional curiosity-driven google searches about programming but never actually writing a program. I initially came to Tufts planning to major only in Cognitive and Brain Sciences (CBS) and hadn’t seriously considered pursuing CS. I have always enjoyed math, and I figured CS would have a similar problem-solving appeal (side note: I know some people who LOVE math but HATE CS so I know that this does not apply to everyone, but in my case it worked out). To my surprise, I found that I truly enjoyed the problem-solving aspect of computer science, much more than I expected. The logical, structured approach to tackling complex problems resonated with me, and I quickly became drawn to the subject. What started as a required course ended up sparking a new passion, and now I am double-majoring in both CBS and CS!	One project I’m particularly proud of is Gerp, a utility designed for searching text files within a directory hierarchy, much like the "grep" command in Linux. Gerp reads all files in a specified directory recursively and stores their contents in a hash table for efficient searching. \n\nI worked on the project through pair programming. It was also my first time programming with a partner, which turned out to be a really enjoyable experience. Pair programming gave us the chance to learn from each other’s different ways of thinking. It wasn’t just about getting through problems faster—it was about exploring different approaches and fine-tuning our solutions together. It made the whole process feel more engaging and rewarding, and it definitely improved the quality of the final product.\n\nOne of the hardest parts of building Gerp was planning for efficiency, especially given the scale of the project. Since Gerp had to recursively read through large directories of text files, it was crucial to consider the time complexity of the algorithm. Managing the algorithm's efficiency was essential to ensure that the program could scale well and handle large datasets without crashing or suffering from poor performance.\n\nAs with any large-scale project, debugging presented its own set of challenges. Despite our extensive planning, we encountered persistent bugs, especially when dealing with edge cases in larger directories. To address these issues, along with unit testing, we also created custom input files to thoroughly test edge cases, ensuring Gerp’s functionality across different scenarios. Additionally, we used Valgrind to detect memory leaks and other potential issues, ensuring that the program ran efficiently and reliably without memory mismanagement.\n\nOverall, working on Gerp was a deeply rewarding experience that strengthened both my technical skills and my ability to collaborate effectively on complex projects.	\N	5	1	10	11	2	3	12	7	4	9	6	8	1 Village Food Hub: Having attended a United World College, where our mission emphasized promoting a sustainable future, I developed a strong commitment to reducing food waste. This aligns perfectly with my desire to work on a web app that tracks the Village Food Hub’s pantry inventory. I am excited about the opportunity to contribute to a project that directly supports community needs while fostering sustainability, reflecting values I deeply resonate with!\n\n2 Dillar Academy: I have experience working on developing an app (called AILEM) aimed at helping refugees and asylum seekers learn the language of the countries they seek asylum in. This has given me a deep understanding of the challenges faced by marginalized communities in language acquisition. I care passionately about these issues and believe I have valuable ideas to contribute to an app designed to help Uyghurs learn English!\n\n3 New England Innocence Project: Ethics in the criminal justice system has always been a topic I have reflected on and been deeply interested in exploring. I feel strongly about preventing wrongful convictions and ensuring justice within criminal justice system. I am eager to further investigate ways to improve the system and contribute to meaningful reforms!	\N	\N	Angbor	
“DO IT!!! LITERALLY DOOOO ITTTT SO SO SO MOTIVATED AND AWESOME” -Sristi Panchu	She/her	2027	Yes	My main source of motivation is inspiration, and the idea of working on projects that provide technical solutions while supporting long term structural changes for nonprofits is truly inspiring to me. I appreciate JumboCode’s focus on redistributing resources and knowledge from the Tufts community to benefit organizations that work to improve social and civic conditions.\nBy joining JumboCode, I hope to gain a deeper understanding of how technology can be applied in real-world situations to create meaningful and sustainable change. I am eager to learn how to balance the technical complexity of projects with the unique needs of nonprofits, ensuring that the solutions we create are scalable and practical for them to maintain. Collaborating with a group of peers that possess a similar enthusiasm for community focused innovation fascinates me because I think that diverse teams that work together produce better, more innovative solutions.\nAdditionally, I see this as an opportunity to improve my problem solving skills in real world contexts and to grow into a developer who writes code and designs systems that have lasting, positive effects on society. The prospect of contributing to the creation of technology that empowers organizations to expand their impact aligns perfectly with my aspirations to work at the intersection of technology and social good in the future.\n	- Intern at a non-profit healthcare solutions company in Jordan: Worked on electronic healthcare solutions for nationwide services, contributing to QA, integration, pharmaceutical, and help desk departments.\n- President of the Jordan Model Parliament Conference forum: Led external affairs and promoted civic engagement, specifically the Jordanian National Commission for Women forum.\n- Co-founder of a dance club and leader of a medical club: Engaged the student body in health-related and cultural activities.\n- 