	he/him	2028	No	I am excited about the opportunity to join JumboCode, as it perfectly combines my passion for technology with my desire to make a positive impact on the community. The initiative to develop pro-bono web and mobile applications for nonprofit organizations really appeals to me, as I believe technology should be used to help others. Collaborating with like-minded peers on meaningful projects, especially those that support educational and community organizations, truly inspires me.\n\nThrough my previous projects, such as developing predictive models for air pollution dispersion and estimating electrical power requirements for AI adoption, I've realized how fulfilling it is to apply technical skills to real-world challenges. Joining JumboCode will not only allow me to continue enhancing my programming abilities in Python and Java but also give me the chance to explore new technologies like MongoDB and FastAPI, which I’m eager to learn more about.\n\nAdditionally, the collaborative atmosphere at JumboCode excites me. Working in teams that include project managers, tech leads, designers, and developers offers a dynamic environment where I can grow both technically and personally. This setting aligns perfectly with my past experiences leading teams in the International Young Physicist Tournament and the New Zealand Physics Olympiad, where I learned the importance of teamwork and clear communication.\n\nBy becoming a part of JumboCode, I hope to improve my ability to turn technical concepts into practical solutions that genuinely help people. I look forward to growing as both a team player and a leader, learning from others, and contributing my skills to meaningful projects. I believe this experience will be invaluable for my future in computer engineering and will give me a richer understanding of how technology can support and empower nonprofit initiatives.\n	My involvement in volunteering and community engagement has been both diverse and fulfilling. At my high school back in New Zealand, I served as a prefect, leading the Environmental and Wellness Committees. I organized events such as Pride Month, International Women’s Day, and Waste-Free Lunch Week to foster inclusivity, raise environmental awareness, and support student well-being. As President of the Rotary Interact Club, I expanded our community reach by organizing collaborative events that encouraged civic engagement and strengthened our connection with local organizations.\n\nI also took on a leadership role in Physics Pathfinders, a student-run group focused on providing physics and engineering workshops for young New Zealanders due to the lacking educational system. As an organizer and speaker, I coordinated over 19 workshops attended by more than 460 students, supported by a dedicated team of over 40 volunteers. This initiative aimed to make STEM education more accessible and engaging for students across the country.\n\nAdditionally, as Head Student Librarian, I launched a reading program to promote student engagement in literature, furthering my commitment to education and community enrichment. These experiences have deepened my belief in the transformative power of community-driven initiatives and the positive impact that dedicated volunteer work can have on society.\n	Yes	Yes	I am free anytime in the weekends but not as free in the evenings for weekdays.	Cambridge A-Level Computer Science	JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python	I was introduced to coding from my dad when I was 7 and I’ve been coding since.	I'd like to share a project I worked on during my high school years in New Zealand. I was involved with Physics Pathfinders, a student-run organization we started to make STEM education more accessible and enjoyable for young people.\n\nThe project began when some friends and I noticed that many younger students, especially those from underrepresented communities, lacked engaging STEM resources. We decided to create free workshops that combined theoretical concepts with hands-on experiments.\n\nAs a key organizer and speaker, I coordinated various aspects of the project—developing content, managing logistics, and reaching out to schools and community centers. We created interactive modules using everyday materials to explain principles like Newton’s laws and basic electronics. I remember how excited students were when they built simple circuits or understood a physics concept for the first time.\n\nOne of the challenges was tailoring the workshops to suit different age groups and levels of understanding. We adapted our teaching methods based on feedback to make the content both informative and engaging. This involved simplifying explanations, using visual aids, and adding hands-on activities that interested the students.\n\nSeeing the students understand and enjoy the material was very rewarding. It reinforced my belief in the importance of education and using our skills to help others.\n\nThis project strengthened my communication and leadership abilities. It showed me how passion and teamwork can create positive change. Being part of Physics Pathfinders was a valuable experience, and it deepened my commitment to making a positive impact in my community through STEM education.	\N	4	3	5	7	9	1	12	2	11	10	8	6	I am passionate about volunteering on projects where I can make a meaningful difference. I want to dedicate my time and skills to help organizations streamline their operations and enhance their services, ultimately creating a positive impact on society. I'm especially drawn to initiatives that tackle social challenges through innovative solutions, as I believe technology and efficient systems can greatly benefit the communities we serve. My goal is to support causes that promote equity and accessibility, and I'm eager to contribute my expertise to advance these important efforts.	\N	\N	Brego	
