e date that the original dataset was collected; my function then locates the correct dataset (in an AWS S3 Bucket), creates a subset, and puts the subset in a separate S3 bucket. I faced a few technical challenges while working on the project. I had issues with uploading libraries to the lambda function as a layer because the layer did not have enough storage, so I had to recreate the same functionality without using certain libraries. 	\N	6	4	7	3	5	11	12	10	1	8	2	9	It was very difficult for me to rank all the choices since all of the projects sound like a lot of fun! I had a lot of trouble deciding between English At Large and A2Empowerment because I’m really interested in the work both organizations do. I’d be especially excited to get involved with either of these projects! 	\N	\N	Aldor	
	She/Her	2026	Yes	Having been part of JumboCode last year, I experienced firsthand the incredible community of developers and collaborators who share a passion for both technology and positive social impact. My time on the team not only allowed me to improve my technical skills but also introduced me to a group of individuals who were as dedicated, funny, and unique as they were talented. I vividly remember a bonding event where we played Catan—what made it so memorable wasn't just the game itself but how playing with fellow computer science majors turned it into a strategic, probability-based challenge. Experiences like these, both in and out of development, deepened my connection to my teammates and reinforced my belief that collaborative work can be both productive and immensely fun.\n\nJumboCode has shown me how powerful technology can be when applied thoughtfully to meet the needs of local organizations. Last year, I saw how our efforts had a tangible impact, and that gave me a sense of fulfillment that goes beyond personal skill development. This year, I’m excited to build upon the skills I developed while continuing to collaborate with my peers and community partners.\n\nWhat drives me to reapply is the unique combination of honing technical skills and creating long-lasting, positive changes for local nonprofits. I’m eager to continue contributing to projects that can create systemic improvements for these organizations, while also deepening my communication and technical skills. JumboCode offers a rare opportunity to be part of something bigger than myself, working on meaningful solutions alongside people who share my passion for using technology as a force for good.	In high school, I served on the cabinet for the Community Service Club, where I was able to actively participate in various community engagement initiatives. One of our most significant events was the annual Hunger Banquet, where we not only raised awareness about world hunger but also fundraised for a local organization dedicated to combating poverty and hunger in the Bronx. This event was especially meaningful for me because growing up in the Bronx exposed me to these issues from a young age. I witnessed firsthand how limited access to resources could profoundly impact people's lives. I think this early exposure is one of the main reasons I have always had a strong desire for social justice. In addition to my involvement with the Hunger Banquet, I dedicated my Sundays during high school to volunteering at a soup kitchen. This soup kitchen served food and provided clothes for people in need, including the homeless, elderly, and disabled communities. Despite the early hours and long commute, I really enjoyed giving back to my community while also forming connections with others who shared a similar passion for helping those in need. Moreover, this experience allowed me to interact with members of the communities we served, enabling me to hear their unique stories and gain a deeper understanding of their challenges. My commitment to giving back to my community has not only been fulfilling but also enjoyable. Therefore, I am confident that working as a part of JumboCode will be an even more rewarding experience. With JumboCode, I would have the opportunity to apply my coding skills to make a meaningful impact on my community, merging my two passions into a singular purpose.	Yes	Yes	Sunday: 12pm - 4pm, 5pm - 8pm\nMonday: 3pm - 8pm\nTuesday: 6pm - 8pm\nWednesday: 3pm - 8pm\nThursday: 3pm - 6pm \nFriday: 12pm - 2pm\nSaturday: 12pm - 6pm	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 160: Algorithms	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, Python	My first introduction to computer science was during my sophomore year of high school when I joined Girls Who Code. It was within this club that I wrote my very first lines of code. Leading Girls Who Code during my senior year was particularly meaningful to me because it allowed me to be the one introducing other girls to the world of coding. This experience offered me the chance to empower fellow girls with the knowledge and skills necessary to excel in the field of computer science. Witnessing their progress felt especially rewarding, as I knew I was equipping them with the tools to solve problems and make a positive impact on the world through technology. Additionally, knowing that I was encouraging more girls to pursue computer science made me feel like I was contributing to closing the gender gap in technology. One of the most enjoyable aspects of Girls Who Code was the supportive atmosphere it provided. As the president of the club, I had the privilege of helping foster a close-knit community where girls could connect, share their ideas, and learn from one another. I am excited about working with JumboCode because I am truly passionate about computer science, and I look forward to collaborating with individuals who share the same enthusiasm. Furthermore, JumboCode gives a unique opportunity to be part of a collaborative and supportive team that genuinely cares about my success and growth as a developer.	In high school, I participated in an AI Ethics Lab, during which I learned how to identify and solve ethical issues in artificial intelligence. During this program, my cohort and I prototyped a social media app that I modeled after Instagram but with the intention for it to be more ethical. One way I did this was by making the app’s privacy policy more transparent to the consumer. I’ve always known that I wanted a career in technology, however, I’m also passionate about social justice. I care about improving the lives of others and having a positive impact on society. Before the CreAItivity AI Ethics Lab, I assumed that I had to pick between STEM and social justice. Through the AI Ethics Lab, I explored both of my interests without sacrificing one or the other, which is why I was so excited about this project. My cohort and I worked together on the project, dividing the project into different parts and having pairs tackle their respective components. We drew up a schedule to stay on track and checked in on each other. One challenge I faced was when one of my cohort members stopped showing up to meetings. We tried to contact her to see what was going on, but we were left with no response. The cohort member she was meant to work with now had twice as much work, so we redivided what was left of the project so everyone could contribute equally. Each of the cohorts in the program had the task of tackling a problem in AI Ethics and providing their own solution. No one in my group knew each other before the program, but by the end of it we had all grown really close and we won first place! It was truly fulfilling to see how my cohort grew as a group. The way we collaborated together and supported one another made a huge difference in our success. Through my experience with the AI Ethics Lab, I was able to explore the field I love while creating lasting memories and forging new friendships. What excites me about working with JumboCode is knowing how rewarding it is to collaborate with others toward a common goal. Witnessing a project evolve from an idea to a finished product, all the while observing the personal and collective growth of your team members, is especially gratifying.	\N	1	4	11	12	3	7	5	10	9	2	8	6	I am drawn to the West Medford Community Center project because it provides an opportunity to directly support an organization whose mission aligns with my values of community connection and social impact. The Center’s dedication to fostering a sense of belonging among individuals of all ages, backgrounds, and experiences is something I admire, especially in today’s world, where building community feels more essential than ever. By improving their newsletter process through a user-friendly web app, we can help the Center focus on what really matters: the people they serve.\nThe programs offered by the West Medford Community Center, from senior health initiatives to after-school programs for middle schoolers, embody the Center’s mission to bring people together and create opportunities for growth and connection. By streamlining their communication via a newsletter tool, we can strengthen these initiatives by keeping the community informed and engaged. This solution also reduces the administrative workload of the Executive Director, allowing them to focus more on fostering community-building events like "Meet Your Neighbor" gatherings and technology lab workshops.\nUltimately, what excites me most about this project is the chance to use technology to amplify the Center’s ability to bring people together, making their work easier and more impactful in the process.\n\n\nThe Bread & Roses project appeals to me because it combines technology with a cause that is both urgent and impactful—addressing food insecurity while fostering community empowerment. I admire how Bread & Roses goes beyond providing meals, offering a dignified, restaurant-style experience that creates a sense of belonging for every individual who walks through their doors. The idea that volunteers know everyone by name highlights the organization’s commitment to creating a safe, welcoming environment. \nWhat excites me most about this project is how it aligns with Bread & Roses’ mission of empowerment. By making it easier for volunteers to sign up and stay informed, we help them feel more connected and engaged with the community they’re serving. Ultimately, this project is a perfect blend of my passion for using technology to create practical solutions and my desire to contribute to organizations that make a tangible difference in people’s lives. I am eager to work with the team to build a tool that can help Bread & Roses continue to uplift the Merrimack Valley community.	\N	SpeakOUT Boston	Fréaláf Hildeson	
