ants now!  	\N	9	5	8	7	2	11	12	4	3	1	6	10	I made my preferences based on which causes spoke to me the most. For me, the language barrier is a meaningful issue that I’d love to make a contribution to because it was a big part of my parents’ transition to life here. I am also friends with a couple of the project leads so it would be great to work with them on a big project like this!	\N	Yes, I was a part of the Sibling Connections project last year!	Folca	
“100000000/10 GOOD ENERGY GOOD VIBES, WILL BE THERE AND LOCKED IN” -Sristi Panchu	he/him	2026	Yes	I am eager to join JumboCode because it aligns perfectly with my passion for both technology and social activism. Growing up in a rural, predominately Hispanic community, I experienced firsthand the technological inequalities that many underserved communities face: outdated equipment in classrooms, little to no computing curriculum, language and cultural barriers in accessing educational software. These experiences motivate me to use my education and skills to give back to communities like my own. \n\nJoining JumboCode would give me the platform to contribute meaningfully to socially impactful projects while learning from experienced peers and mentors. It would also help me engage more deeply with the computer science community at Tufts, as I’ve primarily been involved with the affinity centers so far. I’m excited about the opportunity to apply what I’ve learned in my coursework, expand my technical skill set, and collaborate on projects that drive real change.	Community engagement and activism have always been a central part of my life, both growing up and now at Tufts. In high school, one of my favorite hobbies was volunteering with a local organization to provide technical support across the school district. Although I didn’t yet know how to code, I used my passion for technology to assist in other ways, such as freeing up storage on computers or cleaning hardware.\n\nAt Tufts, most of my free time is spent divided among the identity centers. As a peer leader at the FIRST Center, I support first-generation students and help organize events aimed at fostering community and solidarity. I also intern at the Latinx Center, where I focus on developing programming for the Latinx community, often collaborating with other identity centers to highlight the intersectionality of our experiences. Additionally, I work with the Indigenous Center as a choreographer for the Tufts Ballet Folklórico team, which is dedicated to preserving and celebrating Indigenous dance styles across Latin America.	Yes	Yes	Sunday: Anytime\nMonday: After 4:30 PM\nTuesday: 3:00 - 8:00 PM \nWednesday: 4:30 - 7:30 PM\nThursday: 3:00 - 8:00 PM\nFriday: After 1:30 PM (subject to change)\nSaturday: Anytime\n\n*Weekends or Monday preferred	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	Formally—CS 11, but before that a little bit of Scratch!	One of the most exciting projects I worked on was during my internship at a startup this past summer. It was my first time working with a development team, and since it was a small group, I had a lot of freedom to take charge of the project. At first, it was a bit overwhelming—I didn’t have any prior professional experience, and I had to figure out a lot on my own. But I quickly realized this was a great chance to step up and really make the project my own.\n\nThe main focus of my project was processing and analyzing data from machine-learning models. This data came from job postings in the clean energy industry, covering things like salaries, benefits, and required skills. My job was to automate the data processing using Python, which I wasn’t too familiar with at the time. The learning curve was steep, but after some serious trial and error (and a lot of Googling), I started to get the hang of it. \n\nOne of the biggest challenges was dealing with messy, unstructured data. The job posting data wasn’t exactly neat, so I had to get creative in cleaning it up and making it usable for analysis. I built scripts that not only processed the data but were also designed to be reusable for future projects, which saved a ton of time in the long run. By the end of it, I had set up automated data pipelines that made the whole process much more efficient. \n\nAs I was wrapping up the data work, I noticed that people were having trouble digging through endless Excel sheets to find specific insights. I suggested building a simple web app to make the data easier to access for both the team and stakeholders. My manager was on board but given the scale of the project we settled on focusing on just salary and benefits, and I spent the rest of my internship building this web application.\n\nI used Flask and SQLite to build the app—nothing too grand, but it made sense for the small scale of the project. The trickiest part was designing an interface that was easy to follow, but after a few iterations and feedback from the team, I came up with something that worked well. In the end, I delivered both the data pipelines and the web app, which ended up helping many people within the company. \n\nLooking back, this project was very rewarding because I could see the direct impact of my work. I learned a lot along the way and came out of the experience feeling more confident in my skills.	\N	7	4	10	12	6	5	2	9	1	8	3	11	When ranking the projects, I considered three main factors: the mission of each nonprofit, the team dynamics, and the potential for personal growth.\n\nI first focused on the mission of each organization and whether I felt a personal connection to their values. Many of my top choices reflect nonprofits whose missions resonate with me, especially those serving communities similar to the one I grew up in. As someone deeply committed to giving back to underserved communities, I was naturally drawn to projects where I felt I could make a meaningful impact.\n\nI also factored in the vibes of each group and their leaders, based on my experience attending the JumboCode GIM and hearing from the project leads. While it’s tough to get a full sense of a team from a short presentation, I tried to assess how well I might fit into each group’s culture. Feeling comfortable within a team is important to me, and I want to be part of a group where I can thrive and contribute meaningfully.\n\nLastly, I considered the technical aspects of the projects. I’m eager to expand my skills, so I took into consideration projects that involve technologies or approaches that I haven’t worked with before. This would allow me to push myself outside of my comfort zone and help me grow as a developer.\n\nThat said, I believe in the mission of every organization represented, and I’d be happy to contribute to any project. Regardless of the ranking, I know each one offers a chance to make a real difference.	:)	\N	Glóredhel	
	she/her	2028	Yes	Joining JumboCode would be an opportunity to get a glimpse of what it is like to work on a project that is more than “just another school assignment.” JumboCode would both deepen my experience as a software developer and enable me to use my abilities to benefit local communities. By being a part of JumboCode, I hope to gain experience that will help me lead projects on my own, and look forward to spending hours brainstorming, designing, and problem solving alongside other passionate club members.	The first time I volunteered was on a Saturday morning in 2019 at the FSUMC food pantry. As I distributed fresh produce, helped patrons with their luggage, and organized donated clothes with other determined volunteers, I realized I wanted to continue serving the East Harlem community. After volunteering bi-monthly until the pantry closed in 2022, I took a more front-end role in organizing non-perishable goods and conversing with guests for The Father’s Heart soup kitchen until my senior year of high school. \n\nDuring my senior year I tutored for two hours weekly for students grades 1–6 in geometry and English, developing my ability to explain concepts to those who learn in different ways than I do. Although tutoring was difficult at first, I grew to enjoy helping someone build confidence and learn to solve questions on their own.\n\nOutside of tutoring and volunteering for food distribution centers, I have experience managing a food stall for the Flat Rock environmental education center, participating in local park cleanups, and organizing community tree plantings to spread awareness of environmental issues such as deforestation and food waste.\n	Yes	Yes	Mon: 3:00 PM - 8:30 PM\nTues: 12:00 PM - 4:30 PM\nWed: 4:30 - 5:30 PM; 8:30 - 11PM\nThurs: Any time after 12:00 PM\nSun: Any time after 12:00 PM\n\nFree anytime: Fridays, Saturdays	Currently taking CS 11	Git/GitHub, Python, JAVA	In third grade I created a simple game about asteroids, sparking a curiosity in programming that only grew after joining my school’s STEM Academy. After learning JAVA and Python (and going down an AI video essay rabbit-hole) I decided to create my own machine learning program that predicts a student’s likelihood of dropping out of college. Although I had studied computer science for years prior to this personal project, learning about random forest classifiers, neural networks, and other unfamiliar concepts that once confused me reinvigorated my initial spark for coding.	During my senior year I had the opportunity to intern as a research assistant for the Stevens Institute of Technology’s Coastal Engineering Research Group. It was amazing assisting and learning from graduate students and professors that had more programming experience than me. The notion that the program I helped make (a program that analyzed New Jersey shoreline changes using public satellite images, Anaconda, and Jupyter Notebook) could shape research that affected local environmental policies excited me. So despite the challenges of debugging and brainstorming solutions to seemingly “insurmountable” problems, the idea that my work had purpose drove me to spend countless well-spent (albeit tiring) hours contemplating problems in my code in pursuit of those satisfying light-bulb moments.	Research Paper: https://doi.org/10.58445/rars.653	8	5	9	11	1	12	10	7	4	3	2	6	Dillar Academy: As a person born and raised in the United States, I understand how lucky I am for receiving an education and having basic human liberties. Everyone should have the right to learn skills that enable themselves to better their life, which is why I strongly believe in Dillar Academy's goal of teaching Uyghurs English. I can personally relate to sub-optimal class management systems (in high school there would be many issues of the class sign-up website crashing and bugs leading to scheduling conflicts) and would love to assist in any way I can to help the Uyghur people. I am also really intrigued by the project idea, and am curious at how the team would make a better SIS. \n\nA2Empowerment: I would love to assist in any way to further the education of women in Cameroon. Any nonprofit that works to educate women is one I would like to for. \n\nBread and Roses: Bread and Roses' project mission is a message I strongly resonate with due to my previous experience volunteering for a food pantry and soup kitchen. This is particularly the case because the soup kitchen I volunteered for (Father's Heart) had a large amount of volunteers and served thousands of patrons weekly, which led to frequent confusion during the chaotic serving hours, which caused a lot of headache for the volunteers and higher ups. I therefore understand the importance of organized volunteer data and clear communication, and how it could lead to a better served community.	\N	No, I have not been a part of JumboCode before.	Fundin	
	he/him	2028	No	I want to join JumboCode to apply my CS skills for a better cause. Through working on a project at JumboCode, I hope to learn new technical skills (i.e. learning TypeScript and NEXT.js) as well as learning to collaborate with peers when solving a problem. Most of my previous projects have been done independently, so I am excited to be working with others as I believe this will improve our overall team productivity while also making some friends along the way! From a technical standpoint, I have limited experience with front and back end development, so I would like to build upon those skills through understanding how to operate new softwares and potentially learn new languages as well! 	Throughout high school, I led a club called Codivate where we taught foreign workers programming skills in Python. Through helping them build their programming background, they were able to get better employment opportunities in the future. Furthermore, in my senior year of high school, I was a teaching assistant for the AP Computer Science course, helping students understand various Java programming concepts as well as helping the teacher with creating the lesson plans. Lastly, in my summers in high school, I would hold weekly basketball clinics where I taught younger children around the local area in Singapore basic skills to improve their game. Along with helping them develop these skills, I also raised 300+ dollars for a local charity which runs free basketball programs for underprivileged kids. 	Yes	Yes	Monday (after 2:45pm), Tuesday (2-6pm), Wednesday (11:20 - 1:15pm, 5:45 - 9pm), Thursday (2-6pm), Friday (1:15 onwards), Saturday (Anytime except 1-3pm and nights preferably), Sunday anytime	\N	HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, Python, Java	I was first introduced to CS in my freshman year of high school, where I took an Intro to CS class in Java. I loved the course as I was able to build some really cool projects, such as a Subway self-checkout system, and I really enjoyed getting into the “flow” of programming. Since then, I took every CS course available to me in high school, including AP Computer Science (was a TA for the course as well), a game design course, and a Data Structures course. 	While it definitely wasn’t the most challenging piece  I’ve worked on, my most cherished programming project was when I built a fantasy basketball team simulator in Java. This was the project that truly made me fall in love with CS. I loved it so much as I was able to connect CS with one of my passions–obsessing over NBA player 2K ratings. I used a database of NBA player 2K ratings and searched through them when the user was prompted which players they wanted to build the team, subsequently building a ‘player’ object with their stats. Once the team was built, I generated random basketball teams that the team would go against, and the season would end once the user’s team had finished playing all the randomly generated teams. My biggest challenge during this project was using static/non-static variables, as I remember not understanding the concept fully and having to go to my teacher’s office hours for help (it took me hours to debug). I remember the satisfaction after finishing the project; after hours of planning, coding, and debugging, I had this amazing thing–though simple now that I look back at it–that I was truly proud of. 	Here are some cool things I've worked on: https://github.com/kubsgok/polotno-demo-test (for an internship where I built a certificate-designing application in an education platform called Teamie), fun crossy road game I built in JS: https://openprocessing.org/sketch/575595 	6	5	9	3	4	12	11	2	1	10	7	8	I am very interested in the English at Large project as I remember doing a similar project in my AP Computer Science where I organized book objects in a 2D array and I want to build on this idea further. Additionally, I think it would be interesting to utilize data science to organize textbooks as I care for this field. LCS tutoring would also be very compelling as I have tutored others and see the value of matching mentees with mentors with similar interests as this can make a difference in the quality of the tutoring experience itself! Lastly, the Somerville Museum seems really cool (for the reasons mentioned above), but also I really really want to meet Ben Stiller! 	\N	\N	Meneldor	
	he/him	2027	No	This past summer, I interned at a startup, and one of the things that left a lasting impression on me was how the three founding members talked about leaving big companies to start something they genuinely cared about. They wanted their work to have a real impact on people’s lives, and that wasn’t something they were finding at their previous jobs. I could relate to that sentiment, especially after experiencing the rewarding feeling of seeing my own work positively affect users. Even the smallest contributions—like building a new feature or improving a workflow—gave me a sense of purpose. I realized that I want my career to be about more than just writing code; I want it to be about making people’s lives better. That’s why I want to join JumboCode. I see it as a unique opportunity to use my computer science skills in a way that has a tangible, positive impact on people. The chance to work with non-profits and help build applications that serve meaningful causes is exactly the type of work I want to be involved in. I hope to learn more about how technology can be leveraged for social good and to collaborate with like-minded people who share that passion for making a difference. Ultimately, I want to continue growing as a developer while contributing to projects that genuinely help communities, and I believe JumboCode is the perfect place to do that.	My journey into activism was largely inspired by my sister, who always emphasized the importance of standing up for what you believe in. She founded the Sunrise Movement branch at our school, as well as the Queers and Allies club. After she graduated, I took on leadership roles in both clubs, organizing walkouts for climate change protests and leading panels that focused on queer visibility and representation. Growing up in a city as progressive as New York City, I’ve had the privilege of being surrounded by social movements, and I’ve participated in numerous protests over the years. Living in NYC has also made me aware of the glaring disparities in wealth and privilege. My parents instilled in me the importance of giving back from a young age, often having my siblings and me volunteer at soup kitchens on weekends. These experiences shaped my understanding of community and social good, and reinforced my belief in the power of collective action to create meaningful change. Whether it’s advocating for climate justice or supporting marginalized communities, I’ve always felt a strong sense of responsibility to engage in causes that promote equity and social good.	Yes	Yes	Monday: 11:30AM, 4:30PM, 7:30PM, Tuesday: after 2PM, Wednesday: Between 11:30AM and 6PM, Thursday: Between 2PM and 6PM, Friday: After 2PM, Weekends: All day	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Python	My first introduction to computer science happened the summer after 8th grade when my parents insisted I take an introductory Java course at Haverford College. At the time, I wasn’t particularly excited. I had always leaned more toward subjects like English and never saw myself as someone interested in coding or anything math-related. But as soon as I started learning how to write code, everything changed. I quickly fell in love with the logical yet creative nature of programming. One of our final projects was to create a tic-tac-toe game, and I remember the moment I completed it, I was filled with a sense of accomplishment. It was the first time I fully understood the rewarding feeling of building something from the ground up. That experience made me realize that coding wasn’t just about solving problems—it was about creating new possibilities. From then on, I was hooked, and my love for computer science has only grown deeper as I’ve explored the endless things I can create with it.	This past summer, I interned at a startup that provides software to manage audiology practices, and one of the most exciting projects I worked on involved developing a new tagging system for providers. The task was to enable practice-level management of tags, allowing providers to create their own filters for patients, which was crucial in improving their workflow. Technically, I started by updating the existing Tag model to make it more flexible. This involved removing and adding new fields. I also had to extend our API to support listing, creating, updating, and deleting tags. This meant implementing filters that allowed users to fetch either verified global tags or tags created by their practice. Additionally, when a tag was no longer needed, I had to implement a system where the tag could be deleted if unused, or dissociated from the practice if it was shared across multiple practices. One of the main challenges I faced was ensuring data integrity while adding the flexibility needed for practice-level management. For instance, I had to ensure that the verified field defaulted to True for existing tags while allowing new tags to be unverified, and I needed to manage cases where multiple practices might use the same tag. Another challenge was designing the UI for the new tags management page, which needed to align with existing settings pages and provide a user-friendly interface for administrators to manage their tags. Despite the complexity, it was incredibly fulfilling to build something I knew would make a real difference for our users, helping them manage their practices more efficiently. This project excited me because it wasn’t just about implementing features—it was about solving real-world problems that impact people’s day-to-day work. It was the first time I truly felt like my code was making a difference, and it gave me a deep sense of pride in what I was creating.	\N	10	4	8	11	7	1	2	9	6	5	3	12	Ranking all these projects was extremely difficult for me because these are all projects that I would be honored to be a part of. I feel particularly passionate about wrongful convictions, queer visibility, and food insecurity because these are all things I've been witness to over the course of my life. I Also really enjoy the idea of the projects helping immigrants learn English because my father immigrated to the US and struggled for the first few years before he became fluent in English.	No, thank you for considering my application.	I have not.	Hirgon	
	He/him	2027	No	For the longest time I thought coders and the coding community were super exclusive. I was intimidated, because I felt like everyone knew more than me. After going to the GIM and talking to Julia, a former member, I've realized that Jumbo code is exactly the opposite of that. From the late-night grind seshes, to the team dinners, I’ve realized that Jumbo Code is the perfect place for me. I hope to find the community that Jumbo Code has cultivated. I think this community is where I can really succeed. \n\nSecond, I would like to improve the skills of front-end programming. As a computer engineer, my large experience with coding has been low level, and its relationship with hardware. I haven’t had much experience with front-end programming, and I think this opportunity will help me be a better programmer. Furthermore, I think these learned skills will transcend programming, and will make me a better computer engineer. Lastly, I want to learn the process and the work of managing a project. I am a big people person, and the best part of the project is the ability to work with people. I want to learn from the project leads on how they organize tasks, and bring people together. \n	I have two main experiences. The first is Engineering Without Borders. My project is the Malawi project where we bring water and education access to a small village in rural Malawi called Solomoni. I helped plan an event called Malawi the Journey. At this event, I led a workshop teaching the Malawi language Chichewa. Additionally, I helped communicate with the village residents, and worked with the NGO there to help solve problems. The events that I helped organize and attend were the most fun and rewarding work I’ve done here at Tufts. I got to learn about the culture, the amazing people, and, most importantly, to see how our project really made a difference.\n\n\nThe second experience is working as a Sustainable Core Fellow. Ever since sophomore year of high school, sustainability has always been a huge passion of mine. Climate Change, and sustainable equity are some of the most important issues for our generation. Core Fellows has been an unforgettable part of my time at Tufts. Beyond the amazing other fellows, I’ve had the opportunity to work with multiple culture clubs, and many student organizations to educate people on sustainability. \n	Yes	Yes	I am ideally free tuesday, thursday evening and evening.. I can meet also friday from 12 to 1:15. I have other free parts in the day that I can work my schedule around.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++	CS11. I also learned some Matlab for a math modeling competition.	The best project I've been on is the water automation system at EWB (Engineers Without Borders). In this project, we had three groups to set up an automation system for the plants in the Tufts greenhouse. I helped in the electrical group. The hardest problem of the project was communicating between groups. Our electrical group was responsible for coding the motors and the pump to feed water to the plants according to each plant’s necessary water intake. We had to make an interface for students to put in plant watering information. I was also responsible for the wiring for the system. We had to connect the wiring such that each motor would run depending on which plant needs watering. This was extremely rewarding because I had to learn a little bit of everything. From 3D printing to soldering, I learned a lot of skills for the whole project to come together. It was rewarding to see all the pieces I helped work on slow being brought together. It was almost miraculous that everything worked in the end.\n\n\nThe most challenging part of the project was easily the work across groups. Our group relied on every other group to work, and vice versa. As such, we were often forced to make changes to fit the confines of another group’s design. For example, the motors that I initially wired were too weak to move the water gates; As a result, I had to change the motors which caused us to completely redesign our wires and our coding. This project taught me the key to success in a project is communication. If one group was kept in the dark, no group would have succeeded— each group excels with the help of others.\n	\N	7	6	1	8	5	4	10	9	12	2	3	11	The top 5 are in no particular order. I see many similarities between A2Empowerment and some of my work in EWB. To reiterate, Social and Sustainable Equity is incredibly important to me, and think that a project like Bread and Roses, and the New England Innocence project align with my interests very well. I also recognize my privilege to able to attend Tufts. I want to give people the same opportunity I was given, and I think the Wily Network is perfect for that. Lastly, as a child of Chinese immigrants, I understand the difficulty of learning a new language while under a new environment. My parents were fortunate enough to get help to learn English through a non-profit organization, and I hope to provide the same help my family has received by working with Dillar Academy. The last 7 choices were all incredibly interesting, and it was so difficult to decide, but the top 5 are definitely the ones I prefer the most. 	\N	no	Lord of the Nazgûl	
	she/her	2026	Yes	It's a great opportunity to refine my technical skills as well as a cool opportunity to work on a meaningful project that our local non-profit organizations will get to use! I hope to gain some new friends and new memories, plus better fluency with new and old tools/frameworks!	I am a TA for CS20 (the web programming course) and have helped out at the various events that our cultural clubs host throughout the year (e.g. serving food and setting up the decorations at Matsuri (hosted by JCC). I am also a part of our annual WIT conference as a member of the Outreach Committee (check it out if you're not already going!) and when I'm back at home during the holidays, I help out at my family's small restaurant. I was also part of JumboCode last year, and got to help create an awesome webapp for a very cool non-profit (#welovebiwomenquarterly)!	Yes	Yes	Mostly Friday evenings but can be flexible with the rest of the team :) 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 116: Introduction to Security, CS 160: Algorithms	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL	CS11! 	One of the coolest projects I had the pleasure to work on was actually something me and 3 other friends created during a hackathon. Our project “MyVoterInfo” was a web app designed to target voter suppression by providing up-to-date information ranging from the user’s registration status to nearby ballot locations, polling hours, and more. \n\nThe user’s voter status was scraped from the state’s online public voter registration website using Puppeteer (given the limited time, we were only able to set it up for the state of Massachusetts), and a lot of the location data was sourced from the Google Civic API (a very cool find!). We integrated all this information with the Google Maps API to allow the users to see the various locations relative to their own home address. \n\nIn terms of the actual process, our awesome co-lead was able to set the barebones React Native framework before the start of the competition. From there, the designer of our group made awesome POCs and wireframes as the rest of us went off to do some research on what resources we could use. This included finding the right APIs, learning more about the style library that we were using, and overall getting familiar with the React Native framework. And then from there, we moved on to the implementation steps.\n\nAs someone who had more experience with the frontend side of things, I went about setting the API calls for the Google Civic API and started implementing the various components and layouts that my designer friend had created. My other two friends went about setting up all the backends and tirelessly fixed any last minute bugs before our demo time. \n\nAll in all, the 2-day project was a huge success! Our demo went smoothly and we even went home taking the big prize! (Overall Winner!!) In retrospect, I wished I polished up my backend skills more and familiarized myself more with the framework since the biggest challenge we had was debugging when we began combining the various components. Additionally, given such a short timeframe, we had to sacrifice some functionalities in order to focus on our primary features. So, taking this all into consideration, when I begin my next project, I hope to bring both a solid technical background and a strong sense of organization and prioritization (plus a cheerful spirit) to ship a product from its early development stage to deployment!	https://github.com/lydiachen01	8	1	6	12	3	9	10	7	5	4	2	11	As someone who grew up in a low-income community where getting healthy and nutritious food was not accessible for many, I really resonate with Village Hub Food and their mission of making such a quintessential part of living accessible while also promoting sustainability and waste reduction. As a fellow foodie that recently got into cooking, I realized how essential it is to eat well and have a well-balanced diet and if the software we create can help others better their own life through food, I am 100% all-in. This is why I would love to be a part of this project and help expand this non-profit so that they can create a lasting effect on more people!	Nope!	Yes, I was part of JumboCode last year, working on a webapp for Bi Women Quarterly.	Durin IV	
