“super super passionate - reached out to me to ask about jc” -Lillian Tran	she/her/hers	2028	Yes	     Working with real clients on meaningful projects through JumboCode will challenge me to grow professionally and provide tangible help to affected communities. No other campus club offers such impactful work, and the level of responsibility, carefully chosen projects, and year-round commitment shows how dedicated the members are. \n     After researching and speaking with Lilian, I am confident that JumboCode is the perfect fit for me. The level of personal responsibility expected, the careful choosing of the projects and year-round commitment expected reinforces my idea of the club as a group of people who are dedicated to the mission at hand. JumboCode is the place where the new members are taught real skills, taught how to communicate with the client and gain invaluable practice working on a team project that has real consequences and rewards, outside of the college bubble. \n     I am tired of participating in student organizations whose sole purpose turns out to be putting the founder in the limelight with zero work accomplished behind the scenes (i.e. the nonprofits my high school classmates founded dissipated the moment application season was over). In college, I want to dedicate myself to only a few of my passions at a time, but really explore the fields I choose in depth. At JumboCode, I hope to successfully become who I want to be, professionally and personally, by fully committing myself to the mission of the club and the unique people who make the club into what it is.  \n     I dream of opening my own business of creating websites and helping on projects to the small businesses around me especially in my native country Kazakhstan. There is scattered infrastructure in place in rural areas for entrepreneurs, preservers of culture heritage and ordinary people. My ultimate goal for pursuing data science, computing skills and the knowledge of jumpstarting projects from the ground is to use my experience to craft smart public policy. For example, many of the rural villages hold the cultural heritage of my nomadic culture but there is barely anywhere online to preserve the historical records and pass them down to later generations. I want to personally build change, with my own two hands if needed, to help so many different people around me who don’t have the money to hire a professional team. But I don’t know where to start and I recognize that I need help, experience with making mistakes and failing and getting back up in the end to help my goals come true. I know that I can’t truly achieve the impact or change that I seek without a team, and my motivation remains incomplete without actual experience and mentorship. The more I read about the JumboCode members, the team leads and project managers, I see just how inspirational and well-rounded they are, and I hope to embody the spirit of the club as a member of the team. 	I believe that one of the truly effective ways to improve the lives of others is active help. It’s not enough to take action within a bubble of comfort, such as sympathizing with a cause - an action has to be taken for compassion to be meaningful. \nMy first real step out of my comfort zone was during quarantine. Actually, it was more of a sprint. \nCut to me walking in the smoldering heat, hanging posters on the fences, bulletin boards and bus stops as the tape slipped through my sweaty fingers and the stack of paper occasionally pierced my skin. \nI had decided to host a food drive, despite being the definition of an introvert. On the day of the food drive, I nervously waited under my school’s entrance, listening to the drumming of rain. My doubts dissipated as car after car pulled into the driveway, and with every donation, I remained incredulous that people had come to support a rogue food drive organized by a freshman. But when I advertised my food drive in my Zoom classes, emailed my English teacher whom I’d never met in person to sponsor it and asked the school for a table, words flowed out of me in waves. 500+ food items were collected and on the drive to the food pantry, I knew I had fallen in love with community involvement. \n I realized that I was worried people wouldn't show up because, in all honesty, I was unfamiliar with the US and the city that I called home for the past six years. I decided to get to know my new community better and signed up to be a camp counselor at the Jewish community center across from my house. The unfamiliar faces became family as I danced the mornings away with the entire camp, learning how to celebrate Shabbat with challah and grape juice every Friday. The exhaustion after entertaining competitive eight year olds was worth every minute when I got to know their personalities and stories.\nInvolving myself in my surrounding community has helped me understand that even the smallest actions can help someone and motivates me in dedicating my career to aiding those in need.\nIn partnership with the county executive’s office, I helped organize Middle Eastern American Heritage Month celebrations and when I saw the astonishment on guests’ faces, marveling that the government saw their heritage as worthy of celebration, I felt even more motivated to continue engaging in community work. \nLast summer, I tutored English at a children’s house in Almaty, Kazakhstan. Initially, it was really hard to find any organization that offered volunteering opportunities and upon that discovery, I started calling place after place looking to be useful and using my English knowledge for good. When I eventually found a home willing to let me teach, it was some of the most rewarding tutoring I have yet performed. \nAfter witnessing my goals come to fruition as a result of my passion and commitment, I’m only further imbued to experiment with ideas ahead of me and my future team. \n	Yes	Yes	Mondays: 4-10pm, Tuesday 5-10pm, Wednesday 3-4:30 8-10pm, Thursday 3-4:30 8-10pm, Friday: 12-4pm, 8-10pm, Saturday all day , Sunday all day	\N	C/C++, Python, Java	I took AP COMP Java in High School and taking CS 11 now. 	In the pursuit of a direct impact on my local community, I founded an environmental woodworking club and took it upon myself to master the art of crafting birdhouses. I started the team from the ground up and recruited passersby on word-of-mouth and enthusiasm only. It’s incredible to remember that I’d drag 30 people each weekend to random creeks to pick up trash. I relished my time within the team and devoted myself to the responsibility of hosting study sessions, fundraising and astonishingly many hours carpooling. 	\N	3	10	5	11	1	2	9	7	4	6	8	12	I am a tutor for the Petey Greene program, where I come in to tutor incarcerated students once a week and I am really passionate about changing the carceral system. As for Dillar Academy, this issue is very close to me. I come from Kazakhstan, right near the border with Xinjiang and my grandpa actually emigrated from Xinjiang in the 50s. He brought 100,000 people with him, who now make up over a million Kazakhstan citizens to this day. In other words, those 1 million people would have been in Xinjiang at this time, right now. I would be there too. In fact, at one of the local gatherings by the Kazakh community, I met a Uighur concentration camp survivor. I didn’t know it at the time, but when I found out later on, my heart felt heavy. I wanted to cry on the spot. There are a million of Turkic Central Asians suffering alongside Uighurs, and I have no doubt that if my family had remained in Xinjiang, I would have undergone the same fate. This project means a lot to me. I want to be a part of it to help the Uighurs specifically globally. I have donated to dozens of GoFundMes but helping individuals can’t compare to the scale of the Dillar Academy. \n	\N	\N	Nori	
