a more user-friendly, contemporary platform. My main roles involved video production, where I took charge of video reshooting and editing for multiple courses that totaled 48 hours to match the updated website. The new website now serves as a valuable reference for aspiring tennis players. Here is the site for reference:  https://greatbasetennis.com/	\N	9	1	11	12	4	6	10	7	3	2	5	8	Village Hub Food/ Bread & Roses \nWhen my family first immigrated to America, I experienced food insecurity, often missing meals at school because my family couldn’t afford it. Knowing the feeling of hunger and not being able to eat along with my peers, I wish to become a part of a team that supports initiatives similar to the food shelters I volunteered at during the holidays. Through these experiences, I know such efforts need to have continued support to be able to succeed long-term. This is why I want to help JumboCode make a sustainable impact on a larger scale. \n\nEnglish at Large/ Dillar Academy \nGrowing up, watching my parents navigate a new job market in a foreign country emphasized the challenges of communicating in a second or third language. I was able to witness how the language barrier demanded them to work twice as hard to access the same opportunities as native speakers. I attended an elementary school that discouraged my parents from speaking our native language at home in an effort for me to ‘assimilate,’ which only hindered my sense of identity. This experience made me realize how crucial it is to preserve one’s own culture, even if it requires having to learn a new language and adjusting to living in a foreign country. I see these two projects as a way to help people that have been in positions similar to my family as education doesn’t have to come at the expense of identity. \n\nA2Empowerment \nComing from a country where women are often discouraged from pursuing higher education such as a bachelor's or master's degree, I have seen how gender discrimination limits both academic and professional opportunities. When my mother was expecting, she was refused employment at many companies despite her qualifications due to the chauvinism surrounding maternity leave and her responsibilities as a soon-to-be mother. Due to this, she was forced to work until absolutely needed, and without the guarantee of paid leave. Unfortunately, even though times are changing, I still see my female relatives within my extended family having to fight for the right to attend college and gain independence, and escape the need to rely on a partner for financial support. If women were able to succeed with little to no support before, we can only imagine the great impact this project can make by the same time next year. 	Along with the free tennis lessons, I got you for room lockouts as well. 	\N	Thror	
