	she/her	2025	Yes	I want to join Jumbocode to use coding to make a direct impact in our local community! Oftentimes programming can feel very disconnected from anything related to social good, especially since the internships and classes I have done coding before were all very low level projects. I have always been especially interested in finding a career in CS that is more directly related to social good, and this would be a great opportunity for me do just that! Also, since most things I've done in the past has been very low level/ backend, this would be a great opportunity to learn some front end/ web development skills. 	I've done Tufts Food Rescue since sophomore year. For that, I did both on-campus (packaging up leftover food in the dining hall once a week) and off campus (driving leftover food from a Whole Foods to a local food bank) shifts. Besides that, I was also a TA for CS10 for a year, and now I am a CS40 TA (and I was also a tutor at a math school in high school for 3 years, where I ran an session for helping kids with their homework). I am also the Vice President of Computer Science Exchange (CSX) at Tufts this year, which works to foster community among CS students at Tufts! I also participated in their mentorship program last Fall, which pairs upperclassmen students (as mentors) with underclassmen students (as mentees). 	Yes	Yes	I am available most days in the morning/ early afternoon (M/W, free until 3, Tuesday free until 4:30, Friday is also pretty free)	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 105: Programming Languages, CS 115: Database Systems, CS 121: Software Engineering, CS 160: Algorithms, CS 170: Computation Theory	C/C++, Swift, Git/GitHub, SQL, Python, Go, Java	I've been taking coding classes since freshman year of high school!	For my internship last summer (2023), me and two other interns built a API microservice application to automate dataset summary and improve search for NASDAQ DataLink! Before this project, all of the one line summaries for the datasets were done manually. These summaries were then fed into the search algorithm, so they were resulting in poor search results. We started completely from scratch, designed our own architecture, tested a bunch of different text-summarization LLMs (ChatGPT, Falcon40B, and T5, to name a few), and then spent the last couple weeks of the internship getting our application deployed. The main challenge we faced was that we were given almost no direction with this project, so right from the gate we had to pull together whatever combined knowledge the three of us had from classes and past experience to make something that none of us had ever done before. We also had to figure out how to delegate amongst the three of us, and play into our individual skills to get the project done in the 10 weeks we had. We started the application having only one service (just a Flask app), but then realized about halfway in that, because of the way we had delegated the project, we could have three distinct services and turn the project into a microservice application. One of the services communicated with Databricks, which hosted the model. The second one (the one I worked on) communicated with Gitlab API, which would use a Gitlab Webhook to trigger our summarization every time an update was made to the dataset repo. The third acted as a "master" service, and also facilitated the front end of the application. We actually ended up finishing ahead of schedule, and decided to take on the challenge of trying to deploy our service. Only one of us had ever worked with Docker before, and none of us had done anything with Kubernetes before, so this involved a lot research and asking for help. Besides this, we were also able to add some bonus features (such as keyword generation) to also feed into the search algorithm and further improve results.	github.com/gdozortsev	5	12	11	9	4	1	6	10	2	3	7	8	Criminal Justice and Law 