	she/her	2025	Yes	Throughout my experience as a CS major, I have worked on many large projects, spent tireless hours in the JCC, and gained a lot of valuable experience coding in languages like C, C++, HTML/CSS, and more. However, all of these projects and assignments simply allowed me to code for a grade. Up until now, I have never had a chance to use what I have learned in a real-world environment that would allow me to work with and help out local non-profit organizations. I have had plenty of opportunities to program assignments that have been completed again and again by thousands of Tufts CS students, but through JumboCode, I finally have the chance to apply my skills to real-world projects that benefit local non-profits, making a tangible impact with my work.	When the pandemic began, I started working as a math tutor, helping kids who were struggling with their coursework due to online learning and other challenges. I have continued to work with the same three kids since then (over four years) and have been able to watch their math skills grow immensely. One of the kids I work with initially had difficulty focusing on lessons for more than 15 minutes at a time, and I was recently told by her mom that she ended up placing into advanced math this year. When I started tutoring those kids, I never really thought about the impact much, but seeing their progress over the years has shown me how meaningful and rewarding it is to help others succeed.\n\nI have also been a member of Tufts Public Harmony since the beginning of my sophomore year. Public Harmony is a service club that aims to provide joy and comfort to our community through music. We have weekly service concerts where we go and perform at local nursing homes, pediatric cancer facilities, and other places where music can bring comfort and healing. These performances have allowed me to connect with others in a meaningful way, using music to brighten someone’s day and make a positive impact on the community.\n	Yes	Yes	Mondays, Wednesdays, Thursdays 6:30-9pm\nFridays: anytime before 4:30 and anytime after 7	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 105: Programming Languages	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, SQL, Node.js/Express	CS11!	Over the summer, I was a fellow in the Make-A-Play Program, which is designed to help under-represented athletes gain professional experience. For the second half of the fellowship, the fellows were divided into six groups for a case competition, where we were given a business problem that we needed to solve and present on. I was selected as the “scrum master” of my group, and was in charge of planning our meetings, delegating tasks, and essentially ensuring everything came together at the end. I was completely brand new to this kind of work, as it was essentially a consulting project (we had to come up with a budget, a KPI framework, a risk assessment, etc), and there were a lot of moving parts. We all agreed on the phases we were responsible for, and we scheduled weekly meetings to go over our progress. One issue we ran into was that a lot of the content we were responsible for was unclear, and when we sent out messages to the company, they did not respond. So, it was up to us to investigate and complete additional research to ensure we were going in the right direction. One big challenge I faced was when one of our group members stopped responding one week before the presentation. I worked with another group member to complete his part and we ended up getting everything done in time. \n\nThis project was the first time I was the leader of a real-life business project, and it taught me a lot about leadership, teamwork, and problem-solving under pressure. I learned how to manage unexpected challenges, communicate effectively with a team, and take initiative when things didn’t go as planned. Despite the obstacles, I was proud of how we pulled together and delivered a successful presentation. This experience gave me confidence in my ability to lead and adapt in a professional setting. After our presentation, all of my group members texted in the group chat about how proud they were and how they appreciated me “keeping everything together.” Of course I was incredibly proud of the outcome, but hearing that my group members enjoyed being a part of our team and felt that I had done a good job leading was the most rewarding part of the experience. It reassured me that not only had we delivered a solid project, but I had also created a positive and supportive team environment.\n	\N	2	4	6	12	9	10	3	1	8	7	5	11	For my first choice, I chose LCS Tutoring. As I mentioned previously, I have worked as a math tutor for over four years, and I also briefly volunteered as a tutor during the early stages of the pandemic for a five-year old kid who really just needed some companionship and someone to talk and color with. Being a tutor has been one of the best experiences and it has been so rewarding to watch my kids grow stronger and more confident in their abilities. Being able to work on a project that would aid in giving other tutors the same amazing experience and give kids the support they need would be incredibly fulfilling. I know firsthand how impactful tutoring can be, not just academically, but emotionally as well, and working on this project would help me expand that impact.\n\nFor my second choice, I selected the West Medford Community Center. I chose this because anyone who knows me knows that one of my biggest values is communication. I am always saying that good communication is the basis for successful projects, relationships, and community engagement. The West Medford Community Center plays a vital role in connecting diverse individuals through various activities, and effective communication is key to that mission. By streamlining their newsletter creation process, I believe I can help ensure that vital information reaches the community more efficiently. I'm excited about the opportunity to contribute to a project that enhances communication and strengthens community ties.	Nope!	Not yet!	Alatar	
