	she/her	2025	Yes	I would like to join JumboCode for several reasons! I would love to apply my technical skills to create meaningful, lasting change in the community by working on projects for local non-profits. JumboCode would also give me the opportunity to grow my abilities in software development through hands-on work on real-world projects. I also love that in JumboCode, I would get to work closely on a diverse team of project managers, designers and developers. This collaborative experience would allow me to simulate a professional software development environment. Overall, being part of Jumbo would also give me the opportunity to engage with and give back to the local community while improving my own skills!	I have been a TA for CS11 at Tufts for the past 4 semesters. As a TA, I grade homeworks, and run Office Hours and Labs (my favorite part of a being a TA)!\n\nThis summer, I worked part time as a mentor for the ACE++ (Access to Computing Equity) Program at Tufts. As part of this summer program/fellowship, undergraduates from underrepresented communities in Tech are given guidance on how to begin their career in Tech (through workshops on technical and behavioral topics that are not taught in a classroom), all while taking 2 courses: CS11 / CS15 and Discrete Math.\nI participated in this program 3 summers ago, and decided to come back as peer mentor this summer. As a mentor, I ran Office Hours for the courses that this summer's cohort was taking. I also assisted in running weekly sessions called "CS Enrichment", where the students were taught the basics of data science (with each student getting to implement and present a data science project).\nComing back as a mentor for ACE++ was super meaningful to me because I had first-hand experience of what the program was like as a participant, so I was able to apply that knowledge to support this summer's cohort as much as I could!	Yes	Yes	My schedule is pretty flexible and we can definitely talk about it more once I am hopefully accepted. But in general, I am free Mon - Wed after 6pm; Thu after 1:30pm; Fri after 3pm; Sat anytime; Sun before 5pm.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 105: Programming Languages, CS 116: Introduction to Security, CS 160: Algorithms, CS 170: Computation Theory, CS135 (Intro to ML); CS152 Computer Vision; CS111 (Operating Systems) 	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	My first introduction to CS was in middle school when we were taught how to use Scratch (does that count??). My first proper introduction to CS was in high school when I took a CS course in JavaScript.	Last summer, I created a website for Tufts Conference & Event Services (CES) to be able to easily view and loan furniture on campus for various events they organize. This project was super interesting and challenging to me because before that, my only experience with web programming was taking CS20 (Intro to Web Dev). I got to learn and use technology I had never used before: React.js for the frontend, Node.js & Express.js for the backend, and MySQL for database. My biggest challenge by far was piecing together all these different technologies to connect the frontend to the backend, and the backend to the database; I learned from watching many Youtube videos of people doing full-stack web programming. I implemented features allowing users of the site to view, create, and update the furniture inventory, work orders, and CES event details. To connect the backend to the database, I ended up using an ORM called Sequelize to convert the tables from mySQL database into objects that could be more easily manipulated using JavaScript. This was a super cool aspect of the project to me because I did not even know that it was possible to do that. To connect the frontend and backend, I learned to make API calls.	https://github.com/kelly-tsidji	12	8	9	5	2	1	4