“has reached out abt jumbocode interest many many times” -Lillian Tran	He/Him	2026	Yes	I have been wanting to join JumboCode for the last two years for the same reason. I feel like it is the best place to start getting some more hands on work experience and learning to collaborate with a team in order to help minorities or any of the multiple good causes that all 12 projects contribute to. Today I attended the GIM and not only I was incredibly interested in joining some people's project due to the fact that the knowledge they can help me gather is amazing, but I also really liked the atmosphere, and how all of the different leaders in each team were willing to help, teach, guide and all while keeping a very friendly environment. By joining the the team I hope not only to improve my technical skills and overall knowledge of the field (which I really want to), but I also get to make new friends and learn to contribute with one another, which is something that both personally and professionally are of vital importance to me.	- Back in Valencia (Spain) I volunteered to work a weekend in a social dining hall, where we fed lunch to homeless people and other people in need\n- As part of the tennis team here at tufts, one weekend we also organized a clinic to teach kids around the area how to play the sport and that ended up being very fun.\n- Also as part of the team, we are working with TeamImpact, which is an organization that pairs a child with some disability with a sports team and we consider Nolan as one of our players now, we even had a signing ceremony which was hilarious.\n- Finally, I help out my underclassmen with Cs as they happen to struggle with MetroSim and FilesOfPix. I know the latter probably doesn't count but it does to me...	Yes	Yes	Should be fine, just will have to rearrange a couple of things but will do	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Git/GitHub, SQL, Python	It was indeed Cs11	Has to be filesOfPix. That thing took me roughly 70 hours and half of my mental sanity. Im sure you are familiar with it but this project was the first class-project that had to be very carefully planned ahead, with a detailed design, architecture and testing method before getting too much in the coding and having to change later. This forced me and my partner to start thinking about more carefully about the decisions we were taking, especially as performance in this class was very importance (something I found challenging yet rewarding). I wont even mention debugging this piece of art cause I think it took us 6 hours to debug an incorrect casting of a closure struct, almost cried when we found it (shout out to the TAs)	work on progress for the blog-app and the portfolio but: https://github.com/javigon1	2	1	11	12	9	4	7	5	6	3	10	8	First of all, the reason why the village food hub is the first one is that I love food. Not only I love to eat healthy but I also feel like the price of food if you wanna have a good diet are unpayable. For this reason I loved when they mentioned that in the GIM. I also like the idea of reducing food waste, cause in my house that didn't happen but then you come to Dewick and you see plates and plates of food being thrown away every meal, it starts to get concerning. Also I have to figure out how did Jiyoon get to go to more than 30 concerts. The cherry on top is that all of the technologies that they are using I have been wanting to learn for months now, but I was lacking guidance. Hopefully, not anymore. Other projects I have loved have been the WestMedford Community center (I've never gone rock climbing so hopefully Winston keeps his word and takes us), the Bread and Roses and the New England Innocence Project. I was personally trying to develop a blog app (just small details left) so I love the idea of finally being able to finish a web-app that works as a newsletter. Also I am pretty intrigued on what the AWS does. For the Bread and Roses first of all I found the data they showed impressive. Linking back to the food industry, I like their idea of voluntarily providing hygienic meals 3 times a day, so if I can try and help make life easier to people who are helping to feed people in need, I'll gladly will. Finally, I found this one the most interesting of all in terms of how much the focus shifted to almost law and to prevent wrongful convictions. I find this situation extremely important as back when I was younger I used to think on how is it possible that some people were in jail for like 20 years and then released cause they were found innocent... I feel like those situations are probably one of the worst things that can possibly happen, so we have to hand in the information easily to NEIP employees. I would love to work on any projects but I couldn't and wouldn't write that much so I stuck with my favourites.	No	No	Firefoot	
	he/him	2027	No	I want to join JumboCode because I believe that community service is an important duty that I must fulfil, especially as a guest in a foreign country. \n\nListening to the projects that JumboCode has planned this year, I believe that they will all help alleviate a serious problem holding back every one of the 12 client organizations from operating at their full potential. Given my strong track record of programming, teamwork and problem-solving, I believe I could help any project become a reality. \n\nI want to make a difference with other like-minded people, and hopefully have some fun along the way.	My faith has taught me the importance of helping others from a young age, something I have not forgotten. During all four years of high school in Sevenoaks, England, I spent every Thursday afternoon volunteering. I spent three of those years teaching English to Syrian and Afghan refugees to help them assimilate into British society. Before that, I spent a year teaching local primary school children how to play Gamelan, and Indonesian style of percussion music.\n\nAfter leaving high school, I lived on a boat for three months teaching children aged 11-18 the basics of seamanship and sailing with the Sea Cadets offshore division, helping prepare them for careers at sea.\n\nAt Tufts, I have become closely engaged in the student community. I co-founded and launched TEMPEST – an open electronic music society which aims to showcase underrepresented student talent. I also serve as the Outreach Officer for the Tufts Economics Society, helping connect students with faculty and research/internship opportunities.	Yes	Yes	I am free from 3-4:30 pm and from 8:30 pm on Mondays and Wednesdays (although I can be somewhat flexible). I am free from 4:15 pm on Tuesdays, midday on Thursdays, and 3:30 pm on Fridays. I usually stay on campus during weekends.	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science	C/C++	CS11	One project that I am proud of is Advising Period, a CS11 homework where I implemented a binary search tree data structure which describes the connection between academic advisors and their advisees. \n\nI am proud of how I used my programming and problem-solving skills to surmount the conceptual challenge of how to develop a recursive function, all while dealing with the complicated syntax and pointers that come with the struct that I used to describe each node in the tree. I loved how elegant the recursive solutions felt in comparison to just using a for loop to search for nodes in the tree.\n\nHaving mainly used arrays and strings thus far in CS11, Advising Period taught me power of being able to choose different customizable structs/classes and abstract data types to solve problems in the real world, where no two projects are the same.	\N	1	11	6	2	4	7	5	9	3	10	8	12	To be clear, there is no project on this list which I would not be proud to be a part of because all of them seek to help a cause that I care about deeply. Just because I rank a project 12th, that does not mean that I would not love to get involved.\n\nMy first pick, the West Medford Community Center newsletter project, speaks to me the most because I want to give back to the local community with which I share our streets, our parks, and our local businesses. The WMCC’s main advantage is that it is a centralized, grassroots organization with strong links with Tufts. In my experience, grassroots organizations like Sevenoaks Welcomes refugees in Sevenoaks, UK, are the most effective at helping vulnerable members of the community because the volunteers regularly meet the people they help because they live in the same area. Additionally, my experience with local grassroots organizations has taught me how they operate. That’s why I feel the strongest duty to help the WMCC.\n\n\nThe Somerville Museum’s work is very important to me because of its efforts to preserve local history and culture as well as collaborate with local public schools. The museum’s specialization in the visual arts is also crucial to support local artists. I also have some knowledge of accounting, which could help the team structure merchandise sales software. Giving back to the Somerville Museum would mean giving back to my local community, which is a win for me.\n\n\nHaving witnessed the difficulties of learning a foreign language in a foreign country as an adult with Sevenoaks Welcomes Refugees, I know that non-native English-speaking people need all the help they can get to integrate into society. That’s why I would be honoured to help build a digital library with English at Large. I also have some experience using pdf and epub files and software which I would be excited to put to good use.\n\n\nAlthough I want to focus on giving back to my local community as that is what is most important to me right now, as someone with Turkish heritage, I feel a duty to uplift Turkic people all over the globe, including Uyghur people. Aside from my passion for and experience with English as a Foreign Language, I believe that my conversational knowledge of Turkish, and beginner knowledge of Russian could be of invaluable use to the team. I would be honoured to work with Dillar Academy.\n\n\nAs some of the most marginalized people in society, LGBTQ seniors deserve affordable, LGBTQ-friendly housing in a high-rent city like Boston. Given LGBTQ Senior Housing’s successes with The Pryde project, I would be excited to work on a project that future residents would use as soon as next year and make a difference in their lives.\n\n\nI know people who have gone through college without adequate emotional and financial support, and I know how tough it can be, especially for people with no alternative. I respect social workers that work to help alleviate this issue and I would love to help the Wily Network in their mission.\n\n\nWrongful criminal convictions are unfortunately still common in America and are exacerbated by racial profiling and poor police discipline. These mistakes can ruin lives, so wrongfully convicted folks ought to be exonerated at all costs. Anything I can do to help prevent that is time well spent. \n\n\nIn 2011, only 47.7% of girls attended primary school in Cameroon. This needs to change not only to bridge the gender education gap, but also to help improve the standing of women in Cameroonian society. I would be delighted to put my problem-solving and programming skills to good use with this project.\n\n\nGiven the lack of funding to local public schools, teachers are put under undue pressure to teach students huge amounts of content. That’s why the LCS’ work to relieve the burden on teachers, and their students, is so important. This is especially true for students who do not have the means to access private tutoring, or those who may have been deterred or disillusioned from essential subjects like mathematics, English and science. Working to help LCS Tutoring would be another great way for me to give back locally.\n\n\nLike the WMCC, I believe that community-based, centralized organizations are worth supporting because of the warmth that a friendly face can bring, especially for those with poor food security. I love cooking food and sharing the love with others, I would like to help volunteers at Bread and Roses with their mission. If Bread and Roses were more local to Tufts, I would have ranked it higher.\n\n\nI am essentially indifferent between the Village Food Hub and Bread & Roses projects since they are so similar.\n\n\nI would love to be involved in helping the Office of University Counsel as I admire their efforts to endorse doctors and professors to pharmaceutical companies. I would love to get involved in helping them achieve their goals using software solutions.\n	\N	No	Bill the Pony	
	He/Him/His	2027	Yes	I want to join JumboCode because of the impact it has on uplifting voices and organizations in the local community. I have always wanted to combine my interests in using technology and volunteer work to help others and organizations in the communities I am involved in.\nI hope to improve my web development skills as an engineer, whether it be front-end or back-end technologies. I also hope to join a welcoming and growing community of technology enthusiasts who love to give back and develop their knowledge in all things tech.	I volunteered and worked with non-profits all throughout my high school career. I tutored for a non-profit math company called YMath, tutoring elementary, middle, and high school kids in math topics ranging from pre-algebra to calculus. I also created my own organization in high school with a friend called "DEAS Academy" to create resources for students who come from underrepresented communities. This allowed them to have new study materials and another way for students to learn content.	Yes	Yes	I would be available every evening each week, except for Tuesdays and Thursdays from 8 PM to 10 PM.	CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	My first introduction to computer science was in 9th grade for my "Introduction to Computer Science" in Python and Java	The project I am most proud of is the BCA Research Hub that I made with two of my classmates for my CS Capstone in high school. This was similar to a learning management system where students and teachers could collaborate and communicate together on the progress of a research project that is currently being done. There was also a page for file uploads so students could upload research competition PDFs and other documents for their projects.\nThe tech stack was: HTML/CSS/JS, node.js + Express, Bootstrap, EJS, SQL, Auth0, Google Drive API.\nThe main challenges my group faced was communication and we realized that having a centralized place to communicate with each other from the very beginning would've been better for efficiency. We also sometimes faced "dead ends" where we spent a lot of time trying to solve one small problem on the way, before implementing other major features. This cost us some time and we realized that finishing the "meat" of the project first, and focusing on the details later, is a better way to approach projects like these.	https://emiliolaleman.com/	3	8	2	7	4	11	12	5	6	1	9	10	I would like to join Bread & Roses because of the solution to the current problem: a scheduling web-application. I love anything and everything that has to do with organization and scheduling, and the fact that this application helps those with food insecurities only makes the project even more impactful. Thus, it would be an honor to join this team.\nI would also like to join The Wily Network because of its support to fellow college students. By helping others around my age with emotional and financial support, they would be able to navigate college a bit easier with less troubles and worries. The fact that this web-application is also somewhat organizational also drew me towards it.\nLastly, I would like to join the West Medford Community Center group because of its solution to a common problem for newsletter and publication organizations. By creating a web-app that facilitates templating and histories of past publications, this would take the burden off of the Executive Director and make the Community Center more efficient.	I would truly love to join the JumboCode community and heavily contribute to the project I would be involved in!	No	Snowmane	
	she/her	2027	Yes	I want to join JumboCode because I view it as an excellent opportunity to both connect with the computer science community at Tufts and enhance my own coding skills in a way that gives me real world experience. Additionally, I would love to contribute to the local community by providing assistance. It would be nice to see all the good that coding and CS can do for real people in the area, because I feel like so much of what we do or what we study for in CS classes is not catered towards the benefit of everyday people or nonprofit causes. By joining the club, I would like to gain a connection to the CS community at Tufts, a connection with the community of the area, and experience in software engineering. I think that working on a team with other and having bonding events, meetings, etc, would mean a lot to me, because I haven't really found community within the major at Tufts. 	In the past, I've volunteered by tutoring high school students online for the SAT, which provided a lot of insight into the type of academic support students need when preparing for college applications. I've also volunteered at the soup kitchen in my hometown, helping take stock of inventory as well as serving food. 	Yes	Yes	I am available after 7:30 pm on Sundays, Mondays and Wednesdays, and I am free before 12 every day of the week. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures	C/C++, Python	My first introduction to computer science was in CS11 last year. 	A project I'm proud of is the project I just completed for CS40, Restoration.  The project involved decoding corrupted pgm files and storing the restored files in a new type of file. Me and my partner built it by sorting through each line in the file, creating a function that identified the correct lines, and then storing them in a data structure using the Hanson structures. One challenge I faced was getting used to C with prior experience only in C++, which gave me insight into how one may get accostumed to new programming languages. Additionally, the problems we faced altering memory made me appreicate the value of testing early and often, as well as the difficulties of debugging programs with multiple parts. My partner and I had schedules that were very difficult to line up, and working with her also made me understand the extra effort that goes into working with another person. 	\N	7	3	5	11	6	2	4	8	12	9	1	10	I put A2Empowerment as my top preference because I think the work they do is really meaningful, and I view access to education as one of the most empowering things that young women can receive. Furthermore, I have met the PM for that project through another club, and I would love to work with her. I put the Innocence Project second because I had prior knowledge of the Innocence Project before the GIM, and it has always stuck out to me as a project that is making a notable contribution to social good, as well as fighting against justice system inequality. I would be so excited to work on something that could give people their lives and their freedom back after being unjustly imprisoned. The Village Food Hub is another cause that is pretty inspriational, and I find it especially useful after working at a soup kitchen at home and facing a decrepit inventory spreadsheet. Overall though, I find the these projects to be really inspirational, and I would be excited at the prospect of doing social good no matter the situation. 	\N	No, I have not been a part of JUmboCode before. 	Shadowfax	
	He/Him/His	2025	No	I’ve always enjoyed participating in community events or fundraisers, and it feels good to give back to my hometown, but my interest in CS never really aligned with any volunteer opportunities I found. I haven’t seen an opportunity to use my Python skills or my familiarity with HTML to do social work before JumboCode, so I’m really interested in getting on board with it. It seems like a good opportunity to better connect with like minded people in the Tufts community, give back to the town that's been hosting my college education, and practice my CS skills. It would also be good for my resume lol.	- Volunteered as part of a micro finance NGO in Senegal, working to help local business owners (farmers, restaurants, women-owned shops, etc.) gain access to loans and better credit systems\n- Worked as a math tutor for elementary and middle school students, focusing on algebra and triganometry\n- Worked as part of a cleanup group picking up and disposing of waste in public areas around the Brookline area (parks, beaches, reservations, lakes)\n- Sang in a choir that frequently performed for religious events or other happenings at local churches	Yes	Yes	Monday: Before 2pm\nTuesday: Before 11:30am & After 2pm\nWednesday: Before 2pm\nThursday Before 11:30am & After 4:30pm\nFriday: All day\nSaturday: All day\nSunday: All day	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 121: Software Engineering, CS 160: Algorithms, CS 23: Game Design, CS 61: Discrete Math	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Git/GitHub, Python, Java, C#, Unity	I took a few game design classes in elementary school	At a class here at Tufts, I was tasked with making a full game to release online for anyone to play. We were given instructions on how to use basic Unity features and tutorials on C# scripting, but outside of that and office hours we were mostly left to our own devices. I basically had to juggle coming up with the concept for a new game, learning how to use a new game editor, learning a new programming language, being the project manager, and staying on top of the rest of my school work as well. Frankly, I’m not sure how I managed to pull it off, but the game was fully released with a trailer on YouTube a few months ago. My team definitely came in clutch for me too; they helped handle most of the art assets and picked up lots of slack in the coding department. Making and playing games is something I really love, and I’m really happy I got the opportunity to do so at Tufts. I’d love to work on something like that again.	https://onelines.itch.io/oneline	11	9	5	3	2	6	1	4	7	10	8	12	Ever since I came out as bisexual and started gathering the courage to embrace my sexuality, I’ve always been really interested in supporting LGBTQ+ initiatives. Even just being LGBTQ+ and understanding that part of yourself can be really difficult, and I’m more than eager to help lighten the load however I can. My parents also both were forced to leave China at one point for certain unfortunate reasons, so I feel sort of connected to other groups in China that were/are being exiled or persecuted, like Uyghurs. I feel like both of these initiatives hit close to home for me, so I’d really love the opportunity to help here.	\N	No	Yavanna Kementári	
	He/him/his	Masters Student (non-Tufts undergrad)	No	I want to join JumboCode because it offers a unique opportunity to use my technical skills to make a real impact on the local community. The chance to collaborate with non-profits on meaningful projects aligns with my passion for social good, and I’m excited about working on software that creates long-term structural change. JumboCode’s focus on centering community needs and fostering authentic relationships really resonates with me.\n\nI hope to gain hands-on experience in modern web development and client collaboration, learning from PMs, Tech Leads, and designers in a practical, supportive environment. The year-long project cycle and the opportunity to grow my skills through teamwork make JumboCode the perfect place for me to develop both technically and personally while contributing to causes that matter.	This past summer, I worked with the City of Boston on a project to improve the categorization and routing of 311 service requests. I trained on-device image classification and object detection models to help the city respond more efficiently to community issues like potholes and trash storage.\n\nDuring my undergraduate years, I served as a project lead in the Engineers Without Borders UCL Chapter, where I helped organize and deliver engineering workshops to k-12 students in London. I also volunteered as an A-Level Math tutor with CoachBright.org and provided mentorship for college preparation through Brightside.org.uk for over a year.	Yes	Yes	Monday: 12:00 PM - 4:15 PM, after 6:00 PM\nTuesday: Before 12:00 PM, 2:45 PM - 5:45 PM, after 7:30 PM\nWednesday: 12:00 PM - 4:15 PM, after 6:00 PM\nThursday: Before 5:45 PM\n\nI am also open to discussing other time slots if needed.	CS 15: Data Structures, CS 121: Software Engineering, CS 160: Algorithms	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, React, Git/GitHub, SQL, Node.js/Express, Python	My first introduction to programming was a C course in my first year of undergrad, but I consider my true introduction to computer science to be a second-year course in computer networking. My official CS introduction was through CS 15 at Tufts.	I’m particularly proud of the first web app I ever built and shipped, especially since my background during most of undergrad was focused on AI systems and pipelines, not web development. Although I had some experience building interfaces with Django and PyQt5, this was my first true web app—a simple Gradio interface integrating a Stable Diffusion model.\n\nWhat made it special was the immediate reception. I shared it with my friends, and it quickly gained popularity within my circle. Seeing people actively using it and discussing the images it generated brought me a lot of joy. It was a great feeling to know something I built was bringing value to others. That experience solidified my decision to dive deeper into web development, though I had a brief moment of regret a month later when I realized I hadn’t shut down the AWS instance for the app.	https://github.com/williamli-15	10	3	7	5	11	12	4	9	8	2	6	1	While I have experience with both Next.js and FastAPI, I prefer Python development, which makes the Tufts General Counsel project my first choice. I am also drawn to projects involving scheduling, inventory management, and communication, which is why Bread & Roses, Village Hub Food, and LGBTQ Senior Housing are projects where I believe I can contribute meaningfully given my background. Additionally, I am equally interested in working with NoSQL databases like MongoDB, as well as relational databases using ORM. 	\N	No, I have not been a part of JumboCode before.	Tulkas	
	He/Him/His	2025	No	I have been wanting to gain more real-world coding experience for a long time, and JumboCode is a great way to do that while also helping the local community. I want to use my comp sci powers for good instead of evil :). I hope to improve my skills as a programmer, especially in a real-world setting. I also want more practice working with a coding team, since most of my projects have been solo or with one partner. Lastly, I hope that I can gain more of a connection with the local community and give back!	In high school I volunteered at a soup kitchen and at two different food pantries. I was also a classroom assistant at a Montessori school for pre-k and k students (assisting the same teacher I had when I was in pre-k!!!). 	Yes	Yes	Fridays before 7, anytime Saturday except 1-3, anytime on Sunday, Mondays after 3, and maybe Thursdays after 4	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 20: Web Programming, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 116: Introduction to Security, CS 160: Algorithms, CS 23: Game Design, (also currently taking Intro to App Development for what it's worth)	C/C++, HTML/CSS, JavaScript/TypeScript, Git/GitHub, SQL	I started out making scratch projects in middle school (I actually recently rediscovered my old account), and I made a version of Pong for iPads in 8th grade.	I'm really proud of my final project for cs 20: web design. I worked with a team and it was a good challenge to split up tasks and coordinate with everyone. I liked that we could all play to our strengths when contributing to the project. We built it using HTML, CSS, javascript, PHP, and an SQL database through SiteGround. I think that working with databases and figuring out how to store, pull, and then display data from our database was one of the biggest challenges we faced. We split up the task so that people could work to their strengths and we were able to work much faster. I am particularly proud of designing our "products" and "ordering" pages. We were making a website for a fictional pokemon rental service, and I made the pages that handled orders match with trading interfaces from the games and made the page that displayed the Pokemon resemble the trading cards. In the end I think it turned out really great!	https://colink.sgedu.site/ has links to my most polished projects, including my cs20 final. Pardon the appearance please...	5	11	7	2	9	10	3	4	8	1	6	12	I've heard of Bread & Roses and they seem like a really great organization to work with. I like that this project would have an immediate and direct positive impact on the local community, and I think this project has a lot of potential! I'm also interested in game nights haha	\N	No!	Mairon	
	He/Him	Masters Student (non-Tufts undergrad)	No	The main reason I really want to join JumboCode is due to the current phase of my career I am in. Since my undergraduate is in Biology/Statistics I have struggled to find a place where I can grow as a student and learn as I become an asset to any team and project. I want to build projects and be apart of an organization that will allow me to learn and grow as a student while I refine my skills to become a better programmer. JumboCode since it is student run allows me to learn and grow as a student to be a better programmer and be ready for a full time job. This is an area I can learn from other programmers who are students and learning as well. Since I am struggling to land internships due to my lack of experience this would be the perfect environment for me to grow as a student. 	Social activism: Coordinated and planned Women, life, freedom rallies at my university for the women in my country who lack basic human rights within Iran. Planned school protests and joined larger public protests as this cause is important. \n\nVolunteering: Volunteered at homeless shelters within the San Jose, CA, area where we helped prep meals for dinner for those who are disadvantaged within my community. 	Studying remotely	Studying remotely	Available any time on the weekend. 	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 115: Database Systems, CS 116: Introduction to Security, CS 160: Algorithms, CS 131	C/C++, HTML/CSS, SQL, Python	My first introduction to computer science was a programming course at my Highschool which funny enough at the time I did not enjoy at all 	The project I really enjoyed was a neural network project I designed to predict mice cognitive behavior based on the specific activations of the mice's brains. This was a model that would learn based of prediction and improve the guessing each time. 	https://github.com/sina328/Capstone-project-Predicting-cognitive-behaviors.git	2	5	9	7	12	1	4	8	6	11	3	10	Not too picky I am open to any project in reality as they all do seem very interesting in their own respective ways. The legal service project by Rebecca and Sachin is rather very interesting in my opinion and that one did peak my interest the most. 	None, Im eager and hope I do get selected for this role!	No	Ossë	
	he/him	2025	Prefer not to answer	Participating in JumboCode would mark my first long-term software engineering project. I’ve found that the best way to learn something is to experience an intense — but safe — microcosm of it. The project formats are exactly that. Literally couldn’t be a better fit for the experience I’m looking for.\n\nTeambuilding is also something I value deeply. Over many years of working on theater and film sets I’ve experienced miraculous bouts of creative synergy, rivaled only by horrifying disasters that somehow, even more miraculously, were salvaged in the end. No matter which way it goes, there’s a certain magic\n\nAlso the club culture looks really chill overall which is a huge plus.	In high school, I was part of a mother-son volunteer organization called Service Corps. My mom and I volunteered throughout San Francisco, spending time at food banks, animal shelters, tutoring centers, and doing field work. This experience brought us closer, which I'm really grateful for.\n\nI also volunteered at a retirement home where I ran a tech support booth for seniors. This experience opened my eyes to how delicate usability can be. A single weirdly-named button or badly designed sync system (I’m looking at you, iCloud) can cause a world of pain for someone whose life is already hard enough. My ultimate goal was to empower the residents to troubleshoot on their own, making my role eventually obsolete. It was a huge challenge, as I had to synthesize many disparate CS concepts and terrible UX decisions into simple, usable metaphors. This experience made me an immensely better communicator.\n\nWhile studying abroad in Madrid, I worked on a short documentary focused on interviewing subway musicians. This project was particularly enjoyable because it introduced me to a hidden world of underground (pun intended) performers. Many of the musicians were immigrants with incredible stories, and I felt honored to document their words and songs.\n\nMost recently, I helped finish a promotional video for Eleanor Lives, an organization dedicated to incorporating Eleanor Roosevelt’s Universal Declaration of Human Rights into the UN Charter. The project had a tight deadline and involved an active line of communication with multiple parties. It came together beautifully and I’m so excited for it to be shown at the NY Summit of the Future.\n\nWhile not technically a volunteer role, my favorite job I’ve ever worked was as a receptionist at a hostel in San Francisco. Beyond being efficient and solving logistical problems, a large part of the job was creating a welcoming atmosphere and fostering a sense of community among travelers. Some of the guests I met have since become lifelong friends, whom I even visited while studying abroad.	Yes	Yes	Monday 3pm till end of day\nTuesday 3-6pm\nWednesday 3pm till end of day\nThursday 6pm till end of day\nFriday, Saturday, and Sunday I usually get work done so I'm very flexible to schedule around meetings	CS 11: Intro to Computer Science, CS 15: Data Structures, CS 40: Machine Structures and Programming, CS 121: Software Engineering, CS 23: Game Design	C/C++, HTML/CSS, Git/GitHub, Python	When I was 10 I got really obsessed with Minecraft command blocks. This kickstarted my map-making career, where I produced bangers like Alien Apocalypse and Explosive Diamond PVP. My biggest project, Herobrine’s Threat, received an insanely impressive 3 downloads.	Probably my coolest flex is that when I was 14, I did a commission project for a big Minecraft animation channel. I worked on it in Blender for about 2.5 months. With 17 different scenes — each one with a different environment, lighting setup, characters, rendering — it was by far the biggest self-directed project I had done in my life. While I was mainly self-taught at that point, this project required me to seriously brush up my workflow by taking a formal animation class. In the end it was a huge labor of love and probably the only project I can look back on without finding something I wish I’d done differently.	https://www.linkedin.com/in/peter-wolfe-ab8625213/   The legendary Minecraft animation: https://youtu.be/wU4p2LvSfRw	1	6	9	12	5	4	2	8	11	7	10	3	I feel the urge to work on an ambitious project, especially one where usability is a challenge. (I think this will be my biggest strength.)\n\nI also picked based on which project leaders I thought I’d vibe well with.	As a senior majoring in CS, my relative lack of experience is due to my year abroad, where unfortunately, very few CS classes were offered to me. I’m making up for lost time by taking CS 170, 105, and Web Design this semester. I expect my tech stack familiarity to grow immensely this year.\n\nAlso, I wrote this application wearing Lightning McQueen crocs.	\N	Landroval	
